window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["terraform"]=[{"id":"terraform-f0001","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"What is Terraform, and how does it differ from Ansible or CloudFormation?","a":"<p>Terraform is a declarative IaC tool that provisions infrastructure across clouds. Ansible is procedural and excels at configuration management. CloudFormation is AWS-only and uses JSON/YAML, which is less readable than Terraform's HCL.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0002","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"What is Infrastructure as Code (IaC), and why is it important?","a":"<p>IaC is managing and provisioning infrastructure through machine-readable definition files instead of manual processes. It gives consistency, scalability, automation and version control. Terraform is declarative, uses HCL, supports many cloud providers and keeps a state file so operations are idempotent. Ansible is procedural, uses YAML playbooks, is agentless (SSH) and excels at configuration management and application deployment. Terraform focuses on provisioning, Ansible on configuration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0003","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"What are the key components of Terraform?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Providers</strong>: bridges to cloud APIs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resources</strong>: the infrastructure pieces.</li>\n  <li><strong>Modules</strong>: reusable configs.</li>\n  <li><strong>State</strong>: tracks reality.</li>\n  <li><strong>Variables</strong>: inputs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Outputs</strong>: results.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0004","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"Why is \"declarative\" an advantage of Terraform over writing a shell script that calls cloud CLIs?","a":"<p>In a declarative tool you describe the end state and Terraform works out the steps by comparing the configuration with the state. Running it twice changes nothing the second time. A script describes steps, so you must handle \"already exists\", ordering and cleanup yourself, and re-running it can create duplicates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0005","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"What problems does Infrastructure as Code solve compared with creating resources by hand in the cloud console?","a":"<p>Manual work is slow, hard to repeat and hard to audit. IaC makes environments reproducible (same code, same result), reviewable (pull requests), versioned (Git history and rollback by reverting) and automatable (CI/CD). It also reduces configuration drift between dev, staging and prod.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0006","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between a provider and a resource?","a":"<p>A provider is a plugin that talks to one platform's API (AWS, Azure, Kubernetes, GitHub). A resource is one object that provider can manage, such as <code>aws_instance</code>. Without the provider, Terraform has no resource types for that platform.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0007","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"When would you pick Terraform over CloudFormation, and when would CloudFormation still be reasonable?","a":"<p>Pick Terraform for multi-cloud or multi-service setups (cloud, DNS, monitoring, SaaS) with one workflow and language. CloudFormation is reasonable for AWS-only teams that want AWS-managed state, native stack rollback and first-day support for new AWS features, with no state backend to run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0008","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"Terraform and Ansible are often used together. How do you split the work between them?","a":"<p>Terraform provisions the infrastructure (networks, instances, databases) and keeps it in state. Ansible then configures what runs on it (packages, files, services). Terraform outputs such as IP addresses feed an Ansible inventory. The split follows each tool's strength: immutable-style provisioning versus in-machine configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0009","card":"q-terraform-001-terraform-and-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"terraform-s002","q":"What does \"cloud-agnostic\" mean for Terraform, and what does it not mean?","a":"<p>The same workflow and language (HCL) work across many providers. It does not mean the code is portable: an <code>aws_instance</code> and an <code>azurerm_linux_virtual_machine</code> are different resources with different arguments, so moving clouds still means rewriting resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0010","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"What is the purpose of <code>terraform init</code>?","a":"<p>It sets up the project: downloads providers, configures the backend and fetches modules.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform init</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0011","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"What does <code>terraform plan</code> do, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It shows what will change by comparing desired and current state. It catches errors early and lets you review safely.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform plan</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0012","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"How does <code>terraform apply</code> work?","a":"<p>It executes the plan: creates, updates or deletes resources and updates state. It prompts for confirmation unless <code>-auto-approve</code> is used.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform apply</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0013","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"What is the purpose of <code>terraform destroy</code>?","a":"<p>It deletes all managed resources, a cleanup tool.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform destroy</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0014","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"In Terraform, why must <code>terraform init</code> run before <code>plan</code> or <code>apply</code>, and when do you need to run it again?","a":"<p><code>init</code> downloads the provider plugins, fetches child modules and initializes the backend. Without it, Terraform cannot even parse resource types. Run it again after you add or change a provider, a module source or version, or the backend configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0015","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"In Terraform, what folder and file does <code>terraform init</code> create in the working directory, and what should go into Git?","a":"<p>It creates a <code>.terraform</code> directory (downloaded providers and modules) and a <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code> dependency lock file. Commit the lock file so everyone uses the same provider versions. Do not commit <code>.terraform</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0016","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"How can you make sure <code>terraform apply</code> executes exactly the changes you reviewed?","a":"<p>Save the plan and apply that file:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform plan -out=tfplan\nterraform apply tfplan</code></pre></div>\n<p>Applying a saved plan skips re-planning, so changes made to the infrastructure between plan and apply cannot silently alter what is executed. If the state changed in the meantime, Terraform rejects the stale plan. Plan files can contain secrets, so store them carefully.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0017","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"What does the exit code of <code>terraform plan -detailed-exitcode</code> tell a CI job?","a":"<p><code>0</code> means no changes, <code>1</code> means an error, and <code>2</code> means the plan succeeded and there are changes. A pipeline uses it to detect drift or to decide whether an approval step is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0018","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"What is the difference between <code>terraform destroy</code> and removing a resource block and running <code>apply</code>?","a":"<p><code>destroy</code> removes every resource in the state. Deleting one block and applying removes only that resource, because Terraform sees it in state but not in configuration. For a single resource you can also use <code>terraform destroy -target=&lt;address&gt;</code>, but <code>-target</code> is meant for exceptional cases only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0019","card":"q-terraform-002-init-plan-apply-and-destroy","sec":"terraform-s003","q":"Why is <code>terraform destroy</code> in production considered dangerous, and what protects against it?","a":"<p>It deletes everything the state tracks, including databases and storage, with no automatic undo. Protections: <code>lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }</code> on critical resources, deletion protection on the cloud resource itself (for example RDS), separate state per environment, restricted IAM permissions for the pipeline and a required approval before destroy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0020","card":"q-terraform-003-state-file-and-providers","sec":"terraform-s004","q":"What is the Terraform state file, and why is it critical?","a":"<p>It maps configuration to real infrastructure. If it is lost, Terraform is blind and you get duplicates or errors.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0021","card":"q-terraform-003-state-file-and-providers","sec":"terraform-s004","q":"What is a Terraform provider, and how do you configure one?","a":"<p>A provider is the API bridge to clouds and services. Define it like this:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>provider &quot;aws&quot; {\n    region = &quot;us-east-1&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0022","card":"q-terraform-003-state-file-and-providers","sec":"terraform-s004","q":"What information does the Terraform state store, and why does it matter for security?","a":"<p>It stores the mapping of each resource address to its real ID plus all attributes the provider returned, such as IPs, ARNs and often passwords or keys in plain text. Treat the state like a secret: use an encrypted remote backend with restricted access and do not commit it to Git.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0023","card":"q-terraform-003-state-file-and-providers","sec":"terraform-s004","q":"Why does Terraform need a state file when it could just ask the cloud what exists?","a":"<p>Cloud APIs do not know which resources belong to which configuration, so Terraform needs the mapping from config to real objects. State also stores dependency metadata and speeds up planning on large infrastructures, and it lets Terraform know what to delete when a block is removed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0024","card":"q-terraform-003-state-file-and-providers","sec":"terraform-s004","q":"In Terraform, how does the <code>required_providers</code> block differ from a <code>provider</code> block?","a":"<p><code>required_providers</code> (inside the <code>terraform</code> block) says which provider plugin and version range the configuration needs and where to get it. The <code>provider</code> block configures an instance of it (region, credentials, endpoints). The first drives <code>init</code>, the second drives how API calls are made.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform {\n  required_providers {\n    aws = {\n      source  = &quot;hashicorp/aws&quot;\n      version = &quot;~&gt; 5.0&quot;\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0025","card":"q-terraform-003-state-file-and-providers","sec":"terraform-s004","q":"Why should you never edit the <code>terraform.tfstate</code> file by hand?","a":"<p>It is a JSON file with a serial number and lineage that Terraform uses to keep it consistent, and a mistake can orphan or duplicate resources. Use <code>terraform state</code> subcommands, <code>import</code> or <code>moved</code> blocks instead, which keep state consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0026","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"What is the difference between <code>terraform.tfvars</code> and <code>variables.tf</code>?","a":"<p><code>variables.tf</code> declares variables (type, default). <code>terraform.tfvars</code> sets their values.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0027","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"How do you define variables and assign values in Terraform?","a":"<p>Define:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>variable &quot;size&quot; { \n    type = string \n    default = &quot;t2.micro&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Assign through the default, a tfvars file, <code>-var</code>, <code>TF_VAR_</code> environment variables, or the interactive prompt.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0028","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"Which backends does Terraform support, and why use a remote one?","a":"<p>Local, S3, Terraform Cloud and others. A remote backend gives team access, locking and durability.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0029","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"What is HCL, and how does it compare to JSON in Terraform?","a":"<p>HCL is a readable, comment-friendly config language. JSON is verbose and machine-oriented. Both work, but HCL is human-first.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0030","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"In Terraform, what is the order of precedence when the same variable is set in several places?","a":"<p>From lowest to highest: the <code>default</code> in the variable block, environment variables (<code>TF_VAR_name</code>), <code>terraform.tfvars</code>, <code>terraform.tfvars.json</code>, <code>*.auto.tfvars</code> files (alphabetical), and finally <code>-var</code> and <code>-var-file</code> on the command line (in the order given). A later source wins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0031","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"In Terraform, what does it mean that a variable has no <code>default</code>, and how is its value supplied?","a":"<p>The variable is required. If no tfvars file, <code>-var</code> or <code>TF_VAR_</code> value is supplied, Terraform prompts interactively, which breaks non-interactive CI runs. So pipelines pass values explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0032","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"In Terraform, what is the purpose of <code>backend</code> configuration, and why can it not use normal variables?","a":"<p>A backend defines where state is stored and how locking works. Backend settings are read during <code>init</code>, before variables are evaluated, so they cannot reference input variables. Use a partial configuration with <code>terraform init -backend-config=...</code> for per-environment values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0033","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"When would you choose the local backend, and when a remote backend?","a":"<p>Local state is fine for learning and throwaway experiments by one person. For anything shared, use a remote backend because it gives shared access, locking, encryption and durability, and a CI pipeline can run without a developer's laptop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0034","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"Why do many teams choose HCL over JSON for Terraform files, even though both are supported?","a":"<p>HCL supports comments, expressions, interpolation and is far easier to read and review. Terraform JSON syntax exists mainly for configurations produced by other programs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0035","card":"q-terraform-004-variables-backends-and-hcl","sec":"terraform-s005","q":"What is the difference between an argument and a block in HCL?","a":"<p>An argument assigns a value to a name (<code>instance_type = \"t3.micro\"</code>). A block has a type, optional labels and a body in braces, and can hold arguments and nested blocks (<code>resource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" { ... }</code>, <code>ingress { ... }</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0036","card":"q-terraform-005-installing-terraform-and-data-sources","sec":"terraform-s006","q":"How do you install Terraform, and what is needed?","a":"<p>Download the binary, add it to PATH and verify. You need an OS, internet access and optionally Git.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform -version</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0037","card":"q-terraform-005-installing-terraform-and-data-sources","sec":"terraform-s006","q":"What is the difference between a resource and a data source?","a":"<p>A resource is managed infrastructure (Terraform creates and deletes it). A data source is a read-only lookup of something that already exists.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0038","card":"q-terraform-005-installing-terraform-and-data-sources","sec":"terraform-s006","q":"In Terraform, how do you make sure everyone on a team and in CI uses the same Terraform CLI version?","a":"<p>Set <code>required_version</code> in the <code>terraform</code> block so a wrong CLI fails fast, and install a pinned version in CI (or use a version manager such as <code>tfenv</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform {\n  required_version = &quot;&gt;= 1.5.0, &lt; 2.0.0&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0039","card":"q-terraform-005-installing-terraform-and-data-sources","sec":"terraform-s006","q":"In Terraform, when would you use a data source instead of a resource block?","a":"<p>When the object already exists and is managed elsewhere (a latest AMI, an existing VPC, a secret value, an account ID) and you only need to read its attributes. A data source never creates, changes or deletes anything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0040","card":"q-terraform-005-installing-terraform-and-data-sources","sec":"terraform-s006","q":"What does a Terraform data source look like, and how do you reference its result?","a":"<p>Declare it with <code>data</code> and read its attributes with the <code>data.</code> prefix:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>data &quot;aws_ami&quot; &quot;ubuntu&quot; {\n  most_recent = true\n  owners      = [&quot;099720109477&quot;]\n  filter {\n    name   = &quot;name&quot;\n    values = [&quot;ubuntu/images/hvm-ssd/ubuntu-jammy-22.04-amd64-server-*&quot;]\n  }\n}\n\nresource &quot;aws_instance&quot; &quot;web&quot; {\n  ami           = data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id\n  instance_type = &quot;t3.micro&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>This avoids hardcoding an AMI ID that differs per region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0041","card":"q-terraform-005-installing-terraform-and-data-sources","sec":"terraform-s006","q":"In Terraform, when does a data source get read, and what happens if its arguments depend on a resource that is not created yet?","a":"<p>Normally during the plan's refresh phase. If an argument depends on a value that is only known after apply, the read is deferred to apply time and the plan shows its attributes as \"known after apply\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0042","card":"q-terraform-006-standard-workflow-and-plan","sec":"terraform-s007","q":"What is the typical Terraform workflow?","a":"<p><code>init</code> (setup), <code>plan</code> (preview), <code>apply</code> (execute), <code>destroy</code> (teardown).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0043","card":"q-terraform-006-standard-workflow-and-plan","sec":"terraform-s007","q":"What happens if you apply without a plan?","a":"<p>Terraform plans and applies automatically. This is risky because there is no preview.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0044","card":"q-terraform-006-standard-workflow-and-plan","sec":"terraform-s007","q":"How do you preview changes without applying?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform plan</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0045","card":"q-terraform-006-standard-workflow-and-plan","sec":"terraform-s007","q":"What do the symbols <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>~</code> and <code>-/+</code> in a Terraform plan mean?","a":"<p><code>+</code> create, <code>-</code> destroy, <code>~</code> update in place, <code>-/+</code> destroy and recreate (replace). A forced replacement matters most for databases and stateful resources, so look for <code>-/+</code> and the \"forces replacement\" note on the changed argument before approving.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0046","card":"q-terraform-006-standard-workflow-and-plan","sec":"terraform-s007","q":"How does Terraform build the plan internally?","a":"<p>It reads the configuration, refreshes the state against the real infrastructure, builds a dependency graph, compares desired and actual attributes per resource and produces an ordered set of create, update and delete actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0047","card":"q-terraform-006-standard-workflow-and-plan","sec":"terraform-s007","q":"What is the benefit of running <code>terraform plan</code> in a pull request pipeline?","a":"<p>Reviewers see the exact infrastructure diff before merge, so mistakes such as an unexpected replacement or a destroy are caught in review rather than in production. Teams post the plan output as a PR comment and require approval before apply.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0048","card":"q-terraform-006-standard-workflow-and-plan","sec":"terraform-s007","q":"In Terraform, how do you plan a change for one resource only, and why is it discouraged as routine practice?","a":"<p>Use <code>-target=aws_instance.web</code>. It ignores dependencies outside the target, can leave state and configuration out of sync, and hides pending changes. Use it only for recovery from errors or emergencies, then run a full plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0049","card":"q-terraform-006-standard-workflow-and-plan","sec":"terraform-s007","q":"What does <code>terraform apply -replace=aws_instance.web</code> do?","a":"<p>It forces that resource to be destroyed and recreated during this apply, even without a configuration change. It is the modern way to recreate a broken resource (Terraform 0.15.2+), replacing <code>terraform taint</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0050","card":"q-terraform-007-refresh-validate-and-format","sec":"terraform-s008","q":"What does <code>terraform refresh</code> do?","a":"<p>It syncs state with real infrastructure without changing any resource.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0051","card":"q-terraform-007-refresh-validate-and-format","sec":"terraform-s008","q":"How do you validate a Terraform configuration?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform validate</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0052","card":"q-terraform-007-refresh-validate-and-format","sec":"terraform-s008","q":"What is the purpose of <code>terraform fmt</code>?","a":"<p>It formats code consistently.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform fmt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0053","card":"q-terraform-007-refresh-validate-and-format","sec":"terraform-s008","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>terraform validate</code> and <code>terraform plan</code>?","a":"<p><code>validate</code> checks syntax and internal consistency (argument names, types, references) without contacting any provider API or state. <code>plan</code> also reads state and real infrastructure to compute changes and can fail because of credentials or API problems. Validate is fast and fits as a first CI step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0054","card":"q-terraform-007-refresh-validate-and-format","sec":"terraform-s008","q":"What is the status of <code>terraform refresh</code> in current Terraform versions, and what replaces it?","a":"<p>Since Terraform 0.15.4 the standalone <code>terraform refresh</code> command is deprecated because it writes state with no review step. Use <code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code> to see drift, and <code>terraform apply -refresh-only</code> to accept it into state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0055","card":"q-terraform-007-refresh-validate-and-format","sec":"terraform-s008","q":"How do you run <code>terraform fmt</code> so it fails a CI job when files are unformatted?","a":"<p>Use the check flag, which exits non-zero when any file needs formatting without changing the files:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform fmt -check -recursive</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0056","card":"q-terraform-007-refresh-validate-and-format","sec":"terraform-s008","q":"Why does <code>terraform validate</code> need <code>terraform init</code> first in most setups?","a":"<p>Validation needs the provider schemas to check resource arguments, and those come from the downloaded providers. For a syntax-only check without a backend you can run <code>terraform init -backend=false</code> first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0057","card":"q-terraform-007-refresh-validate-and-format","sec":"terraform-s008","q":"How would you detect that someone changed a resource manually in the console without changing any configuration?","a":"<p>Run <code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code> (or a normal plan). It compares real attributes with state and shows the drift. Then either apply the configuration to revert the change or update the configuration to match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0058","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"How do you import existing infrastructure into Terraform?","a":"<p>Define the resource block, then import it.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform import aws_instance.example i-1234567890abcdef0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0059","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"What is <code>terraform taint</code>, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>It marks a resource for recreation on the next apply. Use it for corrupted or stuck resources.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform taint aws_instance.example</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0060","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"How do you roll back changes if something fails?","a":"<p>Revert the configuration and apply again. There is no automatic rollback, so rely on Git and backups.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0061","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"What does <code>terraform output</code> do?","a":"<p>It shows output values after apply.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform output</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0062","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"What does <code>terraform import</code> change, and what does it not do?","a":"<p>It attaches an existing real object to a resource address in state. It does not write configuration for you (in classic CLI import), so you must write a matching resource block, then run <code>plan</code> until it shows no changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0063","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"In Terraform 1.5+, how does an <code>import</code> block differ from the <code>terraform import</code> command?","a":"<p>An <code>import</code> block is declarative: it lives in code, is previewed by <code>plan</code>, goes through review and can be applied in CI. With <code>terraform plan -generate-config-out=imported.tf</code> it can also draft the resource configuration. The CLI command changes state immediately with no plan.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import {\n  to = aws_instance.example\n  id = &quot;i-1234567890abcdef0&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0064","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"In current Terraform, what replaces <code>terraform taint</code>, and why?","a":"<p><code>terraform apply -replace=&lt;address&gt;</code> (0.15.2+). <code>taint</code> modified state immediately and could be forgotten, so a teammate's next apply might destroy the resource unexpectedly. <code>-replace</code> is part of a reviewed plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0065","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"How do you read a single output value in a script, and why use <code>-raw</code> or <code>-json</code>?","a":"<p><code>terraform output -raw vpc_id</code> prints the plain string without quotes or newline, ideal for shell variables. <code>terraform output -json</code> prints structured data for tools such as <code>jq</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0066","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"What is the difference between an <code>output</code> block and a <code>local</code> value in Terraform?","a":"<p>An output exposes a value to the caller (CLI, a parent module or remote state readers). A local is a named expression visible only inside its own module. Use locals to avoid repeating expressions and outputs for interfaces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0067","card":"q-terraform-008-import-taint-and-output","sec":"terraform-s009","q":"Why does <code>terraform import</code> often leave a plan showing differences, and how do you resolve them?","a":"<p>Your hand-written resource block rarely matches every attribute of the real object. Read the plan diff, adjust arguments until <code>plan</code> reports no changes, and use <code>lifecycle { ignore_changes = [...] }</code> only for attributes you intentionally do not manage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0068","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"How do you define a variable with a default?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>variable &quot;region&quot; { \n    type = string \n    default = &quot;us-east-1&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0069","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"Which variable types does Terraform have?","a":"<p>String, number, bool, list, map, set, object, tuple.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0070","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"How do you pass variables on the command line?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform apply -var=&quot;size=t3.large&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0071","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"What is the difference between input and local variables?","a":"<p>Input variables (<code>variable</code>) are external and customizable. Local values (<code>locals</code>) are internal and computed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0072","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"How do you reference an output from another module?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>vpc_id = module.vpc.vpc_id</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0073","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>validation</code> inside a variable block do?","a":"<p>It rejects bad input early with a custom error message.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>variable &quot;env&quot; {\n  type = string\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([&quot;dev&quot;, &quot;stage&quot;, &quot;prod&quot;], var.env)\n    error_message = &quot;env must be dev, stage or prod.&quot;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0074","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>list(string)</code>, <code>set(string)</code> and <code>map(string)</code>?","a":"<p>A list is ordered and may contain duplicates. A set is unordered and unique. A map is a collection of key-value pairs with string keys and one value type. <code>for_each</code> accepts a map or set, not a list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0075","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"How does an <code>object</code> variable type differ from a <code>map</code>?","a":"<p>A map holds values all of the same type under arbitrary keys. An object has a fixed set of named attributes, each with its own type, so it fits structured settings and gives errors for missing or wrong attributes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0076","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"In Terraform, why would you declare a <code>type</code> for every variable?","a":"<p>It documents the interface, makes Terraform convert or reject wrong input early, and prevents confusing errors later in the plan. Without a type it accepts any value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0077","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"What is a <code>locals</code> block in Terraform, and why use it instead of a variable?","a":"<p><code>locals</code> give a name to a computed expression, such as a common tag map or a combined name. They cannot be set from outside. Use a variable for input the caller should control and a local for derived values you want to define once.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>locals {\n  common_tags = {\n    Project = &quot;billing&quot;\n    Env     = var.env\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0078","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"How do you set a variable value through an environment variable, and what is a caveat for complex types?","a":"<p>Export <code>TF_VAR_&lt;name&gt;</code>, for example <code>export TF_VAR_region=us-east-1</code>. For lists and maps the value must be valid HCL or JSON syntax, which is awkward in shells, so use a tfvars file for complex values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0079","card":"q-terraform-009-defining-variables","sec":"terraform-s010","q":"How do you pass a whole file of variable values to Terraform for one environment?","a":"<p>Use <code>-var-file</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform apply -var-file=&quot;prod.tfvars&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Files named <code>terraform.tfvars</code> or ending in <code>.auto.tfvars</code> are loaded automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0080","card":"q-terraform-010-sensitive-values","sec":"terraform-s011","q":"How do you secure sensitive variables?","a":"<p>Use tfvars files that are gitignored, environment variables (<code>TF_VAR_</code>), or Vault/Secrets Manager.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0081","card":"q-terraform-010-sensitive-values","sec":"terraform-s011","q":"What is the <code>sensitive</code> attribute?","a":"<p>It hides a variable or output in logs. Use it for secrets.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>output &quot;pass&quot; { \n    value = &quot;secret&quot; \n    sensitive = true \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0082","card":"q-terraform-010-sensitive-values","sec":"terraform-s011","q":"Does marking a Terraform variable or output <code>sensitive = true</code> encrypt it or keep it out of state?","a":"<p>No. It only redacts the value in CLI output and logs. The value is still stored in plain text in the state file, so the state itself must be protected (encrypted backend, restricted access).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0083","card":"q-terraform-010-sensitive-values","sec":"terraform-s011","q":"What is Terraform's error when a sensitive value is used in a plain output, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Terraform refuses with \"Output refers to sensitive values\" and asks you to mark the output <code>sensitive = true</code>. Doing so makes the output redacted while still allowing other modules to consume it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0084","card":"q-terraform-010-sensitive-values","sec":"terraform-s011","q":"In Terraform 1.10+, what are ephemeral values meant to solve?","a":"<p>Ephemeral resources and ephemeral input variables hold values such as tokens that are available during a run but are never written to the plan or state. This removes the old problem that secrets read through a data source ended up in state. Support depends on the provider.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0085","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"How do you use <code>count</code> with resources?","a":"<p>It multiplies the resource.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_instance&quot; &quot;server&quot; { \n    count = 2 \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0086","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"What is <code>for_each</code>, and how does it differ from <code>count</code>?","a":"<p><code>for_each</code> iterates over a map or set with keys. <code>count</code> gives only a numeric index.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for_each = { &quot;a&quot; = &quot;val1&quot;, &quot;b&quot; = &quot;val2&quot; }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0087","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"How do you conditionally create resources?","a":"<p>Use <code>count</code> with a condition.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>count = var.create ? 1 : 0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0088","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"In Terraform, what goes wrong when you use <code>count</code> over a list and then remove an item from the middle?","a":"<p>Resources are addressed by index, so every later element shifts down. Terraform sees resources at changed indexes as modified or replaced and may destroy and recreate them. <code>for_each</code> with stable keys avoids this because each instance is addressed by its key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0089","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"In Terraform, why does <code>for_each</code> fail with a list of strings, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p><code>for_each</code> accepts only a map or a set of strings. Convert the list with <code>toset(var.names)</code>. Inside the block use <code>each.key</code> and <code>each.value</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_iam_user&quot; &quot;u&quot; {\n  for_each = toset([&quot;alice&quot;, &quot;bob&quot;])\n  name     = each.value\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0090","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"In Terraform, what is the limitation on the keys passed to <code>for_each</code>?","a":"<p>The keys must be known at plan time. If they depend on a value known only after apply (for example an ID of a resource not yet created), Terraform errors with \"Invalid for_each argument\". Use static keys and put the unknown value in the instance body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0091","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"What does this expression do, and why is <code>count</code> used this way?","a":"<p>It creates the resource when <code>var.create</code> is true and zero copies otherwise, because Terraform has no <code>if</code> for blocks. Other code must then refer to it with an index, such as <code>aws_instance.x[0].id</code>, or with <code>one(...)</code>/splat.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>count = var.create ? 1 : 0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0092","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"How do you refer to all instances created with <code>count</code>, and to one created with <code>for_each</code>?","a":"<p>With count: <code>aws_instance.web[*].id</code> for all, <code>aws_instance.web[0].id</code> for one. With for_each: <code>aws_instance.web[\"key\"].id</code> for one, or <code>values(aws_instance.web)[*].id</code> for all.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0093","card":"q-terraform-011-count-for-each-and-conditionals","sec":"terraform-s012","q":"When should you choose <code>for_each</code> over <code>count</code>?","a":"<p>Choose <code>for_each</code> when instances differ by identity, have names, or may be added and removed independently. Use <code>count</code> for identical copies or the on/off conditional pattern.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0094","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"What is a Terraform module?","a":"<p>A reusable bundle of configuration that simplifies and standardizes infrastructure.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0095","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"How do you create a reusable module?","a":"<p>Make a directory with <code>main.tf</code>, <code>variables.tf</code> and <code>outputs.tf</code>, and parameterize it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0096","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"What is the difference between a root and a child module?","a":"<p>The root module is the main directory where commands run. A child module is a called submodule that can be reused.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0097","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"How do you pass variables to a module?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;vpc&quot; { \n    source = &quot;./vpc&quot; \n    cidr = &quot;10.0.0.0/16&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0098","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"What are the benefits of using modules in Terraform, and what are the costs?","a":"<p>Benefits: reuse, consistent standards, smaller configurations, one place to fix a bug and clear interfaces. Costs: another layer to understand, version management, harder debugging, and over-generic modules with dozens of variables that become harder to use than raw resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0099","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"In a Terraform module, what is the role of <code>variables.tf</code> and <code>outputs.tf</code>?","a":"<p><code>variables.tf</code> declares the inputs the caller may set, which form the module's interface. <code>outputs.tf</code> declares what values the module exposes. A caller cannot read anything inside a module that is not exported as an output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0100","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"In Terraform, why can a root module not read a resource attribute inside a child module directly?","a":"<p>Modules encapsulate their resources. The child must expose the value with an <code>output</code> block and the parent reads it as <code>module.&lt;name&gt;.&lt;output&gt;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0101","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"In Terraform, what files and calls make up a root module versus a child module?","a":"<p>Any directory with <code>.tf</code> files is a module. The root module is the directory you run <code>terraform</code> in. A child module is a directory called from another module with a <code>module</code> block that has a <code>source</code> argument.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0102","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"What makes a good Terraform module interface?","a":"<p>Few required inputs with sensible defaults, typed and validated variables, clear descriptions, outputs for everything a caller will need (IDs, ARNs), and no hardcoded region or account. Avoid provider configuration inside reusable modules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0103","card":"q-terraform-012-module-basics","sec":"terraform-s013","q":"Why should reusable Terraform modules not contain <code>provider</code> blocks?","a":"<p>A module with its own provider block cannot be used with <code>count</code>, <code>for_each</code> or <code>depends_on</code> the way callers expect, and it fixes region and credentials. Callers should configure providers in the root and pass them with the <code>providers</code> argument.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0104","card":"q-terraform-013-sources-versions-and-the-registry","sec":"terraform-s014","q":"What are module sources?","a":"<p>Local paths (<code>./</code>), Git, or the Registry. A remote source:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0105","card":"q-terraform-013-sources-versions-and-the-registry","sec":"terraform-s014","q":"How do you version Terraform modules?","a":"<p>Use Git tags (<code>v1.0.0</code>) or the Registry <code>version = \"2.0\"</code> argument.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0106","card":"q-terraform-013-sources-versions-and-the-registry","sec":"terraform-s014","q":"What is the Terraform Module Registry?","a":"<p>HashiCorp's module hub.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;s3&quot; { \n    source = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/s3-bucket/aws&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0107","card":"q-terraform-013-sources-versions-and-the-registry","sec":"terraform-s014","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between pinning a module with <code>version</code> and with a Git <code>?ref=</code>?","a":"<p><code>version</code> works only for Registry modules and supports constraints. A Git source uses <code>?ref=</code> with a tag or commit for exactness. For example:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;vpc&quot; {\n  source  = &quot;terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws&quot;\n  version = &quot;5.1.0&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;net&quot; {\n  source = &quot;git::https://example.com/org/net.git?ref=v1.2.0&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0108","card":"q-terraform-013-sources-versions-and-the-registry","sec":"terraform-s014","q":"What do the version constraint operators <code>~&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code> and <code>=</code> mean in Terraform?","a":"<p><code>= 1.2.3</code> is exact. <code>&gt;= 1.2</code> means that version or higher. <code>~&gt; 1.2</code> allows only the right-most component to increase, so it means at least 1.2 and less than 2.0. <code>~&gt; 1.2.3</code> means at least 1.2.3 and less than 1.3.0.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0109","card":"q-terraform-013-sources-versions-and-the-registry","sec":"terraform-s014","q":"Why is a floating module version (no <code>version</code> constraint) a risk in production?","a":"<p>A new release can change resources, rename variables or force replacement, so the same code produces a different plan tomorrow. Pin versions and upgrade deliberately after reviewing the plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0110","card":"q-terraform-013-sources-versions-and-the-registry","sec":"terraform-s014","q":"When would you use a local path module source instead of the Registry?","a":"<p>For modules that live in the same repository and change together with the root configuration. There is no versioning and the module is copied as is. Use a Registry or Git source when many repositories share a module.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0111","card":"q-terraform-013-sources-versions-and-the-registry","sec":"terraform-s014","q":"In Terraform, why must you run <code>terraform init -upgrade</code> after changing module or provider versions?","a":"<p><code>init</code> reuses what is already downloaded and locked. <code>-upgrade</code> re-resolves versions within the new constraints and updates the lock file and the module cache.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0112","card":"q-terraform-014-debugging-and-nesting-modules","sec":"terraform-s015","q":"How do you debug module issues?","a":"<p>Use logs (<code>TF_LOG=DEBUG</code>), <code>plan</code>, and temporary outputs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0113","card":"q-terraform-014-debugging-and-nesting-modules","sec":"terraform-s015","q":"Can you nest modules, and is there a depth limit?","a":"<p>Yes, modules can call modules. There is no hard limit, but keep nesting shallow (2-3 levels).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0114","card":"q-terraform-014-debugging-and-nesting-modules","sec":"terraform-s015","q":"How do you update a module without breaking infrastructure?","a":"<p>Test the new version, pin it, and check the plan for no recreation.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0115","card":"q-terraform-014-debugging-and-nesting-modules","sec":"terraform-s015","q":"What problems appear when module nesting becomes too deep?","a":"<p>Resource addresses get long (<code>module.a.module.b.module.c.aws_x.y</code>), outputs must be passed up level by level, changes ripple across layers and it becomes hard to see what is created. Prefer flat composition: the root wires small modules together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0116","card":"q-terraform-014-debugging-and-nesting-modules","sec":"terraform-s015","q":"How do you pass a value from one module into another module in Terraform?","a":"<p>Through the root: output from the first module becomes input to the second.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;network&quot; {\n  source = &quot;./network&quot;\n}\n\nmodule &quot;app&quot; {\n  source    = &quot;./app&quot;\n  subnet_id = module.network.subnet_id\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Terraform infers that <code>app</code> depends on <code>network</code> from the reference.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0117","card":"q-terraform-014-debugging-and-nesting-modules","sec":"terraform-s015","q":"What does it mean when <code>terraform plan</code> wants to destroy and recreate resources after you wrapped them into a module, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>The resource addresses changed (they now include <code>module.&lt;name&gt;.</code>), so Terraform thinks the old ones are gone. Use a <code>moved</code> block (1.1+) to tell Terraform the new address, or <code>terraform state mv</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>moved {\n  from = aws_instance.web\n  to   = module.web.aws_instance.this\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0118","card":"q-terraform-015-why-state-matters","sec":"terraform-s016","q":"Why is the state file key, and what if it is corrupted?","a":"<p>It tracks infrastructure. Corruption causes duplicates and errors. Restore from backup.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0119","card":"q-terraform-015-why-state-matters","sec":"terraform-s016","q":"How do you recover a lost state file?","a":"<p>Restore a backup or rebuild it with <code>terraform import</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0120","card":"q-terraform-015-why-state-matters","sec":"terraform-s016","q":"What is state drift, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Drift is a mismatch between real infrastructure and state. Detect it with <code>plan</code>. Fix it with <code>apply</code> or by updating the config.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0121","card":"q-terraform-015-why-state-matters","sec":"terraform-s016","q":"Why is the state file a concurrency problem for teams, and what fixes it?","a":"<p>Two people applying at once can read the same old state and overwrite each other's changes, corrupting it. A remote backend with state locking lets one operation hold the lock while others wait or fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0122","card":"q-terraform-015-why-state-matters","sec":"terraform-s016","q":"What should you do if <code>terraform plan</code> shows it wants to recreate a resource that already exists?","a":"<p>State probably lacks the mapping (wrong workspace or backend, lost state or a resource that was never imported). Check you are using the right backend and workspace with <code>terraform state list</code>, then import the object or restore state before applying.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0123","card":"q-terraform-015-why-state-matters","sec":"terraform-s016","q":"What are the options for detecting drift in a scheduled way, and what do you do with the result?","a":"<p>Run <code>terraform plan -detailed-exitcode</code> (or <code>-refresh-only</code>) on a schedule in CI and alert on exit code 2. Then decide: revert the manual change by applying, or update configuration to keep the change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0124","card":"q-terraform-015-why-state-matters","sec":"terraform-s016","q":"How can state versioning help after someone applies a bad change?","a":"<p>With S3 versioning enabled on the state bucket, you can restore an earlier state object. This helps for corrupted state, but it does not undo real infrastructure changes, which you still reconcile with a new plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0125","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"How do you configure a remote backend (S3, Terraform Cloud)?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform { \n    backend &quot;s3&quot; { \n        bucket = &quot;state&quot; \n        key = &quot;tfstate&quot; \n    } \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0126","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"What are the benefits of remote state?","a":"<p>Collaboration, locking, safety and versioning.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0127","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"Can you lock the state file, and why?","a":"<p>Locking is automatic with remote backends (for example DynamoDB with S3). It prevents race conditions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0128","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"How do you share state across a team?","a":"<p>Use a remote backend with access control and locking.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0129","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"What happens with two simultaneous <code>terraform apply</code> runs?","a":"<p>With local state they overwrite each other, which is chaos. With a remote backend and locking, one run fails.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0130","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"In Terraform with an S3 backend, how does state locking work, and what changed in recent versions?","a":"<p>Classic setup: a DynamoDB table with a <code>LockID</code> key (<code>dynamodb_table</code> setting) is written when an operation starts. From Terraform 1.10 (experimental, stable in 1.11) the S3 backend can lock with a lock file in the bucket (<code>use_lockfile = true</code>), so DynamoDB is no longer needed, and the DynamoDB option is deprecated.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform {\n  backend &quot;s3&quot; {\n    bucket         = &quot;state&quot;\n    key            = &quot;tfstate&quot;\n    region         = &quot;us-east-1&quot;\n    dynamodb_table = &quot;tf-locks&quot;\n    encrypt        = true\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0131","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"In an S3 backend block, what do <code>key</code> and <code>encrypt</code> control?","a":"<p><code>key</code> is the object path of the state file inside the bucket, so a different key gives separate state per project or environment. <code>encrypt = true</code> enables server-side encryption of the state object at rest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0132","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"How should you secure an S3 bucket used for Terraform state?","a":"<p>Enable versioning, server-side encryption, block all public access, restrict IAM access to the pipeline and administrators, and enable access logging. State can contain secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0133","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"A Terraform run was killed and later runs fail with \"Error acquiring the state lock\". What do you do?","a":"<p>First confirm nobody is really running an apply (check the lock info for owner and time). Then release it with <code>terraform force-unlock &lt;LOCK_ID&gt;</code>. Never force-unlock while another run is active, because that risks corrupting state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0134","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"Why do you need to create the S3 bucket and lock table before configuring the backend that uses them?","a":"<p>The backend is initialized in <code>init</code>, before any resource is created, so the storage must already exist. This bootstrap problem is usually solved with a small separate configuration (or manual or CLI creation) kept with local state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0135","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"What does <code>terraform init -migrate-state</code> do?","a":"<p>After you change the backend configuration, it copies existing state from the old backend to the new one, for example local to S3, after asking for confirmation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0136","card":"q-terraform-016-remote-backends-and-locking","sec":"terraform-s017","q":"In Terraform, how can one configuration read another configuration's outputs without sharing state files?","a":"<p>Use the <code>terraform_remote_state</code> data source for the other state's backend and read <code>data.terraform_remote_state.net.outputs.vpc_id</code>. It requires read access to the whole state, so many teams prefer publishing values via SSM Parameter Store or reading the real resources with a data source.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0137","card":"q-terraform-017-state-commands","sec":"terraform-s018","q":"How do you move a resource between state files?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform state mv -state-out=../new.tfstate aws_instance.example aws_instance.example</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0138","card":"q-terraform-017-state-commands","sec":"terraform-s018","q":"What is <code>terraform state</code>, and what are its subcommands?","a":"<p>It manages state: <code>mv</code> (move), <code>rm</code> (remove), <code>list</code>, <code>show</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0139","card":"q-terraform-017-state-commands","sec":"terraform-s018","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>terraform state rm</code> and deleting the resource block?","a":"<p><code>state rm</code> removes the resource from state only. The real object stays and Terraform stops managing it. Deleting the block and applying destroys the real object. Use <code>state rm</code> to hand a resource over to another configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0140","card":"q-terraform-017-state-commands","sec":"terraform-s018","q":"How do you rename a resource in Terraform configuration without destroying it?","a":"<p>Use a <code>moved</code> block (1.1+) or <code>terraform state mv old new</code>, then plan to confirm there are no changes.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform state mv aws_instance.old aws_instance.new</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0141","card":"q-terraform-017-state-commands","sec":"terraform-s018","q":"What do <code>terraform state list</code> and <code>terraform state show</code> give you?","a":"<p><code>list</code> prints the addresses of all resources in state. <code>show &lt;address&gt;</code> prints the stored attributes of one resource. They are the first tools to inspect what Terraform thinks exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0142","card":"q-terraform-017-state-commands","sec":"terraform-s018","q":"Why should you prefer <code>moved</code> blocks over <code>terraform state mv</code> for refactoring in a team?","a":"<p>A <code>moved</code> block is code. It is reviewed, versioned and applies the rename for every workspace and teammate when they next apply, whereas <code>state mv</code> is a one-time manual change to one state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0143","card":"q-terraform-018-multiple-providers-and-aliases","sec":"terraform-s019","q":"How do you configure multiple providers?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>provider &quot;aws&quot; { \n    region = &quot;us-east-1&quot; \n}\nprovider &quot;aws&quot; { \n    alias = &quot;west&quot; \n    region = &quot;us-west-2&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0144","card":"q-terraform-018-multiple-providers-and-aliases","sec":"terraform-s019","q":"What is provider aliasing?","a":"<p>It names provider variants, for example for multiple regions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_instance&quot; &quot;west&quot; { \n    provider = aws.west \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0145","card":"q-terraform-018-multiple-providers-and-aliases","sec":"terraform-s019","q":"In Terraform, why do you need provider aliases to create resources in two AWS regions?","a":"<p>One provider block configures one region. A second block with <code>alias</code> is a separate instance, and each resource chooses which one to use.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>provider &quot;aws&quot; {\n  alias  = &quot;west&quot;\n  region = &quot;us-west-2&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Resources with no <code>provider</code> argument use the default (unaliased) provider.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0146","card":"q-terraform-018-multiple-providers-and-aliases","sec":"terraform-s019","q":"In Terraform, how do you give a child module a non-default provider?","a":"<p>Pass it with the <code>providers</code> map in the module call:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;dr&quot; {\n  source = &quot;./app&quot;\n  providers = {\n    aws = aws.west\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0147","card":"q-terraform-018-multiple-providers-and-aliases","sec":"terraform-s019","q":"What are typical reasons to configure the same provider more than once?","a":"<p>Multi-region deployments, cross-account setups (assume a role in another account), replica or DR resources, and a CloudFront certificate that must be created in <code>us-east-1</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0148","card":"q-terraform-018-multiple-providers-and-aliases","sec":"terraform-s019","q":"In Terraform, how do you deploy to another AWS account from one run?","a":"<p>Add an aliased provider with <code>assume_role</code> for the target account's role, then point resources or modules at it. The pipeline credentials only need permission to assume that role.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>provider &quot;aws&quot; {\n  alias  = &quot;prod&quot;\n  region = &quot;us-east-1&quot;\n  assume_role {\n    role_arn = &quot;arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/terraform&quot;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0149","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"How do you specify a provider version?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform { \n    required_providers { \n        aws = { version = &quot;~&gt; 4.0&quot; } \n    } \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0150","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"What happens if you do not specify a provider version?","a":"<p>Terraform grabs the latest, which risks inconsistency and breakage.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0151","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"How do you authenticate with AWS, Azure and GCP?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>AWS</strong>: environment variables (<code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Azure</strong>: CLI login (<code>az login</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>GCP</strong>: key file (<code>GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS</code>).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0152","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"What is the difference between an official and a community provider?","a":"<p>Official providers are HashiCorp-backed and stable. Community providers are third-party with variable quality.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0153","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"How do you troubleshoot provider errors?","a":"<p>Check logs (<code>TF_LOG</code>), credentials, configuration and versions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0154","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"What does the dependency lock file <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code> record and why is it committed?","a":"<p>It records the exact provider versions and checksums selected by <code>init</code>. Committing it makes every machine and CI run use the same provider builds, and <code>init</code> fails if a package does not match the recorded hash.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0155","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"In Terraform, how does the constraint <code>~&gt; 4.0</code> on a provider behave when version 5.0 is released?","a":"<p><code>~&gt; 4.0</code> allows 4.x versions only (at least 4.0 and less than 5.0). Terraform will not move to 5.0 until you change the constraint, which is how you avoid major-version breaking changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0156","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"Why should you not put AWS access keys in the <code>provider \"aws\"</code> block in Terraform code?","a":"<p>Code ends up in Git, and the keys would be visible in history and in state or logs. Use environment variables, a shared credentials profile, or better an IAM role (instance profile, OIDC federation from CI) so there are no long-lived keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0157","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"How does a CI system such as GitHub Actions authenticate to AWS for Terraform without stored keys?","a":"<p>With OpenID Connect: the CI job gets a short-lived token, AWS IAM trusts the identity provider and lets the job assume a role, so no long-term access keys are stored as secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0158","card":"q-terraform-019-versions-and-authentication","sec":"terraform-s020","q":"What is the difference between a provider from <code>hashicorp/</code> and a third-party provider in <code>required_providers</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>source</code> address decides who publishes it. Partner and community providers use their own namespace (for example <code>integrations/github</code>), and you must trust and review them. A typo in source can also pull an unintended provider, so always set the source explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0159","card":"q-terraform-020-organizing-code-and-files","sec":"terraform-s021","q":"How do you organize Terraform code for big projects?","a":"<p>Use modules, naming conventions, environment directories, remote state and DRY principles.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0160","card":"q-terraform-020-organizing-code-and-files","sec":"terraform-s021","q":"How do you structure Terraform files?","a":"<p><code>main.tf</code>, <code>variables.tf</code>, <code>outputs.tf</code>, <code>providers.tf</code>, <code>locals.tf</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0161","card":"q-terraform-020-organizing-code-and-files","sec":"terraform-s021","q":"How do you avoid hardcoding?","a":"<p>Use variables, data sources, locals and modules.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0162","card":"q-terraform-020-organizing-code-and-files","sec":"terraform-s021","q":"How do you structure Terraform for dev, staging and prod so changes in one do not affect the others?","a":"<p>Give each environment its own root configuration and its own state (separate folders or repositories) that call shared modules with different variable values. This isolates blast radius and allows different versions per environment. Workspaces share one backend and configuration, so they isolate less.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0163","card":"q-terraform-020-organizing-code-and-files","sec":"terraform-s021","q":"In Terraform, does splitting code across several <code>.tf</code> files change behavior?","a":"<p>No. Terraform loads every <code>.tf</code> file in the directory as one module in no particular order. File names are only for human organization (<code>main.tf</code>, <code>variables.tf</code>, <code>outputs.tf</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0164","card":"q-terraform-020-organizing-code-and-files","sec":"terraform-s021","q":"What does DRY mean for Terraform code and which features support it?","a":"<p>Do not repeat yourself. Use modules for repeated stacks, <code>for_each</code> and <code>count</code> for repeated resources, <code>locals</code> for repeated expressions, variables for inputs, and <code>dynamic</code> blocks for repeated nested blocks. Too much abstraction hurts readability, so apply it where repetition is real.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0165","card":"q-terraform-020-organizing-code-and-files","sec":"terraform-s021","q":"Why should you avoid hardcoding AMI IDs, account IDs or subnet IDs in Terraform?","a":"<p>They differ by region and account and change over time, so code breaks when moved. Use variables, data source blocks (for example <code>aws_ami</code>, <code>aws_caller_identity</code>) or outputs from other modules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0166","card":"q-terraform-021-secrets-and-teamwork","sec":"terraform-s022","q":"How do you handle secrets?","a":"<p>Use <code>sensitive</code> variables, Vault, or environment variables, never hardcoded values.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0167","card":"q-terraform-021-secrets-and-teamwork","sec":"terraform-s022","q":"How do you manage Terraform in teams?","a":"<p>Remote state, workspaces, Git and CI/CD.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0168","card":"q-terraform-021-secrets-and-teamwork","sec":"terraform-s022","q":"What is the role of version control?","a":"<p>It tracks changes, enables collaboration and makes code reproducible.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0169","card":"q-terraform-021-secrets-and-teamwork","sec":"terraform-s022","q":"What should go in <code>.gitignore</code> for a Terraform repository, and what should not?","a":"<p>Ignore <code>.terraform/</code>, <code>*.tfstate</code>, <code>*.tfstate.*</code>, crash logs and <code>*.tfvars</code> that hold secrets. Do not ignore <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code>, which should be committed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0170","card":"q-terraform-021-secrets-and-teamwork","sec":"terraform-s022","q":"How do you protect production Terraform runs in a team?","a":"<p>Run plan and apply in a CI pipeline with a service role, require peer review of the code and the plan, require manual approval before apply, use remote state with locking, restrict who can change the pipeline, and use policy checks (Sentinel, OPA).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0171","card":"q-terraform-021-secrets-and-teamwork","sec":"terraform-s022","q":"Why do teams scan Terraform code with tools such as tfsec, Checkov or Trivy?","a":"<p>They catch insecure settings before apply, such as public S3 buckets, unencrypted volumes or open security groups, and fit in pull request checks as policy-as-code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0172","card":"q-terraform-021-secrets-and-teamwork","sec":"terraform-s022","q":"What is the benefit of storing Terraform in Git with pull requests, beyond history?","a":"<p>Peer review of infrastructure changes, a record of who changed what and why, easy revert, and a gate for automated checks (<code>fmt</code>, <code>validate</code>, plan, security scan) before anything reaches production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0173","card":"q-terraform-022-idempotency-dependencies-and-pitfalls","sec":"terraform-s023","q":"How do you ensure idempotency?","a":"<p>Stay declarative, avoid provisioners and use unique IDs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0174","card":"q-terraform-022-idempotency-dependencies-and-pitfalls","sec":"terraform-s023","q":"How do you handle resource dependencies?","a":"<p>Implicit dependencies come from references. Explicit ones use <code>depends_on</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0175","card":"q-terraform-022-idempotency-dependencies-and-pitfalls","sec":"terraform-s023","q":"What are common Terraform pitfalls?","a":"<p>Hardcoding secrets, manual changes, skipping <code>plan</code>, and version mismatches.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0176","card":"q-terraform-022-idempotency-dependencies-and-pitfalls","sec":"terraform-s023","q":"What is idempotency in Terraform and how does it work?","a":"<p>Applying the same configuration repeatedly gives the same infrastructure with no extra changes. Terraform compares desired attributes with state and real values and only acts on differences, so a second apply reports \"no changes\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0177","card":"q-terraform-022-idempotency-dependencies-and-pitfalls","sec":"terraform-s023","q":"What is the difference between an implicit and explicit dependency in Terraform, and which should you prefer?","a":"<p>Referencing another resource's attribute (<code>subnet_id = aws_subnet.a.id</code>) creates an implicit dependency, and Terraform orders them automatically. <code>depends_on</code> is explicit and only for hidden dependencies that no attribute reference shows. Prefer implicit references because they are precise and self-documenting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0178","card":"q-terraform-022-idempotency-dependencies-and-pitfalls","sec":"terraform-s023","q":"How does Terraform decide what can run in parallel?","a":"<p>It builds a dependency graph. Resources with no dependency between them are created concurrently (default <code>-parallelism=10</code>). Dependent resources wait for what they depend on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0179","card":"q-terraform-022-idempotency-dependencies-and-pitfalls","sec":"terraform-s023","q":"What are the typical causes of Terraform drift in a team?","a":"<p>People change things in the console or CLI, other automation modifies the same resources, autoscaling or services change attributes, and provider defaults change. The fixes are tight IAM permissions, a single pipeline for changes, <code>ignore_changes</code> for attributes managed elsewhere, and scheduled drift checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0180","card":"q-terraform-022-idempotency-dependencies-and-pitfalls","sec":"terraform-s023","q":"What does <code>lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }</code> do and when is it useful?","a":"<p>When a resource must be replaced, Terraform creates the new one first and destroys the old one after. It reduces downtime for things like load balancer targets or certificates. It fails if the two cannot exist together, for example with a fixed unique name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0181","card":"q-terraform-023-workspaces-and-environments","sec":"terraform-s024","q":"What is a Terraform workspace?","a":"<p>Multiple states in one directory.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform workspace new dev</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0182","card":"q-terraform-023-workspaces-and-environments","sec":"terraform-s024","q":"How do you manage multiple environments (dev, prod)?","a":"<p>Use workspaces, separate directories, or variables (<code>-var env=prod</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0183","card":"q-terraform-023-workspaces-and-environments","sec":"terraform-s024","q":"How do you reference the current workspace in Terraform code, and what is it commonly used for?","a":"<p>With <code>terraform.workspace</code>. Typical use: choose sizes or name prefixes per workspace.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>instance_type = terraform.workspace == &quot;prod&quot; ? &quot;m5.large&quot; : &quot;t3.micro&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0184","card":"q-terraform-023-workspaces-and-environments","sec":"terraform-s024","q":"What are the drawbacks of using workspaces for dev and prod?","a":"<p>They share the same backend, credentials and code version, so a prod change can be applied from a dev mindset by switching workspace. The current workspace is not obvious from the code. Separate directories or repositories with separate backends and credentials give stronger isolation, so workspaces suit identical copies of one stack.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0185","card":"q-terraform-023-workspaces-and-environments","sec":"terraform-s024","q":"In an S3 backend, where does Terraform store the state of non-default workspaces?","a":"<p>Under a <code>env:/&lt;workspace&gt;/</code> prefix before the configured key, in the same bucket. The default workspace uses the key directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0186","card":"q-terraform-023-workspaces-and-environments","sec":"terraform-s024","q":"What do <code>terraform workspace list</code>, <code>select</code> and <code>new</code> do?","a":"<p><code>list</code> shows all workspaces, <code>select</code> switches to one, and <code>new</code> creates and switches to it, each with its own empty state. Be careful: <code>show</code> prints the current workspace and you should check it before apply.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0187","card":"q-terraform-023-workspaces-and-environments","sec":"terraform-s024","q":"Are Terraform CLI workspaces the same as Terraform Cloud workspaces?","a":"<p>No. CLI workspaces are multiple state files in one configuration directory. A Terraform Cloud workspace is a full environment with its own state, variables, credentials, permissions and run history, usually tied to one configuration and one environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0188","card":"q-terraform-024-depends-on-and-dynamic-blocks","sec":"terraform-s025","q":"What is <code>depends_on</code>?","a":"<p>It forces ordering when implicit dependencies are not enough.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>depends_on = [aws_vpc.main]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0189","card":"q-terraform-024-depends-on-and-dynamic-blocks","sec":"terraform-s025","q":"How do you handle dynamic blocks?","a":"<p>A <code>dynamic</code> block loops over nested configuration.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dynamic &quot;ingress&quot; { \n    for_each = var.rules \n    content { \n        from_port = ingress.value.port \n    } \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0190","card":"q-terraform-024-depends-on-and-dynamic-blocks","sec":"terraform-s025","q":"When is <code>depends_on</code> really needed in Terraform, and what is its cost?","a":"<p>When one resource relies on another's side effect that no attribute shows, for example an IAM policy attachment that must exist before an instance uses the permissions. Cost: with <code>depends_on</code> on a module or data source, Terraform may defer reads and show more \"known after apply\" values, and it can force unnecessary updates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0191","card":"q-terraform-024-depends-on-and-dynamic-blocks","sec":"terraform-s025","q":"What does a Terraform <code>dynamic</code> block replace, and what are its parts?","a":"<p>It generates repeated nested blocks (such as several <code>ingress</code> rules) from a collection instead of writing each by hand. <code>for_each</code> supplies the collection, the block label is the iterator name, and <code>content</code> holds the body.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dynamic &quot;ingress&quot; {\n  for_each = var.rules\n  content {\n    from_port = ingress.value.port\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0192","card":"q-terraform-024-depends-on-and-dynamic-blocks","sec":"terraform-s025","q":"In a Terraform <code>dynamic \"ingress\"</code> block, how do you refer to the current element?","a":"<p>Using the block label as the iterator: <code>ingress.value</code> for the element and <code>ingress.key</code> for the key or index. You can rename it with the <code>iterator</code> argument.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0193","card":"q-terraform-024-depends-on-and-dynamic-blocks","sec":"terraform-s025","q":"When should you avoid <code>dynamic</code> blocks?","a":"<p>When there are only a few static blocks. Overuse makes the configuration harder to read and debug, so use dynamic blocks only when the number or content of nested blocks truly varies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0194","card":"q-terraform-024-depends-on-and-dynamic-blocks","sec":"terraform-s025","q":"How does a dynamic block differ from <code>for_each</code> on the resource?","a":"<p><code>for_each</code> on a resource creates multiple resources. A <code>dynamic</code> block creates multiple nested blocks inside one resource, such as many ingress rules in one security group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0195","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"What is a provisioner, and when should you avoid it?","a":"<p>A provisioner runs scripts after creation. Avoid it for non-idempotent tasks and use Ansible instead.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0196","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"How do you use Terraform with CI/CD?","a":"<p>Automate plan and apply, and secure credentials.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>run: terraform init &amp;&amp; terraform apply -auto-approve</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0197","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"What is the difference between <code>apply</code> and <code>-auto-approve</code>?","a":"<p><code>apply</code> prompts for confirmation. <code>-auto-approve</code> skips the prompt and executes automatically.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0198","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"Why does HashiCorp call provisioners a last resort in Terraform?","a":"<p>They run arbitrary scripts that Terraform cannot model or plan, are not idempotent, depend on SSH or WinRM connectivity and failed runs leave tainted resources. Prefer user data or cloud-init, pre-built images (Packer), or a configuration tool such as Ansible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0199","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"What do the <code>local-exec</code> and <code>remote-exec</code> provisioners do?","a":"<p><code>local-exec</code> runs a command on the machine running Terraform. <code>remote-exec</code> runs commands on the created resource over SSH or WinRM, so it needs network access and credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0200","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"What happens to a resource when its provisioner fails during creation?","a":"<p>The resource is created but Terraform marks it as tainted and will replace it on the next apply. You can set <code>on_failure = continue</code> to ignore the failure, which is usually unwise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0201","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"What does <code>-auto-approve</code> mean for how you should build a pipeline?","a":"<p>It removes the confirmation, so the safety must come from elsewhere: a reviewed plan (apply the saved plan file), a manual approval gate for production, and a limited-permission role.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>run: terraform init &amp;&amp; terraform apply -auto-approve</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0202","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"What are the typical stages of a Terraform CI/CD pipeline?","a":"<p><code>fmt -check</code>, <code>init</code>, <code>validate</code>, security scan and policy checks, <code>plan</code> (saved and posted for review), approval, then <code>apply</code> of the saved plan. Pull requests run up to plan, and apply runs after merge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0203","card":"q-terraform-025-provisioners-ci-cd-and-approval","sec":"terraform-s026","q":"Why is <code>terraform apply -auto-approve</code> run from a developer laptop risky compared with a pipeline?","a":"<p>It depends on the local credentials and local code state and leaves no shared audit trail or review. A pipeline gives consistent versions, controlled credentials, history and approvals.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0204","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"How do you handle large-scale infrastructure?","a":"<p>Modules, split state, parallel runs and <code>-target</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0205","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"What is Terraform Sentinel?","a":"<p>Policy-as-code for Terraform Cloud that blocks non-compliant applies.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0206","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"How do you integrate Terraform with Ansible or Chef?","a":"<p>Terraform provisions and Ansible configures. Pass outputs such as IPs between them.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0207","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"How do Terraform and Ansible differ when managing AWS resources through Python?","a":"<p>Terraform is declarative (desired state) and Ansible is procedural (steps). Terraform provisions (for example EC2 instances) and Ansible configures (installing software). Terraform keeps a state file and Ansible is stateless. Python drives Terraform through <code>python-terraform</code> or <code>subprocess</code>, and Ansible through its Python API or custom modules.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0208","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"How do you keep Terraform fast and safe as an infrastructure grows to hundreds of resources?","a":"<p>Split into several states by layer or team (network, data, apps) to reduce plan time and blast radius, use modules, share values through outputs or remote state, tune <code>-parallelism</code>, and avoid <code>-target</code> as a routine habit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0209","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"What are the trade-offs of splitting state into many small states?","a":"<p>Benefits: faster plans, smaller blast radius, separate permissions. Costs: cross-state dependencies are managed manually, ordering between stacks must be handled in pipelines and refactoring across states needs state moves.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0210","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"How does policy as code differ from linting in a Terraform workflow?","a":"<p>Linting checks style and syntax. Policy as code (Sentinel, or Open Policy Agent with Conftest) enforces organizational rules on the planned changes, such as \"no public buckets\" or \"only approved instance types\", and can block an apply.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0211","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"How do Terraform outputs help hand off values to Ansible?","a":"<p>Run <code>terraform output -json</code>, read the instance IPs and build an inventory for Ansible, or use a dynamic inventory plugin. Provisioning stays in Terraform and configuration in Ansible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0212","card":"q-terraform-026-scale-policy-and-integration","sec":"terraform-s027","q":"What is Terraform Cloud and what does it add over running the CLI yourself?","a":"<p>It is HashiCorp's hosted service (now part of HCP Terraform) with remote state and locking, remote runs, a private module registry, team access control, policy checks and run history. It removes the need to build state storage and pipeline plumbing yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0213","card":"q-terraform-027-logging","sec":"terraform-s028","q":"How do you enable detailed logging?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>export TF_LOG=DEBUG</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0214","card":"q-terraform-027-logging","sec":"terraform-s028","q":"What is <code>TF_LOG</code>?","a":"<p>It sets the log level (<code>TRACE</code>, <code>DEBUG</code>, and so on) for debugging.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0215","card":"q-terraform-027-logging","sec":"terraform-s028","q":"How do you write Terraform debug logs to a file instead of the terminal?","a":"<p>Set <code>TF_LOG_PATH</code> together with <code>TF_LOG</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>export TF_LOG=DEBUG\nexport TF_LOG_PATH=./terraform.log</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0216","card":"q-terraform-027-logging","sec":"terraform-s028","q":"What does <code>TF_LOG_PROVIDER</code> do and how does it help?","a":"<p>It sets the log level for provider plugins separately from Terraform core (Terraform 0.15.0+ with <code>TF_LOG_CORE</code> for the core). It lets you inspect provider API calls without noise from core.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0217","card":"q-terraform-027-logging","sec":"terraform-s028","q":"Which Terraform log level is the most detailed, and why must you be careful sharing the log?","a":"<p><code>TRACE</code>. Logs can contain request and response data, resource attributes and sometimes credentials or tokens, so review and redact them before sharing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0218","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"How do you fix a \"resource already exists\" error?","a":"<p>Import the resource.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform import aws_instance.example i-1234567890abcdef0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0219","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"How do you fix a \"provider config not present\" error?","a":"<p>Add the missing provider block and re-run init.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0220","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"How do you resolve dependency cycle errors?","a":"<p>Refactor references, use data sources, and inspect <code>terraform graph</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0221","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"Why does <code>terraform plan</code> fail?","a":"<p>Syntax errors, provider issues, conflicts or drift.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0222","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"What does \"Error: Provider configuration not present\" usually mean, and how can it occur?","a":"<p>A resource in state belongs to a provider configuration that is no longer in the configuration, often after removing a provider block or alias while resources still exist. Restore the provider block, or remove the resources properly first. Then run <code>init</code> and <code>plan</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0223","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"What does \"Error: Cycle\" mean in Terraform and how do you break it?","a":"<p>Two or more resources depend on each other directly or indirectly, so there is no valid order. Common case: two security groups referencing each other inline. Break the cycle by using separate rule resources (<code>aws_security_group_rule</code>) or removing one dependency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0224","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"You get \"Error: Unsupported argument\" after a provider upgrade. What do you check?","a":"<p>The provider's changelog and upgrade guide, because an argument may have been renamed, removed or moved to a separate resource. Pin the previous version if needed and update the configuration deliberately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0225","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"Why might <code>terraform apply</code> fail with \"AccessDenied\" even though plan succeeded?","a":"<p>Plan mostly needs read permissions, while apply needs write permissions for every action. The role used by the pipeline lacks the create or modify permission, so grant least privilege for the exact actions that failed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0226","card":"q-terraform-028-common-errors","sec":"terraform-s029","q":"What does \"Error: Inconsistent dependency lock file\" mean?","a":"<p>The selected provider versions in <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code> do not match the configuration's constraints, for example after you edited a version constraint. Run <code>terraform init -upgrade</code> and commit the updated lock file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0227","card":"q-terraform-029-crashes-and-graph","sec":"terraform-s030","q":"What if Terraform crashes mid-run?","a":"<p>Check the logs, inspect the state, then retry the apply.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0228","card":"q-terraform-029-crashes-and-graph","sec":"terraform-s030","q":"How do you debug a silent module failure?","a":"<p>Check logs, isolate the module, add outputs and run plan.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0229","card":"q-terraform-029-crashes-and-graph","sec":"terraform-s030","q":"What is <code>terraform graph</code>?","a":"<p>It maps dependencies.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform graph | dot -Tpng &gt; graph.png</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0230","card":"q-terraform-029-crashes-and-graph","sec":"terraform-s030","q":"What should you check first after a Terraform process crashes in the middle of an apply?","a":"<p>Terraform writes a <code>crash.log</code> and you check whether state was updated. Run <code>terraform state list</code> and <code>plan</code>, and force-unlock if the lock remains. Resources created before the crash may or may not be in state, so import anything missing, then run apply again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0231","card":"q-terraform-029-crashes-and-graph","sec":"terraform-s030","q":"How do you read the output of <code>terraform graph</code>?","a":"<p>It prints a DOT-format dependency graph. Nodes are resources, providers and variables, and edges show dependencies. Render it with Graphviz (<code>dot -Tpng</code>) to see ordering, find cycles and understand why one resource waits on another.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0232","card":"q-terraform-029-crashes-and-graph","sec":"terraform-s030","q":"What is the benefit of <code>terraform plan -out</code> when debugging unexpected changes?","a":"<p>You can read the exact plan with <code>terraform show tfplan</code> (or <code>-json</code> for tools) and compare it with the configuration, instead of re-planning against a state that may have changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0233","card":"q-terraform-030-vpc-terraform-cloud-and-manual-deletion","sec":"terraform-s031","q":"How do you deploy an AWS VPC with subnets?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>module &quot;vpc&quot; { \n    source = &quot;./vpc&quot; \n    public_subnet_cidrs = [&quot;10.0.1.0/24&quot;] \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0234","card":"q-terraform-030-vpc-terraform-cloud-and-manual-deletion","sec":"terraform-s031","q":"How do you migrate to Terraform Cloud?","a":"<p>Add the backend, run init and push the state.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>backend &quot;remote&quot; { \n    organization = &quot;my-org&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0235","card":"q-terraform-030-vpc-terraform-cloud-and-manual-deletion","sec":"terraform-s031","q":"How do you fix a manually deleted resource?","a":"<p>Run <code>apply</code> to recreate it, or <code>terraform state rm</code> to forget it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0236","card":"q-terraform-030-vpc-terraform-cloud-and-manual-deletion","sec":"terraform-s031","q":"What happens if someone deletes a Terraform-managed resource in the console, and what are your options?","a":"<p>The next <code>plan</code> refreshes state, sees the object missing and plans to create it again. Apply to recreate it. If the resource should no longer exist, remove it from the configuration or use <code>terraform state rm</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0237","card":"q-terraform-030-vpc-terraform-cloud-and-manual-deletion","sec":"terraform-s031","q":"How do you migrate existing local state to Terraform Cloud with minimal risk?","a":"<p>Back up the state file, add the <code>cloud</code> (or <code>remote</code>) backend block, run <code>terraform init -migrate-state</code>, confirm the copy, then run <code>plan</code> and expect no changes. Move variables and credentials into workspace settings.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>terraform {\n  cloud {\n    organization = &quot;my-org&quot;\n    workspaces {\n      name = &quot;prod&quot;\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0238","card":"q-terraform-030-vpc-terraform-cloud-and-manual-deletion","sec":"terraform-s031","q":"How would you build a VPC with public and private subnets in Terraform, using which resources?","a":"<p><code>aws_vpc</code>, one <code>aws_subnet</code> per tier and availability zone, <code>aws_internet_gateway</code> and a public route table with a default route to it, an <code>aws_nat_gateway</code> with an elastic IP and a private route table pointing to it, and route table associations. Wrap these in a module with CIDRs as variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0239","card":"q-terraform-031-high-availability-and-kubernetes","sec":"terraform-s032","q":"How do you build a highly available app across regions?","a":"<p>Use multiple provider aliases and Route 53 failover.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0240","card":"q-terraform-031-high-availability-and-kubernetes","sec":"terraform-s032","q":"How do you automate a Kubernetes cluster?","a":"<p>Use the EKS module with CI/CD.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_eks_cluster&quot; &quot;example&quot; { \n    name = &quot;my-cluster&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0241","card":"q-terraform-031-high-availability-and-kubernetes","sec":"terraform-s032","q":"How would you design Terraform for a highly available application in AWS?","a":"<p>Spread subnets and instances over at least two availability zones using <code>for_each</code> over a list of zones, put an Auto Scaling group behind a load balancer, use Multi-AZ for databases, and for multi-region add a second aliased provider with Route 53 health checks and failover records.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0242","card":"q-terraform-031-high-availability-and-kubernetes","sec":"terraform-s032","q":"What are the typical Terraform pieces for creating a Kubernetes cluster on a cloud, and what is a common structure?","a":"<p>Network (VPC, subnets), the managed cluster (<code>aws_eks_cluster</code>, node groups, IAM roles), then Kubernetes-level resources through the <code>kubernetes</code> or <code>helm</code> providers. Many teams keep cluster creation and in-cluster workloads in separate states, because the Kubernetes provider needs the cluster to exist first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0243","card":"q-terraform-031-high-availability-and-kubernetes","sec":"terraform-s032","q":"Why is it a problem to configure the Kubernetes provider from a cluster created in the same Terraform configuration?","a":"<p>The provider's credentials depend on values unknown at plan time, which can fail on first apply or when the cluster is replaced. Splitting into two configurations, or using a dedicated data source and apply ordering, is more reliable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0244","card":"q-terraform-032-rate-limits-and-environment-differences","sec":"terraform-s033","q":"How do you handle a failure caused by rate limits?","a":"<p>Lower <code>-parallelism</code>, retry, and split the configuration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0245","card":"q-terraform-032-rate-limits-and-environment-differences","sec":"terraform-s033","q":"A config works in dev but fails in prod. What do you do?","a":"<p>Compare variables, logs and permissions, then standardize.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0246","card":"q-terraform-032-rate-limits-and-environment-differences","sec":"terraform-s033","q":"How do you replicate on-prem infrastructure?","a":"<p>Map it to cloud equivalents (VPCs, VMs) and start with a proof of concept.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0247","card":"q-terraform-032-rate-limits-and-environment-differences","sec":"terraform-s033","q":"Terraform apply fails with API throttling errors from the cloud provider. What do you do?","a":"<p>Lower concurrency with <code>-parallelism=N</code>, rely on the provider's retry settings (for AWS, <code>max_retries</code>), split large configurations into smaller states, and stagger pipelines that hit the same account.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0248","card":"q-terraform-032-rate-limits-and-environment-differences","sec":"terraform-s033","q":"What does <code>-parallelism</code> control and what is its default?","a":"<p>It limits how many resource operations Terraform runs concurrently while walking the graph. The default is 10. Lowering it avoids API rate limits and raising it may speed up large applies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0249","card":"q-terraform-032-rate-limits-and-environment-differences","sec":"terraform-s033","q":"A Terraform configuration works in dev but fails in prod. Which differences do you check?","a":"<p>Variable values and defaults, provider and module versions, IAM permissions and service quotas in the prod account, existing resources and naming collisions, region and AZ availability, and policies such as SCPs. Compare <code>plan</code> output and logs in both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0250","card":"q-terraform-032-rate-limits-and-environment-differences","sec":"terraform-s033","q":"How do you plan to bring an on-premises environment under Terraform?","a":"<p>Inventory what exists, map each component to a cloud or provider resource, build a small proof of concept, use import for resources that must be kept, and move in stages with testing and a rollback path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0251","card":"q-terraform-033-counts-and-conditions","sec":"terraform-s034","q":"How do you create 50 EC2 instances with unique names?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_instance&quot; &quot;example&quot; { \n    count = 50 \n    tags = { \n        Name = &quot;instance-${count.index + 1}&quot; \n    } \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0252","card":"q-terraform-033-counts-and-conditions","sec":"terraform-s034","q":"How do you deploy conditionally by environment?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>count = var.env == &quot;prod&quot; ? 2 : 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0253","card":"q-terraform-033-counts-and-conditions","sec":"terraform-s034","q":"In Terraform, why is <code>count.index + 1</code> used when naming resources?","a":"<p><code>count.index</code> starts at 0, so adding 1 gives human-friendly names starting at 1.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tags = {\n  Name = &quot;instance-${count.index + 1}&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0254","card":"q-terraform-033-counts-and-conditions","sec":"terraform-s034","q":"How do you make a Terraform setting differ between environments with a conditional expression?","a":"<p>Use the ternary operator <code>condition ? true_value : false_value</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>count = var.env == &quot;prod&quot; ? 2 : 1</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0255","card":"q-terraform-033-counts-and-conditions","sec":"terraform-s034","q":"What happens when you change the <code>count</code> value of a resource from 3 to 2?","a":"<p>Terraform destroys the instance with the highest index (<code>[2]</code>) and keeps <code>[0]</code> and <code>[1]</code>. If the removed instance is not the one you intended, switch to <code>for_each</code> with keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0256","card":"q-terraform-033-counts-and-conditions","sec":"terraform-s034","q":"Why does <code>count</code> with a large number need caution for resources like EC2 instances?","a":"<p>Each instance is a separate API creation, so you can hit quotas, API rate limits and plan time, and a mistaken value can create costly resources. Check the plan and use a variable with validation for upper bounds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0257","card":"q-terraform-034-aws","sec":"terraform-s035","q":"How do you configure Terraform for AWS?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>provider &quot;aws&quot; { \n    region = &quot;us-east-1&quot; \n}\nresource &quot;aws_vpc&quot; &quot;main&quot; { \n    cidr_block = &quot;10.0.0.0/16&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0258","card":"q-terraform-034-aws","sec":"terraform-s035","q":"How do IAM roles differ in Terraform versus the Console?","a":"<p>Terraform is code and repeatable. The Console is manual and quick.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0259","card":"q-terraform-034-aws","sec":"terraform-s035","q":"How do you read secrets from AWS Secrets Manager or Vault?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>data &quot;aws_secretsmanager_secret_version&quot; &quot;secret&quot; { \n    secret_id = &quot;my-secret&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0260","card":"q-terraform-034-aws","sec":"terraform-s035","q":"How would you read a secret in Terraform from AWS Secrets Manager, and what is the caveat?","a":"<p>Use the <code>aws_secretsmanager_secret_version</code> data source and read <code>.secret_string</code>. The retrieved value is stored in state in plain text, so the state needs protection, or consider letting the service manage the secret itself.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>data &quot;aws_secretsmanager_secret_version&quot; &quot;secret&quot; {\n  secret_id = &quot;my-secret&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0261","card":"q-terraform-034-aws","sec":"terraform-s035","q":"What is an advantage of defining IAM roles in Terraform rather than clicking in the console?","a":"<p>The permissions are reviewed in pull requests, repeatable across accounts, tracked in Git and drift-checked, so a manual widening of permissions is noticed at the next plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0262","card":"q-terraform-035-azure-and-gcp","sec":"terraform-s036","q":"How do you create an Azure resource group and VNet?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;azurerm_resource_group&quot; &quot;rg&quot; { \n    name = &quot;my-rg&quot; \n}\nresource &quot;azurerm_virtual_network&quot; &quot;vnet&quot; { \n    name = &quot;my-vnet&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0263","card":"q-terraform-035-azure-and-gcp","sec":"terraform-s036","q":"How do you create a GCP Compute Engine instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;google_compute_instance&quot; &quot;vm&quot; { \n    name = &quot;my-vm&quot; \n    machine_type = &quot;e2-medium&quot; \n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0264","card":"q-terraform-035-azure-and-gcp","sec":"terraform-s036","q":"How does the Terraform workflow differ between Azure and AWS?","a":"<p>The workflow is the same (<code>init</code>, <code>plan</code>, <code>apply</code>). Only the provider (<code>azurerm</code> versus <code>aws</code>), authentication (Azure CLI, service principal or managed identity) and resource types change. In Azure, resources must belong to a resource group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0265","card":"q-terraform-035-azure-and-gcp","sec":"terraform-s036","q":"What makes <code>azurerm_resource_group</code> special among Azure resources in Terraform?","a":"<p>Almost every Azure resource requires a resource group name and location, so the group is created first and others reference its attributes (an implicit dependency).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0266","card":"q-terraform-035-azure-and-gcp","sec":"terraform-s036","q":"How do you authenticate Terraform to Google Cloud in CI, and what is better than a downloaded key file?","a":"<p>A service account key in <code>GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS</code> works but is a long-lived secret. Prefer workload identity federation so the pipeline impersonates a service account without a key file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0267","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"How do you stay updated with Terraform?","a":"<p>Docs, GitHub, blogs and labs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0268","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"What is the most complex infrastructure you have deployed?","a":"<p>A multi-region HA app, with challenges around drift, dependencies and secrets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0269","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"How do you explain Terraform to a newbie?","a":"<p>\"Blueprints for cloud, auto-built.\"</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0270","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"What are Terraform's limitations?","a":"<p>State fragility and no real-time view. Mitigate with remote state and drift checks.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0271","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"How does Terraform compare with other IaC tools?","a":"<p>It is multi-cloud and declarative, versus Ansible (configuration management) and CloudFormation (AWS-only).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0272","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"What are the main limitations of Terraform that you should be ready to discuss?","a":"<p>State is a single point of fragility and can hold secrets, there is no automatic rollback, drift is only seen when you plan, provider coverage can lag new cloud features, HCL has limited programming constructs, and large states get slow. The mitigation is remote state, versioning, small states, scheduled drift checks and review.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0273","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"In a Terraform failure that leaves half the resources created, what is the recovery approach?","a":"<p>Terraform does not roll back. Successfully created resources are recorded in state, so fix the cause and apply again to continue. If the failure left a stuck resource, replace it with <code>-replace</code>, or import and reconcile anything that exists but is missing in state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0274","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"What was a difficult Terraform problem and how would you answer this kind of behavioral question?","a":"<p>Pick a real case and use a structure: the situation (for example state drift after manual changes or a lock conflict), what you did (investigation with <code>plan -refresh-only</code>, <code>state</code> commands, import) and the result and prevention (locking, IAM restrictions, drift checks in CI).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0275","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"How would you introduce Terraform to a team that manages cloud resources by hand?","a":"<p>Start with a small non-critical stack, set up remote state and a CI plan, import existing resources gradually, write a few shared modules, add review and policy checks, and train the team. Avoid a big-bang rewrite.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0276","card":"q-terraform-036-experience-and-limitations","sec":"terraform-s037","q":"What would make you choose a different tool than Terraform?","a":"<p>For an AWS-only team that wants managed stack rollback, CloudFormation or CDK. When you want to write infrastructure in a general programming language, Pulumi or CDK for Terraform. For in-machine configuration, Ansible. Terraform's own license changed to BUSL in 2023, and OpenTofu is an open-source fork some teams choose.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0277","card":"q-terraform-037-create-an-ec2-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s038","q":"In Terraform, what are the minimum arguments that an <code>aws_instance</code> resource needs, and which one decides the operating system?","a":"<p><code>ami</code> and <code>instance_type</code> are the required arguments. The <code>ami</code> decides the operating system and its pre-installed software. <code>instance_type</code> decides CPU and memory. <code>subnet_id</code>, <code>key_name</code> and security groups are optional but are normally set so the instance lands in the right network and can be reached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0278","card":"q-terraform-037-create-an-ec2-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s038","q":"Why is hardcoding an AMI ID in an <code>aws_instance</code> a problem, and how would you avoid it in Terraform?","a":"<p>AMI IDs are region-specific and old ones get deprecated or removed, so the same code breaks in another region or later. Look the AMI up with a <code>data \"aws_ami\"</code> block (filter by name pattern and owner, <code>most_recent = true</code>) or read an SSM public parameter. The trade-off: a \"latest\" lookup can change the AMI between plans and force instance replacement, so pin the filter or use <code>ignore_changes</code> on <code>ami</code> if that is not wanted.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_instance&quot; &quot;my_ec2&quot; {\n  ami           = &quot;ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0&quot;\n  instance_type = &quot;t2.micro&quot;\n  ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0279","card":"q-terraform-037-create-an-ec2-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s038","q":"In Terraform, which <code>aws_instance</code> argument do you change to resize an EC2 instance, and what happens to the instance on <code>apply</code>?","a":"<p>Change <code>instance_type</code>. For EBS-backed instances the AWS provider stops the instance, changes the type and starts it again, so there is downtime but the instance ID and disk stay. Changing <code>ami</code> or <code>subnet_id</code> is different: it forces replacement (destroy and create).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0280","card":"q-terraform-037-create-an-ec2-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s038","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between an argument that updates an <code>aws_instance</code> in place and one that forces replacement, and how do you see it before applying?","a":"<p>Some arguments (tags, <code>instance_type</code>, <code>vpc_security_group_ids</code>) can be changed on the live resource. Others (<code>ami</code>, <code>subnet_id</code>, <code>availability_zone</code>, <code>key_name</code>) cannot, so Terraform destroys and recreates it. <code>terraform plan</code> marks the second kind with <code>-/+</code> and \"forces replacement\" next to the argument. A changed <code>user_data</code> is updated in place (the instance is stopped and started) unless <code>user_data_replace_on_change = true</code> is set, which forces replacement. Always read that marker before approving a plan for a production host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0281","card":"q-terraform-037-create-an-ec2-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s038","q":"In Terraform, why would you write <code>subnet_id = aws_subnet.my_subnet.id</code> instead of a literal subnet ID in an EC2 resource?","a":"<p>A reference gives Terraform an implicit dependency, so it creates the subnet first and passes the real ID. A literal ID works only if the subnet already exists and ties the code to one account and region. Use a literal (or a variable or data source) only for resources Terraform does not manage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0282","card":"q-terraform-038-attach-a-security-group-to-an-ec2-instan","sec":"terraform-s039","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>security_groups</code> and <code>vpc_security_group_ids</code> on an <code>aws_instance</code>, and which should you use in a VPC?","a":"<p><code>security_groups</code> takes group names and is meant for EC2-Classic or the default VPC. <code>vpc_security_group_ids</code> takes group IDs and is the right argument for instances in a non-default VPC or subnet. With a subnet set, use <code>vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.my_sg.id]</code>. Changing <code>security_groups</code> on a running instance in a VPC can force replacement, while changing <code>vpc_security_group_ids</code> updates the instance in place.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_instance&quot; &quot;my_ec2_with_sg&quot; {\n   ...\n   subnet_id     = &quot;subnet-12345678&quot;\n   security_groups = [aws_security_group.my_sg.name]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0283","card":"q-terraform-038-attach-a-security-group-to-an-ec2-instan","sec":"terraform-s039","q":"In Terraform, how does referencing <code>aws_security_group.my_sg.id</code> from an EC2 resource affect the order of creation?","a":"<p>It creates an implicit dependency. Terraform builds the security group first, then the instance, and on destroy removes the instance first. You do not need <code>depends_on</code> for this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0284","card":"q-terraform-038-attach-a-security-group-to-an-ec2-instan","sec":"terraform-s039","q":"In Terraform, an EC2 instance should use several security groups. How do you attach them and how do the rules combine?","a":"<p>Put all IDs in the list: <code>vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.web.id, aws_security_group.ssh.id]</code>. The rules are additive. Traffic is allowed if any attached group allows it, because security groups only have allow rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0285","card":"q-terraform-038-attach-a-security-group-to-an-ec2-instan","sec":"terraform-s039","q":"In Terraform, an EC2 instance should sit behind a load balancer. How should its security group allow web traffic?","a":"<p>Allow the port only from the load balancer's security group, using <code>security_groups = [aws_security_group.alb.id]</code> in the ingress rule, instead of <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>. Then the instance cannot be reached directly from the internet even if it has a public IP.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0286","card":"q-terraform-039-attach-an-iam-role-to-an-ec2-instance-us","sec":"terraform-s040","q":"Why does Terraform need an <code>aws_iam_instance_profile</code> to give an EC2 instance an IAM role?","a":"<p>EC2 does not attach a role directly. It attaches an instance profile, which is a container that holds the role. The AWS console creates the profile silently, but in Terraform (and the API) you must create it yourself and set <code>iam_instance_profile</code> on the instance.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_iam_instance_profile&quot; &quot;my_profile&quot; {\n   name = &quot;my-instance-profile&quot;\n   role = aws_iam_role.my_role.name\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0287","card":"q-terraform-039-attach-an-iam-role-to-an-ec2-instance-us","sec":"terraform-s040","q":"In Terraform, what must the trust policy (<code>assume_role_policy</code>) of a role for EC2 contain, and what does it do?","a":"<p>It names who may assume the role: <code>Principal = { Service = \"ec2.amazonaws.com\" }</code> with <code>Action = \"sts:AssumeRole\"</code>. This is the trust policy. It is separate from the permission policies attached to the role, which say what the role may do.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0288","card":"q-terraform-039-attach-an-iam-role-to-an-ec2-instance-us","sec":"terraform-s040","q":"In Terraform, what is the benefit of an instance profile over putting access keys on the instance?","a":"<p>The instance receives short-lived credentials from the metadata service and they rotate automatically. Nothing secret is stored on disk, in user data or in state, and you can remove access by detaching the policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0289","card":"q-terraform-039-attach-an-iam-role-to-an-ec2-instance-us","sec":"terraform-s040","q":"In Terraform, why is <code>iam_instance_profile</code> set to the profile <code>name</code> and not to the role name or ARN?","a":"<p>The argument accepts the instance profile's name. Giving the role's name works only by coincidence, when the profile has the same name. Referencing <code>aws_iam_instance_profile.my_profile.name</code> also creates the dependency, so the profile exists before the instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0290","card":"q-terraform-040-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s041","q":"In Terraform, why must the <code>availability_zone</code> of an <code>aws_ebs_volume</code> match the instance it is attached to?","a":"<p>An EBS volume lives in one Availability Zone and can only attach to an instance in the same zone. A mismatch makes the attachment fail. Best practice is to reference <code>aws_instance.my_ec2.availability_zone</code> instead of typing the zone.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_ebs_volume&quot; &quot;my_volume&quot; {\n  availability_zone = &quot;us-east-1a&quot;\n  size              = 10\n  type              = &quot;gp3&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0291","card":"q-terraform-040-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s041","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>aws_volume_attachment</code> do that <code>aws_ebs_volume</code> does not?","a":"<p><code>aws_ebs_volume</code> only creates the disk. <code>aws_volume_attachment</code> connects it to an instance at a device name. Because of the references, Terraform creates both the volume and the instance first.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_volume_attachment&quot; &quot;my_attachment&quot; {\n  device_name = &quot;/dev/xvdf&quot;\n  volume_id   = aws_ebs_volume.my_volume.id\n  instance_id = aws_instance.my_ec2.id\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0292","card":"q-terraform-040-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s041","q":"After Terraform attaches an EBS volume, is it ready to use on the instance?","a":"<p>No. The attachment only presents a raw block device. You still have to create a file system (for example <code>mkfs</code>) and mount it, usually through user data, Ansible or cloud-init. On Nitro instances the device appears as an NVMe name such as <code>/dev/nvme1n1</code>, not <code>/dev/xvdf</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0293","card":"q-terraform-040-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s041","q":"In Terraform, when would you use <code>ebs_block_device</code> inside <code>aws_instance</code> instead of a separate <code>aws_volume_attachment</code>?","a":"<p>Use the inline block for disks that live and die with the instance. Use a separate volume and attachment when the data must outlive the instance or be moved between instances, since you can then detach, snapshot and re-attach it independently. Do not mix both styles for the same volume, because they conflict.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0294","card":"q-terraform-040-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s041","q":"A Terraform <code>destroy</code> hangs on an <code>aws_volume_attachment</code> while the instance is running. Why, and what are the options?","a":"<p>Detaching a volume that is still mounted in the guest operating system can stall. Unmount the volume first or stop the instance. The resource also has <code>skip_destroy</code> (leave it attached) and <code>force_detach</code> (force it, with a risk of data corruption) as last resorts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0295","card":"q-terraform-040-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s041","q":"In Terraform, what is the advantage of <code>gp3</code> over <code>gp2</code> for an <code>aws_ebs_volume</code>?","a":"<p>With <code>gp3</code> you set IOPS and throughput independently of size (baseline 3000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s) and it costs less per GB. With <code>gp2</code> performance is tied to volume size. For new volumes <code>gp3</code> is usually the better default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0296","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"In Terraform, why does an <code>aws_autoscaling_group</code> need a launch template?","a":"<p>The group only manages how many instances run. The launch template says what each instance looks like: AMI, instance type, key, security groups, user data. Without a template (or the older launch configuration) the group cannot start instances.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_autoscaling_group&quot; &quot;my_asg&quot; {\n   ...\n   launch_template {\n     id      = aws_launch_template.my_lt.id\n     version = &quot;$Latest&quot;\n   }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0297","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"In AWS Auto Scaling, why are launch templates preferred over launch configurations?","a":"<p>Launch configurations are deprecated and AWS no longer adds new features to them. Launch templates are versioned, support multiple instance types, Spot and On-Demand mixes, newer instance features and in-place editing by creating a new version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0298","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>version = \"$Latest\"</code> and <code>\"$Default\"</code> for a launch template in an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p><code>$Latest</code> always uses the newest template version, so a new version applies to future launches without changing the group. <code>$Default</code> uses whichever version is marked as default. A number pins a version. Pinning is safer for production because changes are explicit. An instance refresh is needed to replace already running instances.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0299","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"A new launch template version was created by Terraform, but old instances in the Auto Scaling group still run the old AMI. Why, and how do you roll it out?","a":"<p>A launch template change only affects instances launched afterwards. Add an <code>instance_refresh</code> block to the <code>aws_autoscaling_group</code> (strategy <code>Rolling</code>) or replace instances manually. The refresh replaces instances in batches while respecting <code>min_healthy_percentage</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0300","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>min_size</code>, <code>max_size</code> and <code>desired_capacity</code> control in an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p><code>min_size</code> and <code>max_size</code> are the hard limits scaling policies cannot cross. <code>desired_capacity</code> is the number of instances the group tries to run right now and must be between them. Scaling policies change the desired capacity at run time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0301","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"In Terraform, why do teams add <code>lifecycle { ignore_changes = [desired_capacity] }</code> to an <code>aws_autoscaling_group</code> that has scaling policies?","a":"<p>Scaling policies change the live desired capacity. Without the ignore rule, the next <code>terraform apply</code> sees a difference from the configured number and resets the group to it, which can shrink a busy fleet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0302","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>vpc_zone_identifier</code> do on an Auto Scaling group, and why list subnets in several zones?","a":"<p>It lists the subnets in which instances may be launched. Using subnets in different Availability Zones spreads instances so one zone failing does not take the service down, and the group rebalances across them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0303","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"In an Auto Scaling group behind a load balancer, why should <code>health_check_type</code> be set to <code>ELB</code>?","a":"<p>The default <code>EC2</code> check only notices stopped or failed instances. With <code>ELB</code> the group also replaces instances that the load balancer reports as unhealthy, such as an application that has crashed on a running host. Set <code>health_check_grace_period</code> so new instances can boot before checks count.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0304","card":"q-terraform-041-create-an-auto-scaling-group-using-terra","sec":"terraform-s042","q":"In Terraform, why does an Auto Scaling group use <code>name_prefix</code> on the launch template with <code>create_before_destroy</code> in many configurations?","a":"<p>A launch template name must be unique. With <code>name_prefix</code>, Terraform can create a replacement with a new name before removing the old one, and <code>create_before_destroy</code> makes the group switch to the new template without a gap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0305","card":"q-terraform-042-create-an-rds-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s043","q":"In Terraform, why should the <code>password</code> argument of <code>aws_db_instance</code> not be a literal in the code, and what are the alternatives?","a":"<p>A literal ends up in Git and in the state file. Better options: pass a <code>sensitive</code> variable from a secret store, read it from AWS Secrets Manager, or in AWS provider 5.x set <code>manage_master_user_password = true</code> so RDS creates and rotates the secret itself. Even with variables the password can still land in state, so protect the state backend.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_db_instance&quot; &quot;my_rds&quot; {\n  ...\n  username            = &quot;admin&quot;\n  password            = &quot;MySecurePassword123&quot;\n  ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0306","card":"q-terraform-042-create-an-rds-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s043","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>allocated_storage</code> and <code>instance_class</code> mean on an <code>aws_db_instance</code>?","a":"<p><code>allocated_storage</code> is the disk size in GiB. <code>instance_class</code> is the compute size, such as <code>db.t3.micro</code>. They are separate: you can scale compute without changing storage and the reverse. Storage can be increased but not reduced.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0307","card":"q-terraform-042-create-an-rds-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s043","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>engine</code> select on an <code>aws_db_instance</code>, and why is <code>engine_version</code> worth setting?","a":"<p><code>engine</code> selects the database software (<code>mysql</code>, <code>postgres</code>, <code>mariadb</code>, <code>oracle-ee</code> and so on). Setting <code>engine_version</code> pins the version. If left empty, RDS picks the current default at creation, and a later minor upgrade by RDS can show as drift unless <code>auto_minor_version_upgrade</code> and <code>ignore_changes</code> are considered.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0308","card":"q-terraform-042-create-an-rds-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s043","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>multi_az = true</code> on an <code>aws_db_instance</code> give you, and what does it not give you?","a":"<p>It adds a synchronous standby in another Availability Zone with automatic failover, for availability. It does not add read capacity (the standby cannot be read in a classic Multi-AZ instance) and is not a backup. For reads use read replicas.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0309","card":"q-terraform-042-create-an-rds-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s043","q":"In Terraform, which <code>aws_db_instance</code> arguments do you set for a production database beyond the basics?","a":"<p><code>multi_az</code>, <code>storage_encrypted = true</code> with a KMS key, <code>backup_retention_period</code> greater than 0, <code>deletion_protection = true</code>, <code>skip_final_snapshot = false</code> with a <code>final_snapshot_identifier</code>, <code>publicly_accessible = false</code>, and a parameter group. Together they cover availability, security and recoverability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0310","card":"q-terraform-042-create-an-rds-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s043","q":"A Terraform change to an <code>aws_db_instance</code> argument such as <code>instance_class</code> shows as applied but the database is unchanged. Why?","a":"<p>RDS applies many modifications at the next maintenance window unless <code>apply_immediately = true</code> is set. The change is recorded as pending on the instance. <code>apply_immediately</code> can cause a brief outage, so use it deliberately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0311","card":"q-terraform-042-create-an-rds-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s043","q":"In Terraform, why does <code>terraform destroy</code> of an <code>aws_db_instance</code> fail with a snapshot error when <code>skip_final_snapshot</code> is not set?","a":"<p><code>skip_final_snapshot</code> defaults to <code>false</code>, so RDS wants a final snapshot and Terraform requires <code>final_snapshot_identifier</code>. Either set the identifier (keep the data) or set <code>skip_final_snapshot = true</code> for throwaway environments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0312","card":"q-terraform-042-create-an-rds-instance-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s043","q":"In AWS provider v4 and later, which argument names the initial database on <code>aws_db_instance</code>?","a":"<p><code>db_name</code>. The older <code>name</code> argument was renamed. If the argument is left out, MySQL creates no initial database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0313","card":"q-terraform-043-attach-a-security-group-to-an-rds-instan","sec":"terraform-s044","q":"In Terraform, how do you attach a security group to an <code>aws_db_instance</code>, and why does it differ from <code>aws_instance</code>?","a":"<p>Set <code>vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.rds_sg.id]</code> on the <code>aws_db_instance</code>. It takes IDs of VPC security groups. The argument name matches the one used by <code>aws_instance</code> in a VPC, but RDS has no <code>security_groups</code> argument for VPC use (the older <code>db_security_groups</code> is for EC2-Classic).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0314","card":"q-terraform-043-attach-a-security-group-to-an-rds-instan","sec":"terraform-s044","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>db_subnet_group_name</code> do on an <code>aws_db_instance</code>?","a":"<p>It places the database in the subnets listed in an <code>aws_db_subnet_group</code>, which decides the VPC and Availability Zones RDS may use. Without it, RDS uses the default VPC's default subnet group. The subnet group must hold subnets in at least two Availability Zones.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>vpc_security_group_ids = [&quot;sg-12345678&quot;]\ndb_subnet_group_name = &quot;my-subnet-group&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0315","card":"q-terraform-043-attach-a-security-group-to-an-rds-instan","sec":"terraform-s044","q":"In Terraform, how do <code>publicly_accessible</code> and the security group on an RDS instance work together?","a":"<p>Both must allow access. <code>publicly_accessible = true</code> gives the instance a public address, and the security group must still allow the client's source. Keep <code>publicly_accessible = false</code> for application databases and reach them from inside the VPC.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0316","card":"q-terraform-044-create-an-rds-snapshot-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s045","q":"In Terraform, what does the <code>aws_db_snapshot</code> resource do, and how is it different from automated backups?","a":"<p>It takes one manual snapshot when Terraform creates the resource. It is not a schedule. Automated backups come from <code>backup_retention_period</code> and <code>backup_window</code> on the instance and give point-in-time recovery. Manual snapshots do not expire by themselves.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_db_snapshot&quot; &quot;my_rds_snapshot&quot; {\n    db_instance_identifier = &quot;my-rds-instance&quot;\n    db_snapshot_identifier = &quot;my-rds-snapshot&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0317","card":"q-terraform-044-create-an-rds-snapshot-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s045","q":"Is <code>aws_db_snapshot</code> a good way to run regular database backups, and what should you use instead?","a":"<p>No. It runs only when the resource is created or replaced. For regular backups use the instance's automated backups, AWS Backup plans, or a scheduled job. In Terraform, use <code>aws_backup_plan</code> and <code>aws_backup_selection</code> for a managed schedule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0318","card":"q-terraform-044-create-an-rds-snapshot-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s045","q":"In Terraform, how do you restore an RDS database from a snapshot?","a":"<p>Create an <code>aws_db_instance</code> with <code>snapshot_identifier</code> pointing at the snapshot. RDS creates a new instance from it, so also set the instance class, subnet group and security groups, and expect a new endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0319","card":"q-terraform-044-create-an-rds-snapshot-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s045","q":"What happens to a manual RDS snapshot managed by Terraform when you run <code>terraform destroy</code>?","a":"<p>Terraform deletes the snapshot with the other resources, so the backup disappears with the stack. For snapshots that must outlive the code, keep them out of the configuration or protect them with <code>prevent_destroy</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0320","card":"q-terraform-044-create-an-rds-snapshot-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s045","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>final_snapshot_identifier</code> on an <code>aws_db_instance</code> and an <code>aws_db_snapshot</code> resource?","a":"<p>The final snapshot is taken by RDS automatically at deletion of the instance. <code>aws_db_snapshot</code> takes a snapshot at creation time of the snapshot resource. The first protects against accidental deletion, the second creates a restore point before a risky change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0321","card":"q-terraform-045-create-an-s3-bucket-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s046","q":"In Terraform, what makes the <code>bucket</code> argument of <code>aws_s3_bucket</code> tricky, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>Bucket names are unique across all AWS accounts in a partition, so a common name fails with \"BucketAlreadyExists\". Add a unique suffix with <code>bucket_prefix</code>, a <code>random_id</code> resource or an account ID. Names are also immutable: changing <code>bucket</code> forces replacement.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_s3_bucket&quot; &quot;my_bucket&quot; {\n  bucket = &quot;my-unique-bucket-name&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0322","card":"q-terraform-045-create-an-s3-bucket-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s046","q":"In AWS provider v4 and later, how do you enable versioning and encryption on an S3 bucket?","a":"<p>Use separate resources: <code>aws_s3_bucket_versioning</code>, <code>aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration</code>, <code>aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration</code> and similar, each pointing at the bucket's ID. Before v4 these were nested blocks inside <code>aws_s3_bucket</code>, which are now deprecated or removed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0323","card":"q-terraform-045-create-an-s3-bucket-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s046","q":"Why does <code>terraform destroy</code> fail on a non-empty S3 bucket, and what are the options?","a":"<p>S3 refuses to delete a bucket with objects. Set <code>force_destroy = true</code> on <code>aws_s3_bucket</code> so Terraform empties it first, or empty it by other means. Be careful: <code>force_destroy</code> deletes data permanently, and with versioning enabled it also removes all versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0324","card":"q-terraform-045-create-an-s3-bucket-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s046","q":"What are the safe defaults you should apply to an S3 bucket created with Terraform?","a":"<p>Block public access with <code>aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block</code>, enable default encryption, turn on versioning for important data, enforce TLS through a bucket policy, and add logging if needed. New buckets already block public access and disable ACLs by default (since 2023), but code should still say so to stay explicit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0325","card":"q-terraform-045-create-an-s3-bucket-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s046","q":"In Terraform, how does an <code>aws_s3_bucket</code> used for application data differ from one used for Terraform state?","a":"<p>A state bucket needs versioning (to recover older state), encryption, strict access limited to the Terraform role, and should be created outside the configuration it stores state for. Application buckets may be created and destroyed with their stack.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0326","card":"q-terraform-046-create-a-dynamodb-table-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s047","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>hash_key</code> and the <code>attribute</code> block define on an <code>aws_dynamodb_table</code>?","a":"<p><code>hash_key</code> is the partition key, the attribute DynamoDB hashes to decide where an item is stored. The <code>attribute</code> block declares its name and type (<code>S</code> string, <code>N</code> number, <code>B</code> binary). Only attributes used as keys (table or index) are declared. Other attributes are schemaless.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_dynamodb_table&quot; &quot;my_dynamodb_table&quot; {\n    name           = &quot;my-dynamodb-table&quot;\n    billing_mode   = &quot;PAY_PER_REQUEST&quot;\n    hash_key       = &quot;id&quot;\n    attribute {\n        name = &quot;id&quot;\n        type = &quot;S&quot;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0327","card":"q-terraform-046-create-a-dynamodb-table-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s047","q":"In DynamoDB, what is the difference between <code>PAY_PER_REQUEST</code> and <code>PROVISIONED</code> billing, and when do you choose each?","a":"<p><code>PAY_PER_REQUEST</code> (on-demand) charges per read and write with no capacity planning, which suits spiky or unknown traffic. <code>PROVISIONED</code> needs <code>read_capacity</code> and <code>write_capacity</code>, is cheaper for steady, predictable traffic and can be combined with auto scaling. On-demand can cost more at constant high load.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0328","card":"q-terraform-046-create-a-dynamodb-table-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s047","q":"In Terraform, how do you add a sort key to an <code>aws_dynamodb_table</code>, and what must you declare?","a":"<p>Set <code>range_key = \"created_at\"</code> and declare it in an <code>attribute</code> block as well. The table key is then the combination of hash and range key, which allows queries over a range within one partition. Changing keys forces table replacement.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0329","card":"q-terraform-046-create-a-dynamodb-table-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s047","q":"In Terraform, why does an <code>aws_dynamodb_table</code> fail validation when you declare an <code>attribute</code> that is not used by a key?","a":"<p>DynamoDB only accepts attribute definitions for key schema elements (table keys and index keys). An extra declared attribute makes the API reject the request. Non-key fields need no declaration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0330","card":"q-terraform-046-create-a-dynamodb-table-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s047","q":"What Terraform settings protect a production DynamoDB table?","a":"<p><code>point_in_time_recovery { enabled = true }</code>, server-side encryption with a KMS key, <code>deletion_protection_enabled = true</code>, and <code>lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }</code>. Add <code>ttl</code> for expiring items.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0331","card":"q-terraform-047-create-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s048","q":"In Terraform, what does the <code>cidr_block</code> of an <code>aws_vpc</code> decide, and what should you consider when choosing it?","a":"<p>It defines the private IP range of the whole network. Choose a range that does not overlap with other VPCs, on-premises networks or VPNs you will connect later, because overlapping ranges cannot be peered or routed. A <code>/16</code> gives about 65,000 addresses, which subnets then split. A VPC's primary CIDR cannot be changed afterwards (you can add secondary ones).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_vpc&quot; &quot;my_vpc&quot; {\n  cidr_block = &quot;10.0.0.0/16&quot;\n  ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0332","card":"q-terraform-047-create-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s048","q":"In Terraform, which <code>aws_vpc</code> arguments do you set so instances get DNS names?","a":"<p><code>enable_dns_support = true</code> (the default) and <code>enable_dns_hostnames = true</code>. Hostnames are off by default in a custom VPC, and services such as RDS public endpoints and VPC endpoints with private DNS need them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0333","card":"q-terraform-047-create-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s048","q":"What does a VPC contain by default when created with <code>aws_vpc</code> in Terraform, and what is missing?","a":"<p>AWS creates a main route table, a default network ACL and a default security group. It does not create subnets, an internet gateway or routes to the internet, so nothing is reachable until you add them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0334","card":"q-terraform-047-create-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s048","q":"In Terraform, why add tags such as <code>Name</code> to an <code>aws_vpc</code>?","a":"<p><code>Name</code> shows in the console and helps people and tools find the network. Tags also support cost allocation and ownership. The <code>default_tags</code> setting of the AWS provider can apply a common set automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0335","card":"q-terraform-048-attach-a-subnet-to-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s049","q":"In Terraform, how does an <code>aws_subnet</code> become part of a VPC, and what else decides its address space?","a":"<p><code>vpc_id</code> links it to the VPC. The <code>cidr_block</code> must be a range inside the VPC's block and not overlap another subnet. A subnet lives in exactly one Availability Zone.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_subnet&quot; &quot;my_subnet&quot; {\n   vpc_id     = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id\n   cidr_block = &quot;10.0.1.0/24&quot;\n   availability_zone = &quot;us-east-1a&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0336","card":"q-terraform-048-attach-a-subnet-to-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s049","q":"In Terraform, does a subnet become public just by setting <code>map_public_ip_on_launch = true</code>?","a":"<p>No. That argument only gives new instances a public IP. A subnet is public when its route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to an internet gateway. Without that route, the public IP is useless.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0337","card":"q-terraform-048-attach-a-subnet-to-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s049","q":"In Terraform, how do you create one subnet per Availability Zone without copying blocks?","a":"<p>Use <code>for_each</code> or <code>count</code> over a list of CIDRs and zones, for example <code>availability_zone = data.aws_availability_zones.available.names[count.index]</code> and <code>cidr_block = cidrsubnet(aws_vpc.my_vpc.cidr_block, 8, count.index)</code>. Using a data source avoids hardcoding zones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0338","card":"q-terraform-048-attach-a-subnet-to-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s049","q":"In Terraform, what does the <code>cidrsubnet</code> function do?","a":"<p>It calculates a subnet range from a parent block: <code>cidrsubnet(\"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, 1)</code> returns <code>10.0.1.0/24</code>. The second argument adds bits to the prefix and the third picks which sub-range. It avoids manual CIDR errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0339","card":"q-terraform-048-attach-a-subnet-to-a-vpc-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s049","q":"Why do AWS services such as RDS or load balancers need subnets in more than one Availability Zone?","a":"<p>A single-zone subnet makes the service exposed to one zone failing. An RDS subnet group and an Application Load Balancer require subnets in at least two zones, so the service can fail over or spread traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0340","card":"q-terraform-049-attach-an-internet-gateway-to-a-vpc-usin","sec":"terraform-s050","q":"In Terraform, what does an <code>aws_internet_gateway</code> do and what else is needed before an instance can reach the internet?","a":"<p>It attaches a gateway to the VPC so traffic can enter and leave. You also need a route table entry for <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> that targets the gateway, a subnet associated with that route table, and a public IP (auto-assigned or Elastic IP) on the instance. Security groups must also allow the traffic.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_internet_gateway&quot; &quot;my_igw&quot; {\n    vpc_id = &quot;vpc-12345678&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0341","card":"q-terraform-049-attach-an-internet-gateway-to-a-vpc-usin","sec":"terraform-s050","q":"In Terraform, why is it better to write <code>vpc_id = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id</code> than a literal <code>vpc-12345678</code> on an internet gateway?","a":"<p>The reference creates the VPC first and avoids copy errors. A literal ID breaks when the VPC is recreated and hides the dependency from Terraform's graph.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0342","card":"q-terraform-049-attach-an-internet-gateway-to-a-vpc-usin","sec":"terraform-s050","q":"How many internet gateways can a VPC have, and is an internet gateway a single point of failure?","a":"<p>A VPC can have only one. It is a managed, horizontally scaled and highly available component, so it is not a single point of failure and has no bandwidth limit you manage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0343","card":"q-terraform-050-attach-a-route-table-to-a-subnet-using-t","sec":"terraform-s051","q":"In Terraform, why are both <code>aws_route_table</code> and <code>aws_route_table_association</code> needed?","a":"<p><code>aws_route_table</code> defines the routes. <code>aws_route_table_association</code> links it to a subnet. Without the association, the subnet uses the VPC's main route table, so a route to the internet gateway in your table would not apply.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_route_table_association&quot; &quot;my_route_table_assoc&quot; {\n    subnet_id      = &quot;subnet-12345678&quot;\n    route_table_id = aws_route_table.my_route_table.id\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0344","card":"q-terraform-050-attach-a-route-table-to-a-subnet-using-t","sec":"terraform-s051","q":"In Terraform, what does a route with <code>cidr_block = \"0.0.0.0/0\"</code> and <code>gateway_id</code> do inside an <code>aws_route_table</code>?","a":"<p>It is the default route: any traffic that does not match a more specific route (like the local VPC range) goes to the internet gateway. This is what makes a subnet public.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>route {\n    cidr_block = &quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;\n    gateway_id = aws_internet_gateway.my_igw.id\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0345","card":"q-terraform-050-attach-a-route-table-to-a-subnet-using-t","sec":"terraform-s051","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between inline <code>route</code> blocks and separate <code>aws_route</code> resources?","a":"<p>Inline blocks make the route table own all its routes, so Terraform removes any route not in the code. Separate <code>aws_route</code> resources manage single routes and are useful when other modules add routes. Do not mix both on one table, because they overwrite each other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0346","card":"q-terraform-050-attach-a-route-table-to-a-subnet-using-t","sec":"terraform-s051","q":"What is the main route table of a VPC, and why is it risky to add an internet route to it?","a":"<p>It is the table used by every subnet without an explicit association. A default route to the internet gateway on it would make all unassociated subnets public. Keep the main table without internet routes and associate public subnets with their own table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0347","card":"q-terraform-050-attach-a-route-table-to-a-subnet-using-t","sec":"terraform-s051","q":"In Terraform, how many subnets can be associated with one route table, and how many route tables with one subnet?","a":"<p>Many subnets can share one route table. A subnet can be associated with only one route table at a time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0348","card":"q-terraform-051-create-a-security-group-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s052","q":"In Terraform, an <code>aws_security_group</code> has an <code>ingress</code> block but no <code>egress</code> block. Can the instance make outbound calls?","a":"<p>No. Security groups created in the console get an allow-all outbound rule, but Terraform removes that default rule. Add an explicit <code>egress</code> block (<code>from_port = 0</code>, <code>to_port = 0</code>, <code>protocol = \"-1\"</code>, <code>cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"]</code>) if outbound traffic is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0349","card":"q-terraform-051-create-a-security-group-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s052","q":"In an <code>aws_security_group</code> rule, what does <code>protocol = \"-1\"</code> mean?","a":"<p>It means all protocols. With it, <code>from_port</code> and <code>to_port</code> must be <code>0</code>. It is typically used for the allow-all egress rule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0350","card":"q-terraform-051-create-a-security-group-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s052","q":"What is the difference between inline <code>ingress</code> blocks and <code>aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule</code> resources in Terraform?","a":"<p>Inline blocks make the group own its whole rule set, and Terraform removes unlisted rules. Separate rule resources manage one rule each and avoid the cycles that appear when two groups reference each other. Do not combine both styles on one group. The dedicated rule resources (<code>aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule</code> and <code>_egress_rule</code>) were added in AWS provider 4.x and are the recommended style in current versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0351","card":"q-terraform-051-create-a-security-group-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s052","q":"In Terraform, why do security group rules usually reference another security group instead of IP ranges for internal traffic?","a":"<p>Instances change IPs, but group membership stays. A rule such as \"allow 3306 from the app group\" stays correct when instances are replaced or scaled, and it never opens the port to unrelated hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0352","card":"q-terraform-052-create-an-elastic-ip-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s053","q":"In Terraform, why would you use an Elastic IP instead of the public IP an instance gets automatically?","a":"<p>An automatic public IP is released when the instance stops. An Elastic IP stays with your account and can be re-attached, so the address is stable for DNS, allowlists and failover. The cost is that you pay for public IPv4 addresses, including idle ones.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_eip&quot; &quot;my_eip&quot; {\n    vpc = true\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0353","card":"q-terraform-052-create-an-elastic-ip-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s053","q":"In AWS provider v5 and later, how do you request an Elastic IP for use in a VPC with <code>aws_eip</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>domain = \"vpc\"</code>. The <code>vpc = true</code> argument was deprecated in v5 in favor of <code>domain</code>. Code written for v4 with <code>vpc = true</code> shows a deprecation warning on newer versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0354","card":"q-terraform-052-create-an-elastic-ip-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s053","q":"In Terraform, what are the two ways to connect an Elastic IP to an instance, and how do they differ?","a":"<p>Set <code>instance = aws_instance.web.id</code> on the <code>aws_eip</code>, or use a separate <code>aws_eip_association</code> with <code>instance_id</code> and <code>allocation_id</code>. The association resource is useful when the address and the instance are created in different places. Use only one of the two for the same address.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0355","card":"q-terraform-052-create-an-elastic-ip-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s053","q":"In Terraform, why does an <code>aws_eip</code> often have <code>depends_on = [aws_internet_gateway.gw]</code>?","a":"<p>An Elastic IP in a VPC needs the internet gateway to exist before it can be used. The dependency is not visible in the arguments, so the AWS provider documentation recommends declaring it explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0356","card":"q-terraform-052-create-an-elastic-ip-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"terraform-s053","q":"In Terraform, how can you avoid paying for Elastic IPs that you do not need?","a":"<p>Use them only where a fixed address is required, and put other instances behind a load balancer with DNS. Since February 2024, AWS charges for all public IPv4 addresses, in use or not, so releasing idle addresses saves money.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0357","card":"q-terraform-053-create-a-load-balancer-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s054","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>load_balancer_type = \"application\"</code> and <code>\"network\"</code>?","a":"<p>An application load balancer works at layer 7 (HTTP and HTTPS): host and path routing, redirects and WAF integration. A network load balancer works at layer 4 (TCP, UDP, TLS) with very high throughput, low latency and static IPs. Choose by protocol and routing needs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lb&quot; &quot;my_alb&quot; {\n    name               = &quot;my-alb&quot;\n    internal           = false\n    load_balancer_type = &quot;application&quot;\n    ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0358","card":"q-terraform-053-create-a-load-balancer-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s054","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>internal = false</code> mean on an <code>aws_lb</code>?","a":"<p>The load balancer is internet-facing and gets public addresses in public subnets. With <code>internal = true</code> it only has private addresses and is reachable only from inside the VPC or connected networks, which suits internal services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0359","card":"q-terraform-053-create-a-load-balancer-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s054","q":"In Terraform, why is an <code>aws_lb</code> alone not enough to serve traffic?","a":"<p>A load balancer also needs an <code>aws_lb_target_group</code>, an <code>aws_lb_listener</code> that forwards a port to the group, and targets registered in the group (instances through <code>aws_lb_target_group_attachment</code> or an Auto Scaling group with <code>target_group_arns</code>). Without them it accepts no useful traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0360","card":"q-terraform-053-create-a-load-balancer-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s054","q":"In Terraform, which <code>aws_lb_listener</code> setup do you use to serve HTTPS and redirect HTTP?","a":"<p>One listener on port 443 with <code>protocol = \"HTTPS\"</code>, an ACM <code>certificate_arn</code> and an SSL policy, forwarding to the target group. A second listener on port 80 with a <code>redirect</code> default action to port 443 and status <code>HTTP_301</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0361","card":"q-terraform-053-create-a-load-balancer-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s054","q":"In Terraform, how should the security group of an application load balancer and its targets be set?","a":"<p>The load balancer group allows 80 and 443 from the internet. The target group's instances allow the application port only from the load balancer's security group, so users cannot bypass it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0362","card":"q-terraform-054-create-a-route-53-record-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s055","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>zone_id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>ttl</code> and <code>records</code> mean in an <code>aws_route53_record</code>?","a":"<p><code>zone_id</code> is the hosted zone that owns the record. <code>name</code> is the DNS name. <code>type</code> is the record type (A for IPv4, AAAA for IPv6, CNAME, MX, TXT). <code>ttl</code> is how many seconds resolvers cache the answer. <code>records</code> holds the values.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_route53_record&quot; &quot;my_record&quot; {\n    zone_id = &quot;Z1234567890ABCDEF&quot;\n    name    = &quot;www.example.com&quot;\n    type    = &quot;A&quot;\n    ttl     = 300\n    records = [&quot;192.0.2.1&quot;]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0363","card":"q-terraform-054-create-a-route-53-record-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s055","q":"In Terraform, when should an <code>aws_route53_record</code> use an <code>alias</code> block instead of <code>records</code> and <code>ttl</code>?","a":"<p>Use an alias when pointing to an AWS resource such as a load balancer, CloudFront distribution or S3 website. Alias records have no TTL, are free for queries to AWS targets, follow the target's IP changes and can sit at the zone apex, where a CNAME is not allowed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0364","card":"q-terraform-054-create-a-route-53-record-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s055","q":"What is the practical effect of a low versus a high TTL on a Route 53 record?","a":"<p>A low TTL makes changes spread quickly (useful before a migration) but increases query volume. A high TTL reduces queries and latency but delays changes. Lower the TTL before a planned cutover, and raise it again afterward.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0365","card":"q-terraform-054-create-a-route-53-record-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s055","q":"In Terraform, how do you reference a hosted zone instead of pasting its ID?","a":"<p>Use <code>aws_route53_zone.main.zone_id</code> when Terraform creates the zone, or <code>data \"aws_route53_zone\"</code> with a <code>name</code> to read an existing one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0366","card":"q-terraform-055-create-an-iam-role-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s056","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>assume_role_policy</code> and a policy attached with <code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code>?","a":"<p><code>assume_role_policy</code> is the trust policy: it says which principals (a service, an account, a user) may assume the role. The attached policy is the permission policy: it says what the role may do once assumed. Both are needed for a usable role.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_iam_role_policy_attachment&quot; &quot;my_role_policy&quot; {\n  role       = aws_iam_role.my_role.name\n  policy_arn = &quot;arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0367","card":"q-terraform-055-create-an-iam-role-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s056","q":"In Terraform, why is <code>jsonencode</code> used for IAM policy documents?","a":"<p>IAM policies are JSON. <code>jsonencode</code> lets you write them as HCL maps and lists, with interpolation and without escaping or heredoc whitespace errors. The alternative is the <code>aws_iam_policy_document</code> data source, which adds merging and validation of policy structure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0368","card":"q-terraform-055-create-an-iam-role-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s056","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code> and <code>aws_iam_policy_attachment</code>?","a":"<p><code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code> attaches one policy to one role and does not affect others. <code>aws_iam_policy_attachment</code> is exclusive for that policy: it takes control of all users, roles and groups attached to it and removes others. Prefer the role-specific resource.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0369","card":"q-terraform-055-create-an-iam-role-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s056","q":"In Terraform, how do you give a role a custom permission policy and attach it?","a":"<p>Create an <code>aws_iam_policy</code> with a JSON policy document, then use <code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code> with the policy's <code>arn</code>. For a policy used by only one role, an inline <code>aws_iam_role_policy</code> is an option, though managed policies are easier to reuse and audit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0370","card":"q-terraform-055-create-an-iam-role-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s056","q":"Why should a Terraform IAM role follow least privilege rather than attaching broad managed policies?","a":"<p>A compromised workload can only do what its role allows. Narrow actions and resource ARNs limit the damage. AWS managed read-only policies are convenient but often broader than needed, so write custom policies for production roles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0371","card":"q-terraform-055-create-an-iam-role-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s056","q":"In Terraform, what happens to <code>aws_iam_role</code> when its <code>name</code> changes?","a":"<p>The name is immutable, so Terraform replaces the role. Anything using the role (instance profiles, Lambda functions) is updated or recreated, which can interrupt services briefly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0372","card":"q-terraform-056-create-a-lambda-function-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s057","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>handler</code>, <code>runtime</code> and <code>role</code> define on an <code>aws_lambda_function</code>?","a":"<p><code>runtime</code> is the language environment. <code>handler</code> is the entry point in the code, in the form <code>file.function</code> (here the <code>handler</code> function in <code>index</code>). <code>role</code> is the execution role the function runs as, which must trust <code>lambda.amazonaws.com</code> and allow logging.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lambda_function&quot; &quot;my_lambda&quot; {\n  function_name = &quot;my-lambda-function&quot;\n  handler       = &quot;index.handler&quot;\n  runtime       = &quot;nodejs18.x&quot;\n  role          = &quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/lambda-exec-role&quot;\n  ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0373","card":"q-terraform-056-create-a-lambda-function-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s057","q":"In Terraform, how can the code of an <code>aws_lambda_function</code> be supplied, and when do you use each way?","a":"<p>With <code>filename</code> for a local zip file, with <code>s3_bucket</code> and <code>s3_key</code> for a zip in S3 (needed for large packages), or with <code>image_uri</code> and <code>package_type = \"Image\"</code> for a container image. Local zip is simple for small functions. S3 or images suit large packages and CI pipelines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0374","card":"q-terraform-056-create-a-lambda-function-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s057","q":"A Terraform-managed Lambda function does not update when the code zip changes. What is missing?","a":"<p>Terraform only compares arguments. If the file name is the same, nothing changes. Set <code>source_code_hash = filebase64sha256(\"lambda-code.zip\")</code> (or use the <code>archive_file</code> data source's hash) so a content change shows up in the plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0375","card":"q-terraform-056-create-a-lambda-function-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s057","q":"In Terraform, why does a Lambda execution role need the basic execution policy?","a":"<p>The function needs permission to write logs to CloudWatch Logs. Attach <code>AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole</code> or an equivalent policy to the role. Without it, the function runs but produces no logs, which makes debugging hard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0376","card":"q-terraform-056-create-a-lambda-function-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s057","q":"Why is a hardcoded Lambda <code>runtime</code> such as <code>nodejs18.x</code> a maintenance risk?","a":"<p>AWS deprecates runtimes when the language version reaches end of support. Deprecated runtimes can no longer be created and later updated, so functions must be upgraded. Check the supported runtimes list and use a variable for the runtime so upgrades are one change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0377","card":"q-terraform-056-create-a-lambda-function-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s057","q":"In Terraform, how do you give a Lambda function environment configuration, and what is the caution?","a":"<p>Use an <code>environment { variables = { ... } }</code> block. Values are stored in state and visible in the console in plain text, so keep secrets in Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store and read them at run time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0378","card":"q-terraform-056-create-a-lambda-function-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s057","q":"In Terraform, why create the CloudWatch log group for a Lambda function explicitly?","a":"<p>Lambda creates <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;function name&gt;</code> on first run with no retention, so logs are kept forever. Creating an <code>aws_cloudwatch_log_group</code> with <code>retention_in_days</code> and the same name lets you control retention and remove it on destroy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0379","card":"q-terraform-057-create-an-sns-topic-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s058","q":"In Terraform, what does an <code>aws_sns_topic</code> represent, and what other resources do you usually add?","a":"<p>It is a publish-subscribe channel. Publishers send messages to the topic and SNS delivers copies to every subscription. Add <code>aws_sns_topic_subscription</code> resources for subscribers and, when other services publish, an <code>aws_sns_topic_policy</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_sns_topic&quot; &quot;my_topic&quot; {\n  name = &quot;my-sns-topic&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0380","card":"q-terraform-057-create-an-sns-topic-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s058","q":"In Terraform, when should an <code>aws_sns_topic</code> be a FIFO topic?","a":"<p>When order and de-duplication matter. Set <code>fifo_topic = true</code> and end the name with <code>.fifo</code>. FIFO topics work with FIFO SQS queues and have lower throughput than standard topics, which give at-least-once delivery with best-effort ordering.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0381","card":"q-terraform-057-create-an-sns-topic-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s058","q":"What is the difference between SNS and SQS, and how are they often combined?","a":"<p>SNS pushes one message to many subscribers (fan-out). SQS is a queue where consumers pull messages. Subscribing SQS queues to an SNS topic gives fan-out with durable buffering, so each consumer processes at its own pace and failed messages can be retried.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0382","card":"q-terraform-057-create-an-sns-topic-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s058","q":"In Terraform, how do you let CloudWatch alarms or another AWS service publish to an <code>aws_sns_topic</code>?","a":"<p>Add an <code>aws_sns_topic_policy</code> that allows the service principal (for example <code>cloudwatch.amazonaws.com</code>) to <code>sns:Publish</code> on the topic ARN, with a condition on the source account or ARN. Without a policy statement, publishing is denied.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0383","card":"q-terraform-058-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"terraform-s059","q":"In Terraform, why does connecting an SNS topic to a Lambda function need both <code>aws_sns_topic_subscription</code> and <code>aws_lambda_permission</code>?","a":"<p>The subscription tells SNS where to deliver. The permission is a resource-based policy on the function that lets SNS invoke it. Without the permission, the subscription exists but invocations are denied.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_lambda_permission&quot; &quot;sns_invoke&quot; {\n    statement_id  = &quot;AllowExecutionFromSNS&quot;\n    action        = &quot;lambda:InvokeFunction&quot;\n    function_name = aws_lambda_function.my_lambda.function_name\n    principal     = &quot;sns.amazonaws.com&quot;\n    source_arn    = aws_sns_topic.my_sns_topic.arn\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0384","card":"q-terraform-058-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"terraform-s059","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>source_arn</code> do in an <code>aws_lambda_permission</code>, and what changes if you remove it?","a":"<p>It restricts the permission to one topic. Without it, any SNS topic (in any account) could invoke the function, a confused deputy risk. Always set it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0385","card":"q-terraform-058-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"terraform-s059","q":"In Terraform, what are the <code>protocol</code> and <code>endpoint</code> values of an <code>aws_sns_topic_subscription</code> for a Lambda function?","a":"<p><code>protocol</code> is <code>\"lambda\"</code> and <code>endpoint</code> is the function's ARN. For other targets the protocol changes (<code>sqs</code>, <code>https</code>, <code>email</code>) and the endpoint is the matching queue ARN, URL or address.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_sns_topic_subscription&quot; &quot;lambda_sns&quot; {\n    topic_arn = aws_sns_topic.my_sns_topic.arn\n    protocol  = &quot;lambda&quot;\n    endpoint  = aws_lambda_function.my_lambda.arn\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0386","card":"q-terraform-058-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"terraform-s059","q":"How does SNS invoke a subscribed Lambda function, and what happens if the function fails?","a":"<p>SNS invokes the function asynchronously and retries failed invocations a few times. Messages that still fail are lost unless a dead-letter queue is configured on the subscription (redrive policy) or on the Lambda function. Configure one for important workloads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0387","card":"q-terraform-058-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"terraform-s059","q":"In a Terraform setup with SNS and Lambda, why does the function code need to be idempotent?","a":"<p>SNS standard topics deliver at least once, so the same message can arrive twice. Handlers should produce the same result when repeated, for example by tracking message IDs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0388","card":"q-terraform-059-create-an-ecs-cluster-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s060","q":"In Terraform, what does an <code>aws_ecs_cluster</code> contain by itself, and what do you add to run containers?","a":"<p>It is only a logical grouping with no compute. To run containers you add a task definition and a service, and either Fargate (serverless) or EC2 capacity registered to the cluster through a capacity provider.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_ecs_cluster&quot; &quot;my_ecs_cluster&quot; {\n    name = &quot;my-ecs-cluster&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0389","card":"q-terraform-059-create-an-ecs-cluster-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s060","q":"In Terraform, how do you enable Container Insights on an <code>aws_ecs_cluster</code>?","a":"<p>Add a <code>setting</code> block with <code>name = \"containerInsights\"</code> and <code>value = \"enabled\"</code>. It sends CPU, memory and network metrics to CloudWatch for tasks and services, at an additional cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0390","card":"q-terraform-059-create-an-ecs-cluster-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s060","q":"What is the difference between ECS with Fargate and ECS on EC2?","a":"<p>Fargate runs tasks without servers to manage and bills per task CPU and memory, so there is less operational work. ECS on EC2 gives control over instance types, GPUs, caching and cost at high utilization, but you manage patching and scaling the instances.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0391","card":"q-terraform-059-create-an-ecs-cluster-using-terraform","sec":"terraform-s060","q":"In Terraform, what is an <code>aws_ecs_cluster_capacity_providers</code> resource used for?","a":"<p>It associates capacity providers (<code>FARGATE</code>, <code>FARGATE_SPOT</code> or an Auto Scaling group provider) with the cluster and sets the default strategy for tasks, for example mostly Fargate with a share on Spot for lower cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0392","card":"q-terraform-060-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"terraform-s061","q":"In Terraform, why does an ECS task need an <code>aws_ecs_service</code> in addition to an <code>aws_ecs_task_definition</code>?","a":"<p>The task definition is a blueprint. The service runs and maintains <code>desired_count</code> copies of it in a cluster, replaces failed tasks and registers them with a load balancer. Attaching the blueprint to a cluster in practice means creating a service (or running a one-off task).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_ecs_service&quot; &quot;my_service&quot; {\n    name            = &quot;my-ecs-service&quot;\n    cluster         = aws_ecs_cluster.my_ecs_cluster.id\n    task_definition = aws_ecs_task_definition.my_task.arn\n    launch_type     = &quot;FARGATE&quot;\n    desired_count   = 1\n    ...\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0393","card":"q-terraform-060-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"terraform-s061","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>execution_role_arn</code> and <code>task_role_arn</code> in an ECS task definition?","a":"<p>The execution role is used by ECS itself to pull the image from the registry, fetch secrets and write logs. The task role is used by the application code inside the container to call AWS APIs, so it holds the application's permissions. Keep them separate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0394","card":"q-terraform-060-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"terraform-s061","q":"In Terraform, why is <code>container_definitions</code> written with <code>jsonencode</code>?","a":"<p>The ECS API expects the container list as a JSON string. <code>jsonencode</code> lets you write it as HCL with references and avoids quoting errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0395","card":"q-terraform-060-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"terraform-s061","q":"In an ECS Fargate task definition, what do <code>network_mode = \"awsvpc\"</code>, <code>cpu</code> and <code>memory</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>awsvpc</code> gives each task its own network interface and IP, and is required for Fargate. <code>cpu</code> and <code>memory</code> set the task size (for instance 256 CPU units, which is 0.25 vCPU, with 512 MiB), and Fargate only accepts certain combinations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0396","card":"q-terraform-060-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"terraform-s061","q":"In Terraform, what happens to the running service when you change the container image in an <code>aws_ecs_task_definition</code>?","a":"<p>A task definition is immutable, so a change registers a new revision. Terraform updates the service to use it and ECS starts a rolling deployment. Old revisions stay as inactive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0397","card":"q-terraform-060-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"terraform-s061","q":"In Terraform, why does <code>assign_public_ip = true</code> appear in an ECS Fargate service in a public subnet?","a":"<p>A Fargate task needs a route to pull its image and send logs. In a public subnet it needs a public IP for that. In a private subnet use a NAT gateway or VPC endpoints and keep <code>assign_public_ip = false</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0398","card":"q-terraform-060-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"terraform-s061","q":"In an ECS task definition, what is the difference between <code>containerPort</code> and <code>hostPort</code>?","a":"<p><code>containerPort</code> is the port the application listens on in the container. <code>hostPort</code> is the port exposed on the host. With <code>awsvpc</code> mode (Fargate) they must be the same value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0399","card":"q-terraform-061-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s062","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>period</code>, <code>evaluation_periods</code> and <code>threshold</code> mean on an <code>aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm</code>?","a":"<p><code>period</code> is the length of each measurement window in seconds. <code>evaluation_periods</code> is how many windows are checked. The alarm fires when the statistic breaches the threshold in those periods, here when average CPU is over 80% for two 5-minute windows (10 minutes).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>period              = 300\nevaluation_periods  = 2\nstatistic           = &quot;Average&quot;\nthreshold           = 80\ncomparison_operator = &quot;GreaterThanThreshold&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0400","card":"q-terraform-061-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s062","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>dimensions</code> do in a CloudWatch alarm, and what changes if you remove it?","a":"<p>Dimensions pick the exact metric stream, here one instance. Without it, the alarm targets the aggregate metric across all instances (or a metric stream with no data), so it watches the wrong scope or sits in <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code> instead of tracking that one instance.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dimensions = {\n    InstanceId = &quot;i-1234567890abcdef0&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0401","card":"q-terraform-061-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s062","q":"In Terraform, what is <code>alarm_actions</code> and how do you notify a team?","a":"<p>It lists the ARNs that run when the alarm goes into <code>ALARM</code> state. Point it at an SNS topic that has subscribers, or at an Auto Scaling policy or EC2 action. Add <code>ok_actions</code> for the recovery notice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0402","card":"q-terraform-061-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s062","q":"An alarm built with Terraform stays in <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check the metric name, namespace, statistic and dimensions against the real metric, that the instance is running and reporting, and that the period fits the metric resolution. Also check <code>treat_missing_data</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0403","card":"q-terraform-061-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s062","q":"What does <code>treat_missing_data</code> do on a CloudWatch alarm?","a":"<p>It decides how gaps count: <code>missing</code> (default, state may become <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>), <code>notBreaching</code>, <code>breaching</code> or <code>ignore</code>. Choose <code>breaching</code> for a heartbeat metric where silence means failure and <code>notBreaching</code> for metrics that are legitimately absent when idle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0404","card":"q-terraform-061-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s062","q":"What is the difference between basic and detailed monitoring for EC2 metrics in CloudWatch?","a":"<p>Basic monitoring publishes at 5-minute granularity at no extra charge. Detailed monitoring (<code>monitoring = true</code> on <code>aws_instance</code>) publishes every minute for a fee. A <code>period</code> of 60 seconds is only useful with detailed monitoring.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0405","card":"q-terraform-061-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s062","q":"Why is a CPU alarm with <code>evaluation_periods = 2</code> often better than one with <code>1</code>?","a":"<p>A single spike would otherwise trigger it. Requiring two consecutive breaches filters short bursts and reduces false alerts, at the cost of a slower reaction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0406","card":"q-terraform-062-nginx-on-ec2-with-ubuntu-20-04-lts","sec":"terraform-s063","q":"In Terraform, when does <code>user_data</code> on an <code>aws_instance</code> run, and what happens if you change it later?","a":"<p>It runs once, at first boot, as root through cloud-init. Changing the text later does not rerun it on the existing machine. In the AWS provider, a changed <code>user_data</code> updates the stored value (the instance is stopped and started), and the script still does not run again unless it is written for every boot. Set <code>user_data_replace_on_change = true</code> to make Terraform replace the instance, so the new script runs on a fresh one.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>user_data     = &lt;&lt;-EOF\n                #!/bin/bash\n                apt-get update -y\n                apt-get install -y nginx\n                systemctl start nginx\n                systemctl enable nginx\n                EOF</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0407","card":"q-terraform-062-nginx-on-ec2-with-ubuntu-20-04-lts","sec":"terraform-s063","q":"In Terraform, what does the <code>&lt;&lt;-EOF</code> heredoc syntax do?","a":"<p>It writes a multi-line string. The <code>-</code> strips leading indentation, so the script can be indented in the file while the first line still starts with <code>#!/bin/bash</code>, which the shell needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0408","card":"q-terraform-062-nginx-on-ec2-with-ubuntu-20-04-lts","sec":"terraform-s063","q":"Why do <code>systemctl start nginx</code> and <code>systemctl enable nginx</code> appear together in a boot script?","a":"<p><code>start</code> runs the service now. <code>enable</code> makes it start again after a reboot. Package installs on Debian and Ubuntu often start and enable it automatically, but being explicit is safe.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0409","card":"q-terraform-062-nginx-on-ec2-with-ubuntu-20-04-lts","sec":"terraform-s063","q":"A Terraform-created EC2 instance has Nginx in user data but the page does not load. How do you troubleshoot?","a":"<p>Check, in order: the security group allows port 80, the subnet route table has a route to an internet gateway, the instance has a public IP, then read <code>/var/log/cloud-init-output.log</code> on the host for script errors. Also check the service with <code>systemctl status nginx</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0410","card":"q-terraform-062-nginx-on-ec2-with-ubuntu-20-04-lts","sec":"terraform-s063","q":"Why is <code>apt-get update -y</code> run before <code>apt-get install -y nginx</code> in a boot script?","a":"<p>A fresh image may have stale package lists, so the install could fail or get an old version. <code>-y</code> answers prompts automatically, which a non-interactive script needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0411","card":"q-terraform-062-nginx-on-ec2-with-ubuntu-20-04-lts","sec":"terraform-s063","q":"In Terraform, why is <code>associate_public_ip_address = true</code> used on an instance in a public subnet?","a":"<p>The instance needs a public address to be reached from the internet and to download packages directly. Setting it per instance overrides the subnet default. For a stable address use an Elastic IP.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0412","card":"q-terraform-062-nginx-on-ec2-with-ubuntu-20-04-lts","sec":"terraform-s063","q":"How would you replace a user-data based Nginx install with something more maintainable?","a":"<p>Bake the software into a custom AMI (Packer or EC2 Image Builder), or run a configuration tool such as Ansible, or use containers. User data is fine for small bootstraps but is hard to test, runs once and gives no state tracking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0413","card":"q-terraform-063-nginx-on-ec2-with-centos-7","sec":"terraform-s064","q":"In Terraform, why does a boot script differ between Ubuntu and CentOS, and what changes?","a":"<p>The two use different package managers and repositories: <code>apt-get</code> on Debian-family systems and <code>yum</code> (or <code>dnf</code>) on Red Hat-family systems. On CentOS 7 the Nginx package comes from the EPEL repository, so <code>epel-release</code> must be installed first.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>user_data     = &lt;&lt;-EOF\n                #!/bin/bash\n                yum update -y\n                yum install -y epel-release\n                yum install -y nginx\n                ...\n                EOF</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0414","card":"q-terraform-063-nginx-on-ec2-with-centos-7","sec":"terraform-s064","q":"What is the status of CentOS 7, and what do you use instead for new Terraform-built servers?","a":"<p>CentOS 7 reached end of life on 30 June 2024 and receives no security updates. Use Amazon Linux 2023, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux or a supported Ubuntu release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0415","card":"q-terraform-063-nginx-on-ec2-with-centos-7","sec":"terraform-s064","q":"In Terraform, why should the same bootstrap logic be parameterized rather than copied for each operating system?","a":"<p>Duplicates drift apart. Keep the script in a <code>templatefile()</code> with variables, or choose the AMI and package manager with a variable and a map, so one resource serves several operating systems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0416","card":"q-terraform-063-nginx-on-ec2-with-centos-7","sec":"terraform-s064","q":"In Terraform, what does the <code>templatefile</code> function do and why is it good for user data?","a":"<p>It renders a separate file with variables into a string, for example <code>user_data = templatefile(\"init.sh.tftpl\", { port = 80 })</code>. It keeps long scripts out of the <code>.tf</code> file, supports loops and conditions, and the script can be edited and linted separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0417","card":"q-terraform-063-nginx-on-ec2-with-centos-7","sec":"terraform-s064","q":"In Terraform, why is <code>data \"aws_ami\"</code> a better way to choose the image for several operating systems than fixed IDs?","a":"<p>Filters (owner and name pattern) resolve the right AMI in every region, so the same code works across regions. Fixed IDs break when the region changes or the image is retired.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0418","card":"q-terraform-064-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"In AWS, how does an EC2 instance reach an RDS instance in the same VPC, and what controls the access?","a":"<p>Over the VPC's private network. The route table already allows local traffic, so access depends on the RDS security group allowing the database port from the EC2 side, and on the database credentials. No internet gateway is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0419","card":"q-terraform-064-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"In Terraform, how do you write the RDS security group rule so only the application instance can connect?","a":"<p>This rule allows MySQL traffic from members of the EC2 security group only. It does not open the port to any IP range, and it keeps working when instance addresses change. The default MySQL port is 3306 (PostgreSQL uses 5432).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ingress {\n    from_port       = 3306\n    to_port         = 3306\n    protocol        = &quot;tcp&quot;\n    security_groups = [aws_security_group.ec2_sg.id]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0420","card":"q-terraform-064-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"In AWS, security groups are stateful. For an application instance that connects to a database, which direction needs an explicit rule and which does not?","a":"<p>Replies to allowed traffic are returned automatically, so no rule is needed for the response direction. A new connection that the instance starts toward the database needs an outbound rule on the instance's group allowing the database port, and the database group needs a matching inbound rule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0421","card":"q-terraform-064-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"In Terraform, why does an <code>aws_db_subnet_group</code> need to exist before an RDS instance in a custom VPC?","a":"<p>A database in a non-default VPC has to know which subnets it may use. The <code>aws_db_subnet_group</code> lists them, and the instance references it by <code>db_subnet_group_name</code>. RDS requires subnets in at least two Availability Zones in that group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0422","card":"q-terraform-064-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"Should an RDS instance used by an EC2 application sit in a public or private subnet, and why?","a":"<p>A private subnet with <code>publicly_accessible = false</code>. The database then has no route from the internet, and only the application tier in the VPC can reach it. Administrators connect through a bastion, VPN or Session Manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0423","card":"q-terraform-065-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"In Terraform, how do you give an application on EC2 the connection address of an <code>aws_db_instance</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>aws_db_instance.my_rds.address</code> for the hostname, <code>.port</code> for the port, or <code>.endpoint</code> which combines host and port (<code>host:port</code>). Pass them to the instance through <code>user_data</code> or <code>templatefile</code>, to Parameter Store, or output them. Never hardcode an endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0424","card":"q-terraform-065-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"In Terraform, why can an EC2 instance start before the database is ready, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>If the EC2 resource uses no reference to the database, Terraform creates both in parallel and the database can take many minutes. If the instance's boot script needs the database, reference its address (creating a dependency), and make the application retry its connection on start.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0425","card":"q-terraform-065-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"How do you verify from the EC2 instance that it can reach RDS?","a":"<p>Test connectivity with the client, for example <code>mysql -h &lt;endpoint&gt; -u admin -p</code>, or check the port with <code>nc -zv &lt;endpoint&gt; 3306</code>. A timeout points to security group, route or subnet issues. An authentication error means the network path works and the credentials are wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0426","card":"q-terraform-065-connecting-an-rds-instance-to-an-ec2-ins","sec":"terraform-s065","q":"Why does a timeout (and not \"access denied\") when connecting to RDS usually mean a network problem?","a":"<p>\"Access denied\" comes from the database itself, so packets reached it. A timeout means packets were dropped before that, usually by a security group, network ACL, wrong subnet or a database not in the same network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0427","card":"q-terraform-066-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In AWS Auto Scaling, how does a CloudWatch alarm cause an Auto Scaling group to change size?","a":"<p>The alarm watches a metric. When it goes into <code>ALARM</code> state, it calls the scaling policy's ARN. The policy then adjusts the group's desired capacity, within <code>min_size</code> and <code>max_size</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>alarm_actions = [aws_autoscaling_policy.step_scaling.arn]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0428","card":"q-terraform-066-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In AWS Auto Scaling, what is the difference between simple, step and target tracking scaling policies?","a":"<p>Simple scaling applies one adjustment and waits for a cooldown. Step scaling applies different adjustments depending on how far the metric is past the threshold, with no wait between steps. Target tracking keeps a metric near a target (for example 50% CPU) and creates the alarms itself. Target tracking is the simplest and the AWS recommendation for most cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0429","card":"q-terraform-066-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In Terraform, why is target tracking often preferred over a hand-built alarm plus step policy?","a":"<p>It needs one resource and manages both scale-out and scale-in, while a step policy attached to an alarm only does what the alarm says. With a manual setup you must write alarms for both directions yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0430","card":"q-terraform-066-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In Terraform, what is wrong with a stack that has only a scale-out policy and alarms for high usage?","a":"<p>The group grows when load is high but never shrinks, so cost stays at the maximum. Add a scale-in policy with a negative <code>scaling_adjustment</code> and a low-usage alarm, or use target tracking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0431","card":"q-terraform-066-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In a Terraform step scaling policy, what do <code>scaling_adjustment</code> and <code>metric_interval_lower_bound</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>scaling_adjustment</code> is the change to apply (here add one instance, using <code>ChangeInCapacity</code>). The interval bounds are measured relative to the alarm threshold: a lower bound of 0 means \"from the threshold upward\". Several step blocks with different bounds scale harder as the breach grows.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>step_adjustment {\n    scaling_adjustment          = 1\n    metric_interval_lower_bound = 0\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0432","card":"q-terraform-066-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In Terraform, what is the effect of <code>adjustment_type = \"ChangeInCapacity\"</code> compared with <code>\"PercentChangeInCapacity\"</code>?","a":"<p><code>ChangeInCapacity</code> adds or removes a fixed number of instances. <code>PercentChangeInCapacity</code> changes the group by a percentage of the current size, which scales better for large fleets. <code>ExactCapacity</code> sets the group to a specific number.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0433","card":"q-terraform-066-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In Terraform, why must the <code>dimensions</code> of an alarm for a group metric use <code>AutoScalingGroupName</code>?","a":"<p>It selects the metric aggregated for all instances in the group, so the alarm reacts to the fleet and not one machine.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dimensions = {\n    AutoScalingGroupName = aws_autoscaling_group.web_asg.name\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0434","card":"q-terraform-067-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"Why does a memory alarm on an EC2 Auto Scaling group need the CloudWatch agent, when CPU needs nothing extra?","a":"<p>EC2 publishes CPU, network and disk I/O from the hypervisor, but it cannot see inside the guest, so memory and disk space need the CloudWatch agent running on the instance. The agent publishes custom metrics such as <code>mem_used_percent</code> in a namespace you choose (default <code>CWAgent</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0435","card":"q-terraform-067-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In Terraform, what must the instance role have for the CloudWatch agent to publish metrics?","a":"<p>Permission to put metrics, usually the managed policy <code>CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy</code>, attached to a role passed through an instance profile in the launch template (<code>iam_instance_profile</code>). Without it the agent runs but metrics never arrive and the alarm shows no data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0436","card":"q-terraform-067-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In Terraform, why does <code>user_data</code> in an <code>aws_launch_template</code> need <code>base64encode</code>?","a":"<p>The launch template API expects user data already base64-encoded, unlike <code>aws_instance</code>, which encodes it for you. Forgetting the encoding makes the script not run.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>user_data = base64encode(&lt;&lt;-EOF\n    ...\n    EOF\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0437","card":"q-terraform-067-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In Terraform, why does a launch template for Auto Scaling set the security group under <code>network_interfaces</code> with <code>associate_public_ip_address</code>?","a":"<p>Setting network interface options in the template lets you control the public IP and groups per launch. If you specify <code>network_interfaces</code>, do not also set <code>vpc_security_group_ids</code> at template level, as they conflict.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0438","card":"q-terraform-067-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In AWS Auto Scaling driven by CloudWatch alarms, how long can it take from a load spike to a new instance serving traffic, and what adds to it?","a":"<p>It is the alarm evaluation time (for example two 5-minute periods, so up to 10 minutes) plus instance launch and boot time and health check grace. Shorter periods, detailed monitoring or target tracking make it faster, but too short causes flapping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0439","card":"q-terraform-067-auto-scaling-with-cloudwatch-alarms","sec":"terraform-s066","q":"In Terraform, why can two alarms (CPU and memory) share one scaling policy?","a":"<p>Either alarm calls the policy when it fires, so any breach scales the group. The policy does not know which alarm called it. Scale-in is harder: it should happen only when all metrics are low.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0440","card":"q-terraform-068-applying-the-auto-scaling-stack","sec":"terraform-s067","q":"Why should you run <code>terraform plan</code> before <code>apply</code> for an Auto Scaling stack, and what do you look for?","a":"<p>The plan lists every resource to create, change or replace. Check the AMI, the group sizes, the policies, and that nothing unexpected is replaced or destroyed. In a team, save it with <code>-out</code> and apply that exact plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0441","card":"q-terraform-068-applying-the-auto-scaling-stack","sec":"terraform-s067","q":"How do you test that a Terraform-built Auto Scaling group scales out?","a":"<p>Generate load on the instances (for example with <code>stress-ng</code> or a load test tool), watch the alarm change to <code>ALARM</code> in CloudWatch, and check scaling activities with <code>aws autoscaling describe-scaling-activities --auto-scaling-group-name &lt;name&gt;</code> or in the console. Confirm the instance count rises to <code>max_size</code> and no further.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0442","card":"q-terraform-068-applying-the-auto-scaling-stack","sec":"terraform-s067","q":"How do you clean up an Auto Scaling stack built with Terraform, and why is it important?","a":"<p>Run <code>terraform destroy</code>. Running instances, load balancers and NAT gateways cost money every hour, and the group would keep replacing instances you terminate by hand, so deleting the group through Terraform is the clean way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0443","card":"q-terraform-068-applying-the-auto-scaling-stack","sec":"terraform-s067","q":"A placeholder AMI or key pair is wrong when applying a Terraform stack. What does the failure look like and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The API call fails during <code>apply</code> with an error such as an invalid AMI ID or key pair not found. Terraform stops, leaves already created resources in state, and a corrected <code>apply</code> continues from there. AMI IDs are region-specific, so check the region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0444","card":"q-terraform-069-s3-bucket-with-public-read-access","sec":"terraform-s068","q":"In Terraform, why does creating the public-read <code>aws_s3_bucket_policy</code> sometimes fail with an access error, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The policy can be applied before the public access block is relaxed, because Terraform sees no dependency between them. Add <code>depends_on = [aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.public_bucket]</code> to the policy so the order is fixed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0445","card":"q-terraform-069-s3-bucket-with-public-read-access","sec":"terraform-s068","q":"What does the policy statement <code>Principal = \"*\"</code>, <code>Action = \"s3:GetObject\"</code> allow, and why is <code>/*</code> in the resource?","a":"<p>It allows anyone on the internet to read objects. <code>GetObject</code> applies to objects, so the resource must be the object ARN (<code>bucket-arn/*</code>), not the bucket ARN. <code>ListBucket</code> would need the bucket ARN and is not granted, so people cannot list the contents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0446","card":"q-terraform-069-s3-bucket-with-public-read-access","sec":"terraform-s068","q":"What is a safer alternative to a public S3 bucket for serving static content?","a":"<p>Keep the bucket private and serve it through CloudFront with an origin access control. Users get caching, HTTPS and optional WAF while the bucket stays closed to direct access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0447","card":"q-terraform-069-s3-bucket-with-public-read-access","sec":"terraform-s068","q":"In Terraform, why should public access to a bucket be a deliberate, reviewed change?","a":"<p>A public bucket is a common cause of data leaks. Reviewers should check that the bucket holds only public content, and scanners such as Checkov or tfsec will flag the public access block settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0448","card":"q-terraform-070-rds-instance-with-mysql-engine","sec":"terraform-s069","q":"In Terraform, which arguments are the minimum for an <code>aws_db_instance</code> running MySQL in AWS provider v4 and later?","a":"<p><code>engine = \"mysql\"</code>, <code>instance_class</code>, <code>allocated_storage</code>, <code>username</code> and <code>password</code> (or a managed master password). Use <code>db_name</code> for an initial database (the older <code>name</code> argument was renamed). Also set <code>skip_final_snapshot</code> or a <code>final_snapshot_identifier</code>, and the security and subnet settings for production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0449","card":"q-terraform-070-rds-instance-with-mysql-engine","sec":"terraform-s069","q":"In Terraform, what do the settings <code>engine = \"mysql\"</code> and <code>engine_version = \"8.0\"</code> pin down?","a":"<p>The database engine and its major version line. <code>8.0</code> selects MySQL 8.0 and RDS picks a minor version unless you give a full number such as <code>8.0.35</code>. Engine versions reach end of standard support and need planned upgrades.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0450","card":"q-terraform-071-s3-bucket-with-glacier-lifecycle-rule","sec":"terraform-s070","q":"In Terraform, what does an <code>aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration</code> with a <code>transition</code> rule do?","a":"<p>It tells S3 to move objects to another storage class after a number of days. For example, objects move to <code>GLACIER</code> 30 days after creation. The rule runs automatically in the background. It lowers storage cost for data accessed rarely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0451","card":"q-terraform-071-s3-bucket-with-glacier-lifecycle-rule","sec":"terraform-s070","q":"In Terraform, what is wrong with writing a lifecycle rule as <code>rule = { id = \"archive\", status = \"Enabled\" }</code>?","a":"<p><code>rule</code> and <code>transition</code> are nested blocks, not arguments. Use <code>rule { id = \"archive\" status = \"Enabled\" transition { days = 30 storage_class = \"GLACIER\" } }</code>. Writing them with <code>=</code> fails with an \"Unsupported argument\" error. Also put each argument on its own line. HCL does not allow several arguments separated by commas on one line in a block.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0452","card":"q-terraform-071-s3-bucket-with-glacier-lifecycle-rule","sec":"terraform-s070","q":"What are the trade-offs of moving S3 objects to Glacier storage classes?","a":"<p>Storage is much cheaper, but retrieval takes minutes to hours and costs money, and there are minimum storage durations (90 days for Glacier Flexible Retrieval, 180 for Deep Archive) with early deletion charges. Use it for archives and compliance data, not for data read often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0453","card":"q-terraform-071-s3-bucket-with-glacier-lifecycle-rule","sec":"terraform-s070","q":"In Terraform, how do the S3 Glacier storage classes in a lifecycle <code>transition</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>storage_class = \"GLACIER\"</code> means Glacier Flexible Retrieval, while <code>DEEP_ARCHIVE</code> and <code>GLACIER_IR</code> (Instant Retrieval) are separate classes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0454","card":"q-terraform-071-s3-bucket-with-glacier-lifecycle-rule","sec":"terraform-s070","q":"In Terraform, how do you add a rule that deletes objects after a retention period?","a":"<p>Add an <code>expiration { days = 365 }</code> block to the lifecycle rule. Combine it with <code>noncurrent_version_expiration</code> on versioned buckets so old versions do not pile up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0455","card":"q-terraform-072-dynamodb-table-with-auto-scaling","sec":"terraform-s071","q":"In Terraform, which resources add auto scaling to a provisioned DynamoDB table?","a":"<p><code>aws_appautoscaling_target</code> registers the table's read or write capacity as scalable with minimum and maximum. <code>aws_appautoscaling_policy</code> with <code>TargetTrackingScaling</code> keeps utilization near a target value. Use one pair for reads and one for writes (and one pair per global secondary index).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0456","card":"q-terraform-072-dynamodb-table-with-auto-scaling","sec":"terraform-s071","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>scalable_dimension</code> and <code>service_namespace</code> identify on <code>aws_appautoscaling_target</code>?","a":"<p><code>service_namespace</code> names the AWS service (DynamoDB). <code>scalable_dimension</code> names which dimension scales, here the table's read capacity. <code>resource_id</code> points at the table. Together they identify what Application Auto Scaling should adjust.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource_id        = &quot;table/${aws_dynamodb_table.example.name}&quot;\nscalable_dimension = &quot;dynamodb:table:ReadCapacityUnits&quot;\nservice_namespace  = &quot;dynamodb&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0457","card":"q-terraform-072-dynamodb-table-with-auto-scaling","sec":"terraform-s071","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>target_value = 70.0</code> mean in a DynamoDB target tracking policy?","a":"<p>Auto scaling raises or lowers provisioned capacity so consumed capacity stays near 70% of provisioned. That leaves room for traffic bursts while scaling catches up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0458","card":"q-terraform-072-dynamodb-table-with-auto-scaling","sec":"terraform-s071","q":"Why should a Terraform-managed DynamoDB table with auto scaling ignore changes to <code>read_capacity</code> and <code>write_capacity</code>?","a":"<p>Auto scaling changes them at run time. Without <code>lifecycle { ignore_changes = [read_capacity, write_capacity] }</code> the next <code>apply</code> resets capacity to the configured numbers and fights the scaler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0459","card":"q-terraform-072-dynamodb-table-with-auto-scaling","sec":"terraform-s071","q":"When would you choose on-demand billing over provisioned capacity with auto scaling for DynamoDB?","a":"<p>Choose on-demand for unpredictable or new workloads where you want no capacity management. Choose provisioned with auto scaling for steady, predictable traffic where it costs less. Auto scaling reacts in minutes, so a sudden large spike can still be throttled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0460","card":"q-terraform-073-import-existing-s3-bucket-into-terraform","sec":"terraform-s072","q":"In Terraform, what do the two arguments of <code>terraform import aws_s3_bucket.existing my-existing-bucket</code> mean?","a":"<p>The first is the resource address in your configuration. The second is the ID the provider uses to find the real object. For an S3 bucket that ID is the bucket name. Other resource types use other IDs (an ARN, an identifier, or a composite), so check the provider docs for each type.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0461","card":"q-terraform-073-import-existing-s3-bucket-into-terraform","sec":"terraform-s072","q":"In Terraform, what happens if you run <code>terraform import</code> for a bucket before writing any <code>resource \"aws_s3_bucket\"</code> block for it?","a":"<p>The command fails because the resource address does not exist in the configuration. With the CLI command, the resource block must exist first. The import only writes to state, never to your <code>.tf</code> files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0462","card":"q-terraform-073-import-existing-s3-bucket-into-terraform","sec":"terraform-s072","q":"In Terraform, you imported a bucket and later run <code>terraform destroy</code>. What happens to the real bucket?","a":"<p>Terraform deletes it, because it is now a managed resource like any other. A bucket with objects fails to delete unless <code>force_destroy = true</code> is set. Use <code>lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }</code>, or <code>terraform state rm</code> to release it, if Terraform should not own it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0463","card":"q-terraform-073-import-existing-s3-bucket-into-terraform","sec":"terraform-s072","q":"In Terraform, when should you use a data source instead of importing an existing bucket?","a":"<p>Use a data source when you only need to read or reference the bucket (its ARN or name) and another team owns it. Import it when this configuration should manage the bucket's settings and lifecycle. Importing takes ownership, so a later <code>destroy</code> can delete it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0464","card":"q-terraform-073-import-existing-s3-bucket-into-terraform","sec":"terraform-s072","q":"In Terraform, why does a team import existing resources instead of deleting and recreating them?","a":"<p>Recreating a bucket loses its objects and may break its name, policies and dependants. Import brings the live object under Terraform control with no downtime or data loss. It is the standard route when adopting Terraform for infrastructure that was built by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0465","card":"q-terraform-074-manage-existing-rds-instance-without-rec","sec":"terraform-s073","q":"In Terraform, which ID does <code>terraform import aws_db_instance.existing_rds &lt;id&gt;</code> expect for an RDS instance?","a":"<p>The DB instance identifier (the name you gave it), not the ARN or the endpoint. The <code>identifier</code> in your resource block must match it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0466","card":"q-terraform-074-manage-existing-rds-instance-without-rec","sec":"terraform-s073","q":"In Terraform, after importing an <code>aws_db_instance</code>, why can <code>password</code> and <code>skip_final_snapshot</code> not be read back from the real database?","a":"<p>The AWS API never returns the master password, and <code>skip_final_snapshot</code> and <code>apply_immediately</code> are Terraform-only settings with no AWS-side value. The first plan may show an in-place update that sets them in state. For the Terraform-only ones this changes nothing on the real instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0467","card":"q-terraform-074-manage-existing-rds-instance-without-rec","sec":"terraform-s073","q":"In Terraform, which differences between your <code>aws_db_instance</code> block and the real database can force replacement, and how do you guard against that?","a":"<p>Arguments such as <code>identifier</code> or <code>engine</code> can force a new resource if they differ. Run <code>terraform plan</code> after import and look for <code>-/+</code> (replace) before applying. Protect production databases with <code>lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0468","card":"q-terraform-074-manage-existing-rds-instance-without-rec","sec":"terraform-s073","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>deletion_protection = true</code> on an <code>aws_db_instance</code> do, and does it stop <code>terraform destroy</code>?","a":"<p>It makes AWS refuse to delete the instance. <code>terraform destroy</code> fails until you set it to <code>false</code> and apply that change first. It protects against accidental deletes by people or by a bad plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0469","card":"q-terraform-074-manage-existing-rds-instance-without-rec","sec":"terraform-s073","q":"In Terraform, an existing <code>aws_db_instance</code> is managed elsewhere for its password. How do you stop Terraform from planning a change to it?","a":"<p>Add <code>lifecycle { ignore_changes = [password] }</code> to the resource. Terraform then ignores differences in that argument, so the real value can be rotated or set outside the configuration. Keep the secret itself out of code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0470","card":"q-terraform-075-s3-bucket-with-server-side-encryption","sec":"terraform-s074","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between SSE-S3 and SSE-KMS, and which <code>sse_algorithm</code> values select them in Terraform?","a":"<p>SSE-S3 uses keys that S3 manages, set with <code>AES256</code>. SSE-KMS uses a KMS key you control, set with <code>aws:kms</code> plus <code>kms_master_key_id</code>. KMS gives key policies, rotation control and CloudTrail audit of each use, at the cost of KMS request charges and quotas.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0471","card":"q-terraform-075-s3-bucket-with-server-side-encryption","sec":"terraform-s074","q":"Since January 2023 S3 encrypts every new object with SSE-S3 by default. Why still configure encryption in Terraform?","a":"<p>Explicit configuration lets you choose SSE-KMS with your own key, meet compliance rules that require customer-managed keys, and keep the setting visible and enforced in code. The default only guarantees SSE-S3, not a key you control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0472","card":"q-terraform-075-s3-bucket-with-server-side-encryption","sec":"terraform-s074","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>bucket_key_enabled = true</code> do on an SSE-KMS bucket encryption rule?","a":"<p>It makes S3 use a bucket-level key derived from the KMS key, so S3 makes far fewer KMS calls. This cuts KMS request cost and avoids KMS throttling on busy buckets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0473","card":"q-terraform-075-s3-bucket-with-server-side-encryption","sec":"terraform-s074","q":"In Terraform, where does <code>kms_master_key_id</code> go in this skeleton?","a":"<p>Inside <code>apply_server_side_encryption_by_default</code>, next to <code>sse_algorithm</code>. The <code>rule</code> and <code>apply_server_side_encryption_by_default</code> parts are nested blocks, not attributes assigned with <code>=</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration&quot; &quot;enc&quot; {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.b.id\n  rule {\n    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {\n      sse_algorithm = &quot;aws:kms&quot;\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0474","card":"q-terraform-075-s3-bucket-with-server-side-encryption","sec":"terraform-s074","q":"In AWS, what do the four settings of <code>aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block</code> do?","a":"<p><code>block_public_acls</code> rejects new public ACLs. <code>ignore_public_acls</code> ignores existing public ACLs. <code>block_public_policy</code> rejects bucket policies that grant public access. <code>restrict_public_buckets</code> limits a bucket that has a public policy to AWS services and authorized users. Together they stop a bucket becoming public by mistake, even if someone adds a bad policy later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0475","card":"q-terraform-075-s3-bucket-with-server-side-encryption","sec":"terraform-s074","q":"In AWS, does encrypting an S3 bucket at rest stop the data being read if the bucket is public?","a":"<p>No. Encryption at rest is transparent to anyone S3 authorizes, so a public object is served decrypted. Access control (public access block, bucket policy, IAM) protects against exposure. Encryption protects against lost media and, with KMS, adds a second permission check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0476","card":"q-terraform-075-s3-bucket-with-server-side-encryption","sec":"terraform-s074","q":"In AWS, how do you force all clients to use HTTPS when talking to a bucket managed by Terraform?","a":"<p>Add a bucket policy with a <code>Deny</code> statement on <code>s3:*</code> when the condition <code>aws:SecureTransport</code> is <code>false</code>. Encryption at rest does not cover data in transit, so this policy is the control for transport security.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0477","card":"q-terraform-076-enable-versioning-on-existing-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s075","q":"In AWS, what does S3 versioning do when an object is overwritten or deleted?","a":"<p>An overwrite keeps the old data as a non-current version. A delete without a version ID adds a delete marker, and the data stays recoverable. Deleting a specific version ID removes that version permanently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0478","card":"q-terraform-076-enable-versioning-on-existing-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s075","q":"In Terraform, can you turn S3 versioning fully off again after enabling it?","a":"<p>No. Once enabled, a bucket can only be <code>Suspended</code>, not returned to the never-versioned state. Existing versions stay and keep costing money. Set <code>status = \"Suspended\"</code> in <code>versioning_configuration</code> to stop creating new versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0479","card":"q-terraform-076-enable-versioning-on-existing-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s075","q":"In Terraform, what is the cost trade-off of enabling versioning, and how do you control it?","a":"<p>Every non-current version is stored and billed. Add an <code>aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration</code> rule with <code>noncurrent_version_expiration</code> (and optionally transitions to cheaper storage) to remove old versions after a set number of days.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0480","card":"q-terraform-076-enable-versioning-on-existing-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s075","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>force_destroy = true</code> do to a versioned bucket on destroy?","a":"<p>The provider deletes all objects, object versions and delete markers first, then deletes the bucket. Without it, destroying a non-empty versioned bucket fails. Use it only for buckets whose data is disposable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0481","card":"q-terraform-076-enable-versioning-on-existing-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s075","q":"In Terraform, a bucket exists but is not managed by Terraform. What do you need before <code>aws_s3_bucket_versioning</code> can be applied to it?","a":"<p>The <code>aws_s3_bucket</code> resource must be in state (import it), or you must reference the bucket by name or a data source. Otherwise Terraform tries to create a bucket with the same name and fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0482","card":"q-terraform-077-resolve-terraform-resource-already-exist","sec":"terraform-s076","q":"In Terraform, <code>apply</code> fails with <code>BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou</code> or <code>EntityAlreadyExists</code>. What does it mean and what are the fixes?","a":"<p>Terraform tried to create something that already exists in AWS but is not in its state. Fixes: import it with <code>terraform import</code> (or an <code>import</code> block), rename the new resource if it is a real duplicate, or delete the stray resource if it is not needed. Import is right when the config should manage the existing object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0483","card":"q-terraform-077-resolve-terraform-resource-already-exist","sec":"terraform-s076","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between <code>BucketAlreadyExists</code> and <code>BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou</code> for an S3 create?","a":"<p>S3 bucket names are global. <code>BucketAlreadyExists</code> means another account owns the name, so you must pick a different one. <code>BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou</code> means your own account owns it, so you can import it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0484","card":"q-terraform-077-resolve-terraform-resource-already-exist","sec":"terraform-s076","q":"In Terraform, a remote state file was lost and <code>apply</code> reports many resources as already existing. How do you recover?","a":"<p>First try restoring the previous state from the backend (a versioned S3 bucket keeps old versions). If that is impossible, rebuild state by importing each resource, then run <code>terraform plan</code> until it shows no unexpected changes. Do not run <code>apply</code> before the imports.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0485","card":"q-terraform-077-resolve-terraform-resource-already-exist","sec":"terraform-s076","q":"In Terraform, what practices stop the 'already exists' error from coming back?","a":"<p>Keep one source of truth, so nobody creates managed resources by hand. Use a remote backend with locking, so state is shared and not lost. Restrict console write access, and run <code>terraform plan</code> in CI to catch drift early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0486","card":"q-terraform-078-update-rds-instance-storage-capacity","sec":"terraform-s077","q":"In Terraform, changing <code>allocated_storage</code> on an existing <code>aws_db_instance</code> shows <code>~</code> in the plan. What does that mean?","a":"<p>It is an in-place update with no replacement. RDS grows the volume while the instance keeps running. Always check the plan symbol: <code>-/+</code> would mean destroy and recreate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0487","card":"q-terraform-078-update-rds-instance-storage-capacity","sec":"terraform-s077","q":"In AWS RDS, can you reduce <code>allocated_storage</code> by changing it in Terraform?","a":"<p>No. RDS storage can only grow, so the API call fails. To shrink, create a new smaller instance and migrate the data (for example with a dump and load).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0488","card":"q-terraform-078-update-rds-instance-storage-capacity","sec":"terraform-s077","q":"In AWS RDS, how soon can you modify storage again after a storage change?","a":"<p>Only after the storage optimization finishes and at least six hours have passed since the last modification. Back-to-back increases in Terraform applies can fail until that wait ends.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0489","card":"q-terraform-078-update-rds-instance-storage-capacity","sec":"terraform-s077","q":"In Terraform, how do you let RDS grow storage on its own without Terraform fighting it?","a":"<p>Set <code>max_allocated_storage</code> to enable storage autoscaling. Because AWS then changes <code>allocated_storage</code> outside Terraform, the value can show as drift, so many teams add <code>lifecycle { ignore_changes = [allocated_storage] }</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0490","card":"q-terraform-078-update-rds-instance-storage-capacity","sec":"terraform-s077","q":"In AWS, what should you do before changing a production database through Terraform?","a":"<p>Read the plan for replace actions, take a manual snapshot, and plan the change for a low-traffic window. Some changes (instance class, engine version) cause downtime or a reboot, while storage growth usually does not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0491","card":"q-terraform-079-check-missing-terraform-created-s3-bucke","sec":"terraform-s078","q":"A bucket that Terraform supposedly created cannot be found. In what order do you check?","a":"<p>Confirm the account, region and credentials profile in use. Check that you are on the right backend and workspace. Run <code>terraform state list</code> to see whether the bucket is in state. Then run <code>aws s3 ls s3://&lt;name&gt;</code> or <code>aws s3api head-bucket</code> to see whether it exists in AWS. The combination tells you which side is missing it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0492","card":"q-terraform-079-check-missing-terraform-created-s3-bucke","sec":"terraform-s078","q":"In Terraform, the bucket exists in AWS but is not in <code>terraform state list</code>. What do you do?","a":"<p>Import it with <code>terraform import aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket &lt;bucket-name&gt;</code> (or an <code>import</code> block) after the resource block exists. Then run a plan to reconcile differences.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0493","card":"q-terraform-079-check-missing-terraform-created-s3-bucke","sec":"terraform-s078","q":"What do <code>NoSuchBucket</code> and <code>AccessDenied</code> mean when you run <code>aws s3 ls s3://&lt;bucket&gt;</code>?","a":"<p><code>NoSuchBucket</code> means no bucket with that name exists. <code>AccessDenied</code> means the name exists but your identity lacks permission, or another account owns it. Both explain a bucket that seems missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0494","card":"q-terraform-080-update-rds-instance-confirm-with-cli","sec":"terraform-s079","q":"In AWS, an RDS change was applied by Terraform but <code>describe-db-instances</code> shows <code>modifying</code>. What does that mean?","a":"<p>The instance is still working through the change. Wait for <code>available</code> before relying on the new settings. You can block on it with <code>aws rds wait db-instance-available --db-instance-identifier &lt;id&gt;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0495","card":"q-terraform-080-update-rds-instance-confirm-with-cli","sec":"terraform-s079","q":"In AWS, Terraform finished but the CLI still shows the old storage size. What do you look at?","a":"<p>Check <code>PendingModifiedValues</code> in the <code>describe-db-instances</code> output. If <code>apply_immediately</code> was <code>false</code>, the change is queued for the maintenance window. Set it to <code>true</code> or wait for the window.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0496","card":"q-terraform-080-update-rds-instance-confirm-with-cli","sec":"terraform-s079","q":"In AWS CLI, what does the <code>--query</code> option do, as in <code>--query \"DBInstances[*].[DBInstanceStatus, AllocatedStorage]\"</code>?","a":"<p>It filters the JSON response on the client with a JMESPath expression, so you print only the fields you need. It does not reduce what the API returns, only what is displayed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0497","card":"q-terraform-080-update-rds-instance-confirm-with-cli","sec":"terraform-s079","q":"In Terraform, why verify an apply with the AWS CLI when <code>apply</code> already succeeded?","a":"<p>Terraform state records what the API call returned, and the CLI reads the live resource independently. Checking status, size and class catches pending changes or drift. A second <code>terraform plan</code> that shows no changes is the other check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0498","card":"q-terraform-081-vpc-with-two-private-subnets","sec":"terraform-s080","q":"In AWS, how many IP addresses in each subnet can you not use, and what does that mean for sizing?","a":"<p>AWS reserves five addresses per subnet (network, VPC router, DNS, future use, broadcast). A <code>/28</code> therefore has 11 usable addresses. Size subnets with room for growth and for services that take many IPs, such as EKS pods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0499","card":"q-terraform-081-vpc-with-two-private-subnets","sec":"terraform-s080","q":"In Terraform, how can you avoid hardcoding <code>us-east-1a</code> in the <code>availability_zone</code> of subnets?","a":"<p>Read the zones with <code>data \"aws_availability_zones\" \"available\"</code> and use <code>data.aws_availability_zones.available.names[0]</code>, or loop over them. That keeps the configuration valid in any region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0500","card":"q-terraform-082-module-for-alb-with-target-group","sec":"terraform-s081","q":"In Terraform, why wrap an ALB and its target group in a module?","a":"<p>A module packages related resources behind input variables and outputs, so teams reuse one tested definition for many environments instead of copying code. Changes to the ALB design then happen in one place.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0501","card":"q-terraform-082-module-for-alb-with-target-group","sec":"terraform-s081","q":"In Terraform, which outputs should an ALB module expose and why?","a":"<p>Typically the ALB <code>arn</code>, <code>dns_name</code>, <code>zone_id</code>, and the target group <code>arn</code>. Callers need the DNS name and zone ID for Route 53 and the target group ARN to attach instances or an Auto Scaling group. A module can only be used through its outputs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0502","card":"q-terraform-082-module-for-alb-with-target-group","sec":"terraform-s081","q":"In Terraform, how does a child module receive values, and where do the <code>variable</code> blocks live?","a":"<p>The caller passes values as arguments in the <code>module</code> block. The matching <code>variable</code> blocks are declared inside the child module (commonly <code>variables.tf</code>). A variable without a default is required, so Terraform errors if the caller omits it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0503","card":"q-terraform-082-module-for-alb-with-target-group","sec":"terraform-s081","q":"In Terraform, when does the <code>version</code> argument work in a <code>module</code> block?","a":"<p>Only for modules from a registry. A local path source like <code>./modules/alb</code> has no version, because it is whatever is on disk. Git module addresses are pinned with <code>?ref=</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0504","card":"q-terraform-082-module-for-alb-with-target-group","sec":"terraform-s081","q":"In Terraform, should an ALB module create the VPC it lives in?","a":"<p>No. Accept <code>vpc_id</code> and <code>subnet_ids</code> as inputs. Keeping network creation outside gives loose coupling, so the module works with any existing VPC.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0505","card":"q-terraform-082-module-for-alb-with-target-group","sec":"terraform-s081","q":"In an AWS <code>aws_lb_target_group</code>, what do <code>port</code> and <code>protocol</code> mean, and how do they differ from the listener's?","a":"<p>They are what the load balancer uses to reach the targets. The listener's port and protocol are what clients use to reach the load balancer. The two can differ, for example HTTPS 443 on the listener and HTTP 8080 to targets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0506","card":"q-terraform-082-module-for-alb-with-target-group","sec":"terraform-s081","q":"In AWS, what do <code>target_type</code> values <code>instance</code>, <code>ip</code> and <code>lambda</code> change on a target group?","a":"<p>They set what you register as a target: instance IDs, IP addresses (needed for ECS <code>awsvpc</code> tasks and peered or on-premises IPs), or a Lambda function. Choose it at creation, because changing it replaces the group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0507","card":"q-terraform-082-module-for-alb-with-target-group","sec":"terraform-s081","q":"In AWS, what is the default health check of a target group if you do not set one?","a":"<p>A check on path <code>/</code> that expects HTTP 200. An app that returns a redirect or 404 on <code>/</code> shows as unhealthy. Set <code>path</code> and <code>matcher</code> to match a real health endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0508","card":"q-terraform-083-security-group-with-ssh-http-inbound-rul","sec":"terraform-s082","q":"In AWS, why is allowing port 22 from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> a problem, and what are the better options?","a":"<p>It exposes SSH to the whole internet, so bots try passwords and exploits constantly. Restrict it to a known CIDR (office or VPN), use a bastion host, or skip SSH and use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager, which needs no open inbound port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0509","card":"q-terraform-083-security-group-with-ssh-http-inbound-rul","sec":"terraform-s082","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between a security group and a network ACL?","a":"<p>A security group is stateful and attached to a network interface, with allow rules only. A network ACL is stateless and applies to a subnet, with allow and deny rules evaluated in order. Return traffic is allowed automatically by a security group but needs its own rule in a NACL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0510","card":"q-terraform-083-security-group-with-ssh-http-inbound-rul","sec":"terraform-s082","q":"In AWS, can a security group rule deny traffic?","a":"<p>No. Security groups only have allow rules, and anything not allowed is denied. Use a network ACL when you need an explicit deny, such as blocking one IP range.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0511","card":"q-terraform-083-security-group-with-ssh-http-inbound-rul","sec":"terraform-s082","q":"In Terraform, how do you build many similar ingress rules from a variable list without repeating blocks?","a":"<p>Use a <code>dynamic \"ingress\"</code> block with <code>for_each = var.ingress_rules</code> and read the fields from <code>ingress.value</code>. Define the rule list once as a variable of objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0512","card":"q-terraform-083-security-group-with-ssh-http-inbound-rul","sec":"terraform-s082","q":"In Terraform, what happens to a security group if you change its <code>name</code>, and how can you soften it?","a":"<p>The name cannot change in place, so Terraform replaces the group. Replacement fails while instances still use it unless <code>create_before_destroy</code> is set, and then you need <code>name_prefix</code> so the new name does not collide.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0513","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, why put CloudFront in front of an S3 bucket for a static site?","a":"<p>CloudFront caches content at edge locations for lower latency, supports HTTPS with custom domains, reduces requests to S3, and lets the bucket stay private. It also works with AWS WAF and Shield.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0514","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, what is Origin Access Control (OAC) and how does it compare with Origin Access Identity (OAI)?","a":"<p>Both let CloudFront read a private S3 bucket. OAC is the newer, recommended mechanism: it supports SSE-KMS buckets and signed requests. OAI is the legacy one. In Terraform use <code>aws_cloudfront_origin_access_control</code> and reference it in the origin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0515","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, how does the S3 bucket policy look when CloudFront uses OAC?","a":"<p>It allows <code>s3:GetObject</code> on the bucket's objects for the service principal <code>cloudfront.amazonaws.com</code>, with a condition <code>AWS:SourceArn</code> equal to the distribution's ARN. That restricts reads to that one distribution.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0516","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, why use <code>bucket_regional_domain_name</code> as the origin domain instead of the global bucket domain name?","a":"<p>The global name can return redirects for newly created buckets until DNS propagates, and it can break requests signed for the bucket's region. The regional domain name points straight to the bucket's region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0517","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, what are the options for <code>viewer_protocol_policy</code> in a CloudFront cache behavior?","a":"<p><code>allow-all</code> serves HTTP and HTTPS, <code>redirect-to-https</code> redirects HTTP to HTTPS, and <code>https-only</code> rejects plain HTTP. Production sites typically use <code>redirect-to-https</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0518","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, what does <code>default_root_object = \"index.html\"</code> do and what does it not do?","a":"<p>It serves that object when the viewer requests the distribution's root URL. It does not rewrite subdirectory paths such as <code>/docs/</code>. Those need a CloudFront Function or Lambda@Edge to map to <code>index.html</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0519","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, a CloudFront custom domain needs an HTTPS certificate. In which region must the ACM certificate be, and what does it mean in Terraform?","a":"<p>It must be in <code>us-east-1</code>, whatever region the rest of the stack uses. In Terraform you need a second <code>provider \"aws\"</code> with an alias for <code>us-east-1</code> and use it on the <code>aws_acm_certificate</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0520","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, you updated a file in S3 but viewers still get the old copy. Why, and what are the fixes?","a":"<p>CloudFront serves the cached copy until its TTL expires. Create an invalidation (<code>aws cloudfront create-invalidation --paths \"/*\"</code>) or, better, use versioned file names so each release has a new URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0521","card":"q-terraform-084-cloudfront-distribution-for-s3-bucket","sec":"terraform-s083","q":"In AWS, a single-page app on CloudFront returns 403 or 404 for deep links on refresh. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Add <code>custom_error_response</code> rules that map 403 and 404 to <code>/index.html</code> with response code 200, so the client-side router handles the path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0522","card":"q-terraform-085-nat-gateway-in-a-vpc","sec":"terraform-s084","q":"In AWS, what problem does a NAT gateway solve?","a":"<p>It lets instances in private subnets start connections to the internet (updates, external APIs) without being reachable from the internet. Replies return, but no one outside can open a connection in.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0523","card":"q-terraform-085-nat-gateway-in-a-vpc","sec":"terraform-s084","q":"In Terraform, in which subnet does an <code>aws_nat_gateway</code> go, and which route table points at it?","a":"<p>The NAT gateway sits in a public subnet because it needs a route to the internet gateway. The route table of the private subnets gets <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> pointing to the NAT gateway's ID.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0524","card":"q-terraform-085-nat-gateway-in-a-vpc","sec":"terraform-s084","q":"In Terraform, why does an <code>aws_nat_gateway</code> carry <code>depends_on = [aws_internet_gateway.igw]</code>?","a":"<p>Terraform sees no reference from the NAT gateway to the internet gateway, so it could create them in either order. The NAT gateway needs the internet gateway to exist, so an explicit dependency fixes the order. Without it the apply can fail intermittently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0525","card":"q-terraform-085-nat-gateway-in-a-vpc","sec":"terraform-s084","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between an internet gateway and a NAT gateway?","a":"<p>An internet gateway gives two-way internet access to resources that have public IPs. A NAT gateway gives outbound-only access to resources with private IPs by translating them to its own Elastic IP.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0526","card":"q-terraform-085-nat-gateway-in-a-vpc","sec":"terraform-s084","q":"In AWS, how do you make NAT highly available, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>A NAT gateway lives in one AZ, so create one per AZ, each with its own private route table. This avoids losing outbound access when an AZ fails and avoids cross-AZ data charges, but each gateway costs money per hour and per GB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0527","card":"q-terraform-085-nat-gateway-in-a-vpc","sec":"terraform-s084","q":"In AWS, how can you cut NAT gateway data charges for traffic to S3 and DynamoDB?","a":"<p>Add gateway VPC endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB. They are free, add routes to the route table, and keep that traffic off the NAT gateway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0528","card":"q-terraform-085-nat-gateway-in-a-vpc","sec":"terraform-s084","q":"In AWS, how does a NAT gateway compare with a NAT instance?","a":"<p>A NAT gateway is managed, scales automatically and is highly available inside its AZ, but costs more at low volume. A NAT instance is an EC2 instance you patch, size and fail over yourself. Managed gateways are the usual choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0529","card":"q-terraform-086-ec2-instance-behind-a-load-balancer","sec":"terraform-s085","q":"In Terraform, which resources are needed to put an EC2 instance behind an Application Load Balancer?","a":"<p>An <code>aws_lb</code>, an <code>aws_lb_target_group</code>, an <code>aws_lb_listener</code> that forwards to the target group, and a registration of the instance with the target group (<code>aws_lb_target_group_attachment</code>). Security groups and subnets in two AZs complete it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0530","card":"q-terraform-086-ec2-instance-behind-a-load-balancer","sec":"terraform-s085","q":"In AWS, what is the role of the listener compared with the target group?","a":"<p>The listener accepts client connections on a port and protocol and applies rules. The target group holds the backends and their health checks. The listener's <code>default_action</code> forwards to a target group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0531","card":"q-terraform-086-ec2-instance-behind-a-load-balancer","sec":"terraform-s085","q":"In Terraform, when do you use <code>aws_lb_target_group_attachment</code> and when an Auto Scaling group's <code>target_group_arns</code>?","a":"<p>The attachment registers one fixed instance or IP. For a group that scales, set <code>target_group_arns</code> on the <code>aws_autoscaling_group</code> so instances register and deregister themselves.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0532","card":"q-terraform-086-ec2-instance-behind-a-load-balancer","sec":"terraform-s085","q":"In AWS, how do you choose between an Application, Network and Classic Load Balancer?","a":"<p>ALB works at layer 7 (HTTP, host and path routing, WebSockets). NLB works at layer 4 for TCP and UDP with very high throughput, static IPs and low latency. Classic is legacy and not recommended for new work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0533","card":"q-terraform-086-ec2-instance-behind-a-load-balancer","sec":"terraform-s085","q":"In AWS, why place the instances in private subnets and the ALB in public subnets?","a":"<p>Clients reach only the ALB, and the instances have no public IPs, which shrinks the attack surface. The instance security group allows traffic only from the ALB's security group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0534","card":"q-terraform-086-ec2-instance-behind-a-load-balancer","sec":"terraform-s085","q":"In Terraform, <code>aws_lb</code> fails to create with a subnet error when given one subnet. What is the fix?","a":"<p>Pass at least two subnet IDs from different Availability Zones in <code>subnets</code>; the create call is rejected otherwise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0535","card":"q-terraform-087-route-53-record-for-alb","sec":"terraform-s086","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>evaluate_target_health = true</code> do in a Route 53 alias block?","a":"<p>Route 53 checks the health of the alias target (the load balancer) and stops returning the record when the target is unhealthy. This matters for failover routing. For a plain single record it adds little.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0536","card":"q-terraform-087-route-53-record-for-alb","sec":"terraform-s086","q":"In Terraform, in an alias record pointing at an ALB, whose hosted zone ID goes in the alias <code>zone_id</code>?","a":"<p>The load balancer's own hosted zone ID (the <code>zone_id</code> attribute of <code>aws_lb</code>), not the ID of your Route 53 zone. The record's top-level <code>zone_id</code> is your hosted zone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0537","card":"q-terraform-087-route-53-record-for-alb","sec":"terraform-s086","q":"In DNS, why can the root domain (zone apex) not be a CNAME, and how does Route 53 solve this for an ALB?","a":"<p>DNS standards forbid a CNAME next to the other records the apex must have (SOA, NS). Route 53 alias records behave like A or AAAA records, so they work at the apex, and queries to AWS targets are free.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0538","card":"q-terraform-087-route-53-record-for-alb","sec":"terraform-s086","q":"In Terraform, after creating an <code>aws_route53_zone</code> for a domain you bought elsewhere, what must you do?","a":"<p>Copy the zone's <code>name_servers</code> to the domain registrar so it delegates to Route 53. Until then, public DNS does not use your records.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0539","card":"q-terraform-087-route-53-record-for-alb","sec":"terraform-s086","q":"In AWS, what extra record do you need to serve an ALB over IPv6?","a":"<p>An <code>AAAA</code> alias record in addition to the <code>A</code> record, and the ALB needs <code>ip_address_type = \"dualstack\"</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0540","card":"q-terraform-087-route-53-record-for-alb","sec":"terraform-s086","q":"In Terraform, which argument must you set on a Route 53 record when you use weighted, latency or failover routing?","a":"<p><code>set_identifier</code>, a unique name per record in the set, plus the routing policy block (such as <code>weighted_routing_policy</code>). Records with the same name and type are told apart by it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0541","card":"q-terraform-087-route-53-record-for-alb","sec":"terraform-s086","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between a public and a private hosted zone?","a":"<p>A public zone answers queries from the internet. A private zone answers only inside the VPCs you associate with it, so internal names are not exposed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0542","card":"q-terraform-088-vpc-peering-connection-between-two-vpcs","sec":"terraform-s087","q":"In AWS, why does VPC peering need a route in both VPCs?","a":"<p>Peering sets up the link but does not route traffic. Each side needs a route to the other VPC's CIDR through the peering connection, otherwise requests or replies have no path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0543","card":"q-terraform-088-vpc-peering-connection-between-two-vpcs","sec":"terraform-s087","q":"In AWS, can two VPCs with overlapping CIDR ranges be peered?","a":"<p>No. Peering needs non-overlapping CIDR blocks. Plan address ranges up front, because changing them later is painful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0544","card":"q-terraform-088-vpc-peering-connection-between-two-vpcs","sec":"terraform-s087","q":"In AWS, is VPC peering transitive?","a":"<p>No. If A peers with B and B with C, A cannot reach C through B. Peering also does not carry traffic through a peer's internet gateway, NAT or VPN. Use a Transit Gateway when many VPCs need to talk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0545","card":"q-terraform-088-vpc-peering-connection-between-two-vpcs","sec":"terraform-s087","q":"In Terraform, when does <code>auto_accept = true</code> work on an <code>aws_vpc_peering_connection</code>?","a":"<p>Only when both VPCs are in the same account and region. For a different account or region, the other side must accept it with an <code>aws_vpc_peering_connection_accepter</code> and an aliased provider.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0546","card":"q-terraform-088-vpc-peering-connection-between-two-vpcs","sec":"terraform-s087","q":"In Terraform, why add peering routes to the route tables that subnets actually use rather than only the main route table?","a":"<p>A subnet with its own explicit route table does not use the main one. Without the route in its table, traffic from that subnet never reaches the peer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0547","card":"q-terraform-088-vpc-peering-connection-between-two-vpcs","sec":"terraform-s087","q":"In AWS, how do you choose between VPC peering, Transit Gateway and PrivateLink?","a":"<p>Peering is simple and cheap for a few VPCs. Transit Gateway is a hub for many VPCs and on-premises links, with transitive routing. PrivateLink exposes one service privately to other VPCs without opening whole networks, and it works even with overlapping CIDRs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0548","card":"q-terraform-088-vpc-peering-connection-between-two-vpcs","sec":"terraform-s087","q":"In AWS, what else must allow traffic after peering is routed?","a":"<p>Security groups and network ACLs. Security groups can reference a peer VPC's security group ID in some setups, or use its CIDR.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0549","card":"q-terraform-089-add-new-subnet-to-existing-vpc","sec":"terraform-s088","q":"In Terraform, what does the plan show when you add an <code>aws_subnet</code> to a VPC already under management?","a":"<p>One resource to add and nothing changing on the VPC or existing subnets. The subnet attaches through its <code>vpc_id</code> argument.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0550","card":"q-terraform-089-add-new-subnet-to-existing-vpc","sec":"terraform-s088","q":"In AWS, what rules must the CIDR of a new subnet follow?","a":"<p>It must fit inside the VPC's CIDR range and not overlap any other subnet. Otherwise the API returns an error such as <code>InvalidSubnet.Conflict</code> or <code>InvalidSubnet.Range</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0551","card":"q-terraform-089-add-new-subnet-to-existing-vpc","sec":"terraform-s088","q":"In Terraform, the VPC was created outside Terraform. How do you add a subnet to it?","a":"<p>Look it up with <code>data \"aws_vpc\"</code> (by ID or tag) and use <code>data.aws_vpc.x.id</code> as <code>vpc_id</code>. Importing the VPC is the alternative if this configuration should manage the VPC itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0552","card":"q-terraform-089-add-new-subnet-to-existing-vpc","sec":"terraform-s088","q":"In Terraform, which changes to an existing <code>aws_subnet</code> force replacement?","a":"<p>Changing <code>cidr_block</code> or <code>availability_zone</code>. A subnet cannot move, so Terraform destroys and recreates it, and deletion fails while network interfaces or instances still use it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0553","card":"q-terraform-089-add-new-subnet-to-existing-vpc","sec":"terraform-s088","q":"In AWS, what do you do when a VPC runs out of address space?","a":"<p>Add a secondary CIDR block with <code>aws_vpc_ipv4_cidr_block_association</code>, then create subnets in the new range.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0554","card":"q-terraform-090-fix-terraform-alb-not-routing-traffic","sec":"terraform-s089","q":"In AWS, what do ALB responses 502, 503 and 504 usually point to?","a":"<p>502 means the target sent an invalid response or closed the connection. 503 means no healthy targets are available. 504 means the target did not answer before the timeout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0555","card":"q-terraform-090-fix-terraform-alb-not-routing-traffic","sec":"terraform-s089","q":"In AWS, requests to a new ALB just time out. What do you check?","a":"<p>The ALB must be <code>internal = false</code> and in public subnets whose route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to an internet gateway. The ALB security group must allow the listener port from clients. Then check that DNS resolves the right name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0556","card":"q-terraform-090-fix-terraform-alb-not-routing-traffic","sec":"terraform-s089","q":"In AWS, targets are registered but show unhealthy. What are the common causes?","a":"<p>The target security group does not allow the ALB (or the health check port), the health check path or <code>matcher</code> does not match what the app returns, the app listens on a different port than the target group, or the app is not running.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0557","card":"q-terraform-090-fix-terraform-alb-not-routing-traffic","sec":"terraform-s089","q":"In Terraform, a plan applies cleanly but the ALB still does not route. Why did Terraform not catch it?","a":"<p>Terraform only creates the resources. It does not test runtime behaviour such as target health, security group logic or application startup, so you verify with the AWS CLI, health checks and test requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0558","card":"q-terraform-090-fix-terraform-alb-not-routing-traffic","sec":"terraform-s089","q":"In AWS, which three things must all be correct for traffic to flow through an ALB?","a":"<p>A listener that forwards to a target group, a target group with registered targets that pass health checks, and security groups plus routes that allow client to ALB and ALB to target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0559","card":"q-terraform-091-update-security-group-with-new-inbound-r","sec":"terraform-s090","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>type</code>, <code>security_group_id</code> and <code>source_security_group_id</code> mean on an <code>aws_security_group_rule</code>?","a":"<p><code>type</code> is <code>ingress</code> or <code>egress</code>. <code>security_group_id</code> is the group the rule is added to. <code>source_security_group_id</code> allows traffic from another group, and it cannot be combined with <code>cidr_blocks</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0560","card":"q-terraform-091-update-security-group-with-new-inbound-r","sec":"terraform-s090","q":"In AWS, does adding an ingress rule to a security group interrupt existing connections?","a":"<p>No. Rule changes apply within seconds and existing tracked connections continue. The group is not replaced, because rules are separate objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0561","card":"q-terraform-091-update-security-group-with-new-inbound-r","sec":"terraform-s090","q":"In Terraform, what happens if you apply a rule that already exists in the group?","a":"<p>AWS returns <code>InvalidPermission.Duplicate</code> and the apply fails. The rule was probably added by hand or by another resource, so remove the duplicate or manage it only in one place.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0562","card":"q-terraform-091-update-security-group-with-new-inbound-r","sec":"terraform-s090","q":"Why is it risky to open port 443 to <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> on an application instance but fine on a public load balancer?","a":"<p>A public load balancer is meant to take internet traffic and should be the only public entry. An instance should accept traffic only from the load balancer's security group. Keep wide-open rules on the edge only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0563","card":"q-terraform-091-update-security-group-with-new-inbound-r","sec":"terraform-s090","q":"In Terraform, what happens to a rule managed by <code>aws_security_group_rule</code> when you change its <code>from_port</code>?","a":"<p>The rule is replaced: the old one is deleted and a new one created. There can be a short moment without it, so change rules on production traffic carefully.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0564","card":"q-terraform-092-ec2-instance-with-specific-key-pair","sec":"terraform-s091","q":"In Terraform, does <code>key_name</code> on an <code>aws_instance</code> create a key pair?","a":"<p>No. It names a key pair that already exists in that region. To create one from your own public key use <code>aws_key_pair</code> with <code>public_key = file(\"~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\")</code> and reference its <code>key_name</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0565","card":"q-terraform-092-ec2-instance-with-specific-key-pair","sec":"terraform-s091","q":"In Terraform, why is it a bad idea to create the key with the <code>tls_private_key</code> resource?","a":"<p>The private key is stored in Terraform state in plain text, so anyone with state access can read it. Create the key pair outside Terraform and give Terraform only the public key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0566","card":"q-terraform-092-ec2-instance-with-specific-key-pair","sec":"terraform-s091","q":"In Terraform, what happens if you change <code>key_name</code> on a running instance?","a":"<p>The instance is replaced. A key pair is injected at launch only, so the change cannot be applied in place.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0567","card":"q-terraform-092-ec2-instance-with-specific-key-pair","sec":"terraform-s091","q":"In AWS, the apply fails with <code>InvalidKeyPair.NotFound</code>. What are the causes?","a":"<p>The key pair does not exist in the provider's region, or its name is mistyped. Key pairs are regional, so a key created in another region is not found.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0568","card":"q-terraform-092-ec2-instance-with-specific-key-pair","sec":"terraform-s091","q":"In Linux, SSH rejects a <code>.pem</code> key with 'UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE'. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Run <code>chmod 400 key.pem</code> so only the owner can read it. SSH refuses keys that other users can access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0569","card":"q-terraform-092-ec2-instance-with-specific-key-pair","sec":"terraform-s091","q":"In AWS, what is an alternative to key pairs for getting a shell on an instance?","a":"<p>Systems Manager Session Manager. It needs the SSM agent, an instance profile with <code>AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore</code>, and no inbound port or key. Access is logged and controlled by IAM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0570","card":"q-terraform-093-auto-scaling-group-with-launch-template","sec":"terraform-s092","q":"In Terraform, what is <code>health_check_grace_period</code> for?","a":"<p>It is the time after launch during which health check failures are ignored, so a slow-starting app is not killed before it is ready. Too short causes launch-terminate loops, too long delays real replacements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0571","card":"q-terraform-093-auto-scaling-group-with-launch-template","sec":"terraform-s092","q":"In Terraform, how is the launch template written inside an <code>aws_autoscaling_group</code>, as an attribute or a block?","a":"<p>It is a nested block, <code>launch_template { ... }</code>, with <code>id</code> (or <code>name</code>) and <code>version</code>. Writing it as an attribute with <code>=</code> is not valid for a block.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_autoscaling_group&quot; &quot;asg&quot; {\n  min_size            = 1\n  max_size            = 3\n  vpc_zone_identifier = var.subnet_ids\n\n  launch_template {\n    id      = aws_launch_template.lt.id\n    version = &quot;$Latest&quot;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0572","card":"q-terraform-093-auto-scaling-group-with-launch-template","sec":"terraform-s092","q":"In Terraform, how do you run an Auto Scaling group on a mix of On-Demand and Spot instances?","a":"<p>Use a <code>mixed_instances_policy</code> with the launch template and <code>instances_distribution</code> for the On-Demand base and Spot share. Offer several instance types in <code>override</code> blocks so Spot capacity is easier to find.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0573","card":"q-terraform-094-cloudwatch-monitoring-on-ec2-instance","sec":"terraform-s093","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>alarm_actions</code> do on an <code>aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm</code>?","a":"<p>It lists the ARNs to notify or trigger when the alarm enters <code>ALARM</code>. Targets include an SNS topic, an Auto Scaling policy, or an EC2 action such as <code>arn:aws:automate:&lt;region&gt;:ec2:recover</code>. Without actions the alarm only changes state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0574","card":"q-terraform-094-cloudwatch-monitoring-on-ec2-instance","sec":"terraform-s093","q":"In AWS, what are the three CloudWatch alarm states?","a":"<p><code>OK</code>, <code>ALARM</code> and <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>. The last means the metric has no data yet or is not reporting. <code>ok_actions</code> and <code>insufficient_data_actions</code> can fire on the other transitions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0575","card":"q-terraform-094-cloudwatch-monitoring-on-ec2-instance","sec":"terraform-s093","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>datapoints_to_alarm</code> add to <code>evaluation_periods</code>?","a":"<p>It gives an M-out-of-N rule. With <code>evaluation_periods = 5</code> and <code>datapoints_to_alarm = 3</code>, the alarm fires when 3 of the last 5 periods breach the threshold. This reduces noise from short spikes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0576","card":"q-terraform-094-cloudwatch-monitoring-on-ec2-instance","sec":"terraform-s093","q":"In AWS, which EC2 alarm detects a failed host rather than high load?","a":"<p><code>StatusCheckFailed</code> (or its <code>_System</code> and <code>_Instance</code> variants). A system check failure means an AWS-side hardware or network problem, and the <code>recover</code> action can move the instance. An instance check failure points to the OS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0577","card":"q-terraform-094-cloudwatch-monitoring-on-ec2-instance","sec":"terraform-s093","q":"In AWS, why can the CPU metric alone be misleading for a monitored EC2 instance?","a":"<p>CPU says nothing about memory, disk space or the application. Those need the CloudWatch agent or custom metrics. Pair CPU with status checks, application metrics and logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0578","card":"q-terraform-095-eks-cluster-with-single-node-group","sec":"terraform-s094","q":"In Terraform, why does an EKS setup use two IAM roles, and which managed policies does each need?","a":"<p>The cluster role is assumed by <code>eks.amazonaws.com</code> and needs <code>AmazonEKSClusterPolicy</code>. The node role is assumed by <code>ec2.amazonaws.com</code> and needs <code>AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy</code>, <code>AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy</code> and <code>AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly</code>. The control plane and the worker nodes do different jobs and need separate permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0579","card":"q-terraform-095-eks-cluster-with-single-node-group","sec":"terraform-s094","q":"In Terraform, why do EKS resources use <code>depends_on</code> for the IAM policy attachments?","a":"<p>Terraform does not see that the cluster needs the attachments finished first. Without the dependency the cluster can be created before its policies exist, or destroyed after they are removed, leaving it stuck.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0580","card":"q-terraform-095-eks-cluster-with-single-node-group","sec":"terraform-s094","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between a managed node group, self-managed nodes and Fargate for EKS?","a":"<p>A managed node group lets AWS provision and update the EC2 nodes in an Auto Scaling group. Self-managed nodes give full control but you handle AMIs and upgrades. Fargate profiles run pods serverlessly with no nodes to manage, with some limits (no DaemonSets, no GPUs).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0581","card":"q-terraform-095-eks-cluster-with-single-node-group","sec":"terraform-s094","q":"In AWS, what does an EKS cluster need from its subnets?","a":"<p>At least two subnets in different AZs. Nodes in private subnets also need a route to pull images (a NAT gateway or VPC endpoints for ECR and S3).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0582","card":"q-terraform-095-eks-cluster-with-single-node-group","sec":"terraform-s094","q":"In AWS, what do <code>endpoint_public_access</code> and <code>endpoint_private_access</code> in <code>vpc_config</code> control?","a":"<p>Who can reach the Kubernetes API server. Public access lets clients on the internet in (optionally limited by <code>public_access_cidrs</code>). Private access lets clients in the VPC reach it. For production, many teams use private access plus restricted public CIDRs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0583","card":"q-terraform-095-eks-cluster-with-single-node-group","sec":"terraform-s094","q":"In AWS, how do you get <code>kubectl</code> access after Terraform creates a cluster?","a":"<p>Run <code>aws eks update-kubeconfig --name &lt;cluster&gt;</code>. Access for IAM identities is granted with EKS access entries (newer) or the <code>aws-auth</code> ConfigMap (older).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0584","card":"q-terraform-095-eks-cluster-with-single-node-group","sec":"terraform-s094","q":"In AWS, how do pods get their own IAM permissions in EKS?","a":"<p>With IAM Roles for Service Accounts: an <code>aws_iam_openid_connect_provider</code> for the cluster's OIDC issuer, and a role whose trust policy names a Kubernetes service account. EKS Pod Identity is a newer alternative. This avoids giving every pod the node role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0585","card":"q-terraform-095-eks-cluster-with-single-node-group","sec":"terraform-s094","q":"In Terraform, why do many teams use the <code>terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws</code> module instead of raw resources?","a":"<p>EKS needs many linked pieces (roles, security groups, OIDC, add-ons, node groups). The community module wires them with tested defaults. The cost is less visibility and having to follow its upgrades.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0586","card":"q-terraform-096-iam-role-with-s3-full-access","sec":"terraform-s095","q":"In AWS, which service principal goes in the trust policy for EC2, Lambda, ECS tasks and EKS?","a":"<p><code>ec2.amazonaws.com</code>, <code>lambda.amazonaws.com</code>, <code>ecs-tasks.amazonaws.com</code> and <code>eks.amazonaws.com</code>. The principal names who may assume the role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0587","card":"q-terraform-096-iam-role-with-s3-full-access","sec":"terraform-s095","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between a role's trust policy and its permissions policy?","a":"<p>The trust policy (<code>assume_role_policy</code>) says who can assume the role. The permissions policy says what the role can do once assumed. A role needs both to be useful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0588","card":"q-terraform-096-iam-role-with-s3-full-access","sec":"terraform-s095","q":"Why is attaching <code>AmazonS3FullAccess</code> usually the wrong choice, and what is better?","a":"<p>It grants every S3 action on every bucket, which breaks least privilege. Write a customer-managed policy with only the needed actions (for example <code>s3:GetObject</code>) on the specific bucket ARN and its <code>/*</code> objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0589","card":"q-terraform-097-lambda-function-triggered-by-s3-event","sec":"terraform-s096","q":"In Terraform, why does an S3 trigger for Lambda need both <code>aws_s3_bucket_notification</code> and <code>aws_lambda_permission</code>?","a":"<p>The notification tells S3 which function to call. The permission allows the S3 service principal to invoke the function. Without the permission, S3 cannot create the notification and the apply fails with a validation error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0590","card":"q-terraform-097-lambda-function-triggered-by-s3-event","sec":"terraform-s096","q":"In Terraform, how do you make sure the permission exists before the bucket notification is created?","a":"<p>Add <code>depends_on = [aws_lambda_permission.allow_s3]</code> on the <code>aws_s3_bucket_notification</code>. The notification does not reference the permission, so Terraform could otherwise create it first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0591","card":"q-terraform-097-lambda-function-triggered-by-s3-event","sec":"terraform-s096","q":"In AWS, what does <code>source_arn</code> on an S3 <code>aws_lambda_permission</code> protect against?","a":"<p>It limits invocation to events from that one bucket. Without it, any S3 bucket in any account could trigger the function, which is the confused deputy problem. Setting <code>source_account</code> adds another guard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0592","card":"q-terraform-097-lambda-function-triggered-by-s3-event","sec":"terraform-s096","q":"In AWS, what is the risk of a Lambda function writing to the same S3 bucket that triggers it?","a":"<p>It can trigger itself in an endless loop and run up cost. Write results to a different bucket, or to a different prefix, and filter the trigger by prefix or suffix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0593","card":"q-terraform-097-lambda-function-triggered-by-s3-event","sec":"terraform-s096","q":"In Terraform, how do you limit an S3 notification to certain objects?","a":"<p>Add <code>filter_prefix</code> and <code>filter_suffix</code> on the <code>lambda_function</code> block of the notification, for example only <code>.csv</code> under <code>uploads/</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0594","card":"q-terraform-097-lambda-function-triggered-by-s3-event","sec":"terraform-s096","q":"In AWS, why do the S3 bucket and the Lambda function have to be in the same region for a direct trigger?","a":"<p>S3 event notifications target destinations in the same region as the bucket. For other regions, route events through SNS, SQS or EventBridge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0595","card":"q-terraform-098-ecs-cluster-with-fargate-task-definition","sec":"terraform-s097","q":"In AWS, which <code>cpu</code> and <code>memory</code> combinations are allowed for Fargate?","a":"<p>Only fixed pairings, for example 256 CPU units with 512, 1024 or 2048 MiB, and 512 CPU with 1 to 4 GiB. An unsupported pair makes the task definition registration fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0596","card":"q-terraform-098-ecs-cluster-with-fargate-task-definition","sec":"terraform-s097","q":"In AWS, what happens if a Fargate task in a private subnet cannot pull its image?","a":"<p>The task stays in <code>PROVISIONING</code> or stops with a <code>CannotPullContainerError</code>. The subnet needs a route to ECR (NAT gateway or VPC endpoints for ECR API, ECR DKR and S3) and the execution role needs ECR permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0597","card":"q-terraform-098-ecs-cluster-with-fargate-task-definition","sec":"terraform-s097","q":"In AWS, how do you send container logs to CloudWatch from Fargate?","a":"<p>Set the <code>awslogs</code> log driver in <code>logConfiguration</code> with <code>awslogs-group</code>, <code>awslogs-region</code> and <code>awslogs-stream-prefix</code>. The log group must exist, and the execution role needs permission to write to it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0598","card":"q-terraform-098-ecs-cluster-with-fargate-task-definition","sec":"terraform-s097","q":"In Terraform, a Fargate service sits behind an ALB. What is special about the target group?","a":"<p>It must use <code>target_type = \"ip\"</code>, because <code>awsvpc</code> tasks get their own network interfaces. The service block also needs a <code>load_balancer</code> block with the container name and port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0599","card":"q-terraform-099-sns-topic-with-email-subscription","sec":"terraform-s098","q":"In AWS, why can Terraform not fully manage an SNS email subscription?","a":"<p>The subscriber must click a confirmation link in an email. Until then the subscription is <code>PendingConfirmation</code>, and Terraform cannot confirm it for the recipient. Use HTTPS, SQS or Lambda endpoints when you need full automation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0600","card":"q-terraform-099-sns-topic-with-email-subscription","sec":"terraform-s098","q":"In Terraform, what does <code>aws_sns_topic_subscription</code> need besides <code>endpoint</code>?","a":"<p><code>topic_arn</code> and <code>protocol</code>, such as <code>email</code>, <code>sqs</code>, <code>lambda</code> or <code>https</code>. The endpoint format depends on the protocol: an address, a queue ARN, a function ARN or a URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0601","card":"q-terraform-099-sns-topic-with-email-subscription","sec":"terraform-s098","q":"In AWS, why use a topic with several subscribers instead of calling each system directly?","a":"<p>SNS fan-out decouples the sender from the receivers. The publisher sends once, and subscribers can be added or removed without changing it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0602","card":"q-terraform-099-sns-topic-with-email-subscription","sec":"terraform-s098","q":"In AWS, how can a subscriber receive only some of the messages on a topic?","a":"<p>Add a <code>filter_policy</code> to the subscription, matching on message attributes. Messages that do not match are not delivered to that subscriber.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0603","card":"q-terraform-100-cloudwatch-alarm-for-ec2-cpu-utilization","sec":"terraform-s099","q":"In AWS, what does a CPU alarm with <code>period = 300</code>, <code>evaluation_periods = 2</code> and <code>threshold = 80</code> actually require?","a":"<p>The 5-minute average CPU must exceed 80 percent for two consecutive periods, so about 10 minutes of sustained load. It ignores short spikes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0604","card":"q-terraform-100-cloudwatch-alarm-for-ec2-cpu-utilization","sec":"terraform-s099","q":"In Terraform, why is <code>statistic = \"Average\"</code> a common choice for CPU, and when would you choose <code>Maximum</code>?","a":"<p>Average smooths noise and shows sustained load. Maximum catches short peaks, for example to detect a single hot instance. Pick based on what you want to be paged for.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0605","card":"q-terraform-100-cloudwatch-alarm-for-ec2-cpu-utilization","sec":"terraform-s099","q":"In AWS, what do <code>comparison_operator</code> options like <code>GreaterThanThreshold</code> and <code>GreaterThanOrEqualToThreshold</code> change?","a":"<p>They decide whether a value exactly equal to the threshold counts as a breach. Choose the one that matches your intent. For metrics where low is bad, use the <code>LessThan</code> variants.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0606","card":"q-terraform-101-elastic-beanstalk-application","sec":"terraform-s100","q":"In AWS, what is Elastic Beanstalk and when would you choose it over ECS or EKS?","a":"<p>It is a platform service where you upload application code and it provisions the environment (instances, load balancer, scaling, health monitoring). Choose it for a quick, conventional web app with little infrastructure work. Choose ECS or EKS when you need container control, custom networking or many services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0607","card":"q-terraform-101-elastic-beanstalk-application","sec":"terraform-s100","q":"In Elastic Beanstalk, what is the difference between an application, an application version and an environment?","a":"<p>The application is a logical container. A version is a labelled deployable bundle of code stored in S3. An environment is a running set of AWS resources that runs one version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0608","card":"q-terraform-101-elastic-beanstalk-application","sec":"terraform-s100","q":"In Terraform, what does a <code>setting</code> block on an <code>aws_elastic_beanstalk_environment</code> do?","a":"<p>It sets one option in a namespace, such as an instance type or scaling limits. Each block has <code>namespace</code>, <code>name</code> and <code>value</code>. They are the Terraform form of Beanstalk option settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0609","card":"q-terraform-101-elastic-beanstalk-application","sec":"terraform-s100","q":"In AWS, why is a hardcoded <code>solution_stack_name</code> a maintenance risk?","a":"<p>Platform versions are retired and replaced, so a fixed name eventually stops being available. Look it up with <code>data \"aws_elastic_beanstalk_solution_stack\"</code> and a name regex, or use a managed platform update policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0610","card":"q-terraform-101-elastic-beanstalk-application","sec":"terraform-s100","q":"In AWS, what deployment policies does Elastic Beanstalk offer?","a":"<p>All at once, rolling, rolling with an additional batch, immutable and traffic splitting. All at once is fastest but has downtime risk. Immutable and traffic splitting are safest, and cost extra capacity during the deploy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0611","card":"q-terraform-101-elastic-beanstalk-application","sec":"terraform-s100","q":"In Elastic Beanstalk, what is the drawback of heavy changes made in the console outside Terraform?","a":"<p>Terraform sees the option drift and may revert it on the next apply. Beanstalk also manages some resources itself, so manage the environment settings in one place.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0612","card":"q-terraform-102-debug-terraform-ec2-instance-not-accessi","sec":"terraform-s101","q":"In AWS, an EC2 instance launched by Terraform cannot be reached over SSH. In what order do you check?","a":"<p>First check the instance is <code>running</code> and passes status checks. Then check it has a public IP, its subnet's route table has an internet gateway route, the security group allows port 22 from your IP, and the network ACL allows it both ways. Then check the key pair and the OS user name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0613","card":"q-terraform-102-debug-terraform-ec2-instance-not-accessi","sec":"terraform-s101","q":"In AWS, how do you see why an instance fails status checks or cannot boot?","a":"<p>Check the system log or screenshot in the console or with <code>aws ec2 get-console-output</code>. Instance status checks point to OS-level problems, and system status checks point to AWS-side problems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0614","card":"q-terraform-102-debug-terraform-ec2-instance-not-accessi","sec":"terraform-s101","q":"In AWS, SSH times out versus 'connection refused' versus 'permission denied'. What does each suggest?","a":"<p>A timeout usually means a network block (security group, route, NACL). Connection refused means the packet arrived but nothing listens on that port. Permission denied means the network is fine but the key or user name is wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0615","card":"q-terraform-103-add-iam-policy-to-existing-role","sec":"terraform-s102","q":"In Terraform, what is the difference between <code>aws_iam_role_policy</code> and <code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code>?","a":"<p><code>aws_iam_role_policy</code> creates an inline policy that belongs to one role. <code>aws_iam_role_policy_attachment</code> attaches a separate managed policy, which can be reused by many roles. Managed policies are easier to audit and reuse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0616","card":"q-terraform-103-add-iam-policy-to-existing-role","sec":"terraform-s102","q":"In Terraform, the role was created outside Terraform. How do you attach a policy to it?","a":"<p>Reference it with <code>data \"aws_iam_role\"</code> by name and use <code>data.aws_iam_role.x.name</code> in the attachment. The role itself stays unmanaged, and Terraform only owns the attachment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0617","card":"q-terraform-103-add-iam-policy-to-existing-role","sec":"terraform-s102","q":"In AWS, how many managed policies can be attached to one IAM role by default?","a":"<p>10 by default, and the quota can be raised to 20. Consolidate policies or use inline ones if you hit it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0618","card":"q-terraform-103-add-iam-policy-to-existing-role","sec":"terraform-s102","q":"In AWS, how do you scope an S3 read policy to one bucket?","a":"<p>Allow <code>s3:GetObject</code> on <code>arn:aws:s3:::bucket/*</code> (objects) and <code>s3:ListBucket</code> on <code>arn:aws:s3:::bucket</code> (the bucket). The two actions apply to different resource ARNs, and mixing them is a common mistake.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0619","card":"q-terraform-104-scale-eks-node-group-to-3-nodes","sec":"terraform-s103","q":"In Terraform, what do <code>min_size</code>, <code>desired_size</code> and <code>max_size</code> in an EKS <code>scaling_config</code> mean?","a":"<p>The node group keeps the node count between min and max. <code>desired_size</code> is the current target. A cluster autoscaler or Karpenter can change the desired size at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0620","card":"q-terraform-104-scale-eks-node-group-to-3-nodes","sec":"terraform-s103","q":"In Terraform, why do teams add <code>ignore_changes = [scaling_config[0].desired_size]</code> to an EKS node group?","a":"<p>An autoscaler changes the desired size outside Terraform. Without the ignore rule, the next apply would reset it to the value in code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0621","card":"q-terraform-104-scale-eks-node-group-to-3-nodes","sec":"terraform-s103","q":"In AWS, does scaling a managed node group from 1 to 3 nodes disrupt running pods?","a":"<p>No. Terraform updates the group in place and EKS adds nodes. Scaling down is different: nodes are drained, and pods without a disruption budget or spare capacity can be interrupted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0622","card":"q-terraform-104-scale-eks-node-group-to-3-nodes","sec":"terraform-s103","q":"In AWS, new nodes do not appear after raising <code>desired_size</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>The node group status and health issues, instance quotas, available IPs in the subnets, and the node IAM role policies. Check <code>aws eks describe-nodegroup</code> for the issue messages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0623","card":"q-terraform-104-scale-eks-node-group-to-3-nodes","sec":"terraform-s103","q":"In Kubernetes, why is <code>kubectl get nodes</code> a useful check after scaling?","a":"<p>It shows whether the new nodes joined the cluster and are <code>Ready</code>. A node that exists in EC2 but not in Kubernetes usually has an IAM, CNI or networking problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0624","card":"q-terraform-105-debug-terraform-lambda-function-failure","sec":"terraform-s104","q":"In AWS, where do you look first when a Lambda function fails after Terraform deploys it?","a":"<p>The CloudWatch Logs group <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code> and the function's <code>Errors</code> metric. A missing log group or empty logs often means the execution role lacks permission to write logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0625","card":"q-terraform-105-debug-terraform-lambda-function-failure","sec":"terraform-s104","q":"In AWS, what are common reasons a newly deployed Lambda function fails to start?","a":"<p>A wrong <code>handler</code> path, a runtime that does not match the code, a missing dependency in the zip, an unreadable zip, or an execution role that cannot be assumed. <code>Runtime.ImportModuleError</code> and <code>Runtime.HandlerNotFound</code> point to code packaging or handler issues.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0626","card":"q-terraform-105-debug-terraform-lambda-function-failure","sec":"terraform-s104","q":"In AWS, what do the <code>Task timed out</code> and <code>Out of memory</code> log messages mean and how do you fix them?","a":"<p>The first means the function ran longer than its <code>timeout</code> (default 3 seconds), so raise <code>timeout</code> or speed up the code. The second means the function used all <code>memory_size</code>, so raise memory. Memory also scales CPU.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0627","card":"q-terraform-105-debug-terraform-lambda-function-failure","sec":"terraform-s104","q":"In AWS, a Lambda function in a VPC cannot reach the internet. Why?","a":"<p>Functions in a VPC have no public IP, even in a public subnet. They need a NAT gateway in a public subnet or VPC endpoints for the AWS services they call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0628","card":"q-terraform-106-fix-terraform-ec2-launch-issue-verify-wi","sec":"terraform-s105","q":"In AWS, what do <code>InsufficientInstanceCapacity</code> and <code>InstanceLimitExceeded</code> mean when launching EC2?","a":"<p>The first means AWS has no capacity of that type in that zone right now, so retry later, use another instance type or another AZ. The second means you hit a vCPU quota, so request a limit increase.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0629","card":"q-terraform-106-fix-terraform-ec2-launch-issue-verify-wi","sec":"terraform-s105","q":"In AWS, how do you check the state of an instance from the CLI after <code>terraform apply</code>?","a":"<p>Run <code>aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids &lt;id&gt; --query \"Reservations[*].Instances[*].[InstanceId, State.Name]\"</code>. States include <code>pending</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>stopping</code>, <code>stopped</code> and <code>terminated</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0630","card":"q-terraform-106-fix-terraform-ec2-launch-issue-verify-wi","sec":"terraform-s105","q":"In AWS, an instance goes from <code>pending</code> straight to <code>terminated</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>Look at <code>StateReason</code> in <code>describe-instances</code>. Common causes are an encrypted volume whose KMS key the launch cannot use, an EBS quota limit, or a missing snapshot. Terraform shows an error as well.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0631","card":"q-terraform-106-fix-terraform-ec2-launch-issue-verify-wi","sec":"terraform-s105","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between <code>stopped</code> and <code>terminated</code>, and which one can be restarted?","a":"<p>A stopped instance keeps its EBS volume and can be started again. A terminated instance is deleted and cannot be recovered. Stopped instances still pay for storage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0632","card":"q-terraform-106-fix-terraform-ec2-launch-issue-verify-wi","sec":"terraform-s105","q":"In AWS, what do <code>InvalidAMIID.NotFound</code> and <code>InvalidSubnetID.NotFound</code> usually tell you?","a":"<p>The ID is wrong for the region or account. AMI IDs and subnet IDs are regional, so a value copied from another region fails. Look AMIs up with a data source.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0633","card":"q-terraform-107-debug-terraform-auto-scaling-group-not-s","sec":"terraform-s106","q":"In AWS, an Auto Scaling group is not adding instances. Which command shows why?","a":"<p><code>aws autoscaling describe-scaling-activities --auto-scaling-group-name &lt;name&gt;</code> lists each launch attempt with a status and cause, such as a quota error, missing capacity or a bad AMI. It is the first place to look.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0634","card":"q-terraform-107-debug-terraform-auto-scaling-group-not-s","sec":"terraform-s106","q":"In AWS, can a group scale out when <code>desired_capacity</code> already equals <code>max_size</code>?","a":"<p>No. The group never goes past <code>max_size</code>, so scale-out policies have no effect. Raise <code>max_size</code> if the limit is too low.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0635","card":"q-terraform-107-debug-terraform-auto-scaling-group-not-s","sec":"terraform-s106","q":"In AWS, what if there are no scaling policies at all on an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>The group stays at its desired capacity and only replaces unhealthy instances. Scaling needs a policy (target tracking, step or scheduled) and, for step policies, CloudWatch alarms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0636","card":"q-terraform-107-debug-terraform-auto-scaling-group-not-s","sec":"terraform-s106","q":"In AWS, what is the effect of a cooldown or instance warm-up on scaling?","a":"<p>After a scaling action, the group waits before another one, and new instances are not counted in metrics until warm-up ends. Too long a value makes scaling feel slow, and too short can cause over-scaling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0637","card":"q-terraform-107-debug-terraform-auto-scaling-group-not-s","sec":"terraform-s106","q":"In AWS, instances launch and are terminated again in a loop. What are the common causes?","a":"<p>The health check fails (grace period too short or a wrong health path), the launch template has a bad AMI or user data, or the instance cannot reach what it needs at boot. Check the scaling activities and the instance logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0638","card":"q-terraform-107-debug-terraform-auto-scaling-group-not-s","sec":"terraform-s106","q":"In AWS, what do <code>Insufficient capacity</code> and <code>Invalid subnet</code> messages in scaling activities mean for <code>vpc_zone_identifier</code>?","a":"<p>The chosen subnets or AZs cannot supply the instance type, or a subnet ID does not belong to the VPC. Spread the group over more AZs and allow several instance types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0639","card":"q-terraform-108-create-sns-topic-with-terraform-subscrib","sec":"terraform-s107","q":"In Terraform, why add an <code>output</code> for the ARN of an <code>aws_sns_topic</code> when the topic is created by the same configuration?","a":"<p>The ARN is the identifier that other tools need. Scripts and the AWS CLI (for example <code>aws sns subscribe --topic-arn ...</code>), other Terraform configurations (through <code>terraform_remote_state</code>) and CI jobs can read it with <code>terraform output -raw sns_topic_arn</code> instead of copying it by hand from the console.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Hcl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resource &quot;aws_sns_topic&quot; &quot;example&quot; { name = &quot;my-sns-topic&quot; }\noutput &quot;sns_topic_arn&quot; { value = aws_sns_topic.example.arn }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0640","card":"q-terraform-108-create-sns-topic-with-terraform-subscrib","sec":"terraform-s107","q":"In AWS, which protocols can an SNS subscription use besides <code>email</code>?","a":"<p><code>email</code>, <code>email-json</code>, <code>sms</code>, <code>http</code>, <code>https</code>, <code>sqs</code>, <code>lambda</code>, <code>application</code> (mobile push) and <code>firehose</code>. The protocol decides what the <code>--notification-endpoint</code> value must be: an address for email, a phone number for SMS, a URL for HTTP(S), or an ARN for SQS, Lambda and Firehose.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0641","card":"q-terraform-108-create-sns-topic-with-terraform-subscrib","sec":"terraform-s107","q":"A team creates the SNS topic with Terraform but subscribes people with <code>aws sns subscribe</code> from a script. What is the downside of that split?","a":"<p>The subscription is not in Terraform state, so it is not reviewed, not versioned and not recreated when the topic is rebuilt. It is also invisible to <code>plan</code>. For targets Terraform can confirm automatically (SQS, Lambda, HTTPS with auto-confirm), use <code>aws_sns_topic_subscription</code>. For email, the CLI or console step is acceptable because the recipient must confirm anyway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0642","card":"q-terraform-109-check-ec2-status-reboot-if-unresponsive","sec":"terraform-s108","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between the instance state and the instance status checks of an EC2 instance, and which CLI commands show each?","a":"<p>The state (<code>pending</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>stopping</code>, <code>stopped</code>, <code>terminated</code>) comes from <code>aws ec2 describe-instances</code> and says what AWS is doing with the instance. The status checks come from <code>aws ec2 describe-instance-status</code> and say whether it is healthy. A <code>running</code> instance can still fail its checks, so \"running\" alone does not mean \"responsive\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0643","card":"q-terraform-109-check-ec2-status-reboot-if-unresponsive","sec":"terraform-s108","q":"In AWS, an EC2 instance is <code>running</code> but fails the instance status check. What does <code>aws ec2 reboot-instances</code> do, and when is it not enough?","a":"<p>It asks the operating system to restart, like a reboot on a physical machine. The instance keeps its instance ID, its private and public IP addresses, its EBS volumes and its instance store data. It helps with a hung OS or an out-of-memory host. A failing <strong>system</strong> status check points at the underlying host, so a reboot does not help; stop and start the instance, which moves it to new hardware.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0644","card":"q-terraform-109-check-ec2-status-reboot-if-unresponsive","sec":"terraform-s108","q":"In AWS, how does stop and start of an EC2 instance differ from a reboot?","a":"<p>A reboot keeps the instance on the same host. Stop and start moves it to a new host, releases the automatically assigned public IPv4 address (an Elastic IP stays), and loses instance store data. EBS data survives both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0645","card":"q-terraform-109-check-ec2-status-reboot-if-unresponsive","sec":"terraform-s108","q":"An Auto Scaling group member is unresponsive. Why is rebooting it by hand usually the wrong fix?","a":"<p>The group's health checks replace unhealthy instances on their own, and a manual reboot hides the problem and leaves no record. Mark the instance unhealthy (<code>aws autoscaling set-instance-health --health-status Unhealthy</code>) or fix the cause in the launch template, so the group replaces it with a fresh instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0646","card":"q-terraform-110-identify-terminate-ec2-instances-older-t","sec":"terraform-s109","q":"In a shell, what does <code>xargs -r</code> do in this pipeline, and what goes wrong without it?","a":"<p><code>-r</code> (<code>--no-run-if-empty</code>) stops <code>xargs</code> from running the command when the input is empty. Without it, <code>terminate-instances --instance-ids</code> runs with no IDs and fails with an error. The <code>-r</code> option is GNU <code>xargs</code>; BSD/macOS <code>xargs</code> skips empty input by default.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 describe-instances --query &quot;Reservations[*].Instances[?LaunchTime&lt;&#x27;$(date -u -d &#x27;30 days ago&#x27; +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ&#x27;)&#x27;].InstanceId&quot; --output text | xargs -r aws ec2 terminate-instances --instance-ids</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0647","card":"q-terraform-110-identify-terminate-ec2-instances-older-t","sec":"terraform-s109","q":"In a shell, <code>date -d '30 days ago'</code> works on Linux but fails on macOS. Why, and what do you do in a script that has to run on both?","a":"<p><code>-d</code> is a GNU <code>date</code> option. macOS uses BSD <code>date</code>, which uses <code>-v-30d</code>. For a portable script compute the timestamp in Python or use <code>gdate</code> from GNU coreutils.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0648","card":"q-terraform-110-identify-terminate-ec2-instances-older-t","sec":"terraform-s109","q":"What safeguards would you add before terminating every EC2 instance older than 30 days with a script?","a":"<p>Terminate is irreversible, so:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Filter by a tag (for example <code>Environment=dev</code> or <code>AutoDelete=true</code>) instead of age alone.</li>\n  <li>Skip instances in an Auto Scaling group (the group would relaunch them) and anything managed by Terraform.</li>\n  <li>Run <code>aws ec2 terminate-instances --dry-run</code> or just print the list first.</li>\n  <li>Turn on termination protection for important instances (<code>disableApiTermination</code>).</li>\n  <li>Log what was deleted.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0649","card":"q-terraform-110-identify-terminate-ec2-instances-older-t","sec":"terraform-s109","q":"An EC2 instance managed by Terraform is terminated by hand with the CLI. What does the next <code>terraform plan</code> show, and why is this a bad habit?","a":"<p>The resource is in state but gone, so <code>plan</code> shows it will be created again. The CLI change bypasses review and state, so the team learns about it only on the next plan. To remove an instance, delete it from the configuration and apply, or use <code>terraform destroy -target</code> for a one-off.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0650","card":"q-terraform-110-identify-terminate-ec2-instances-older-t","sec":"terraform-s109","q":"In <code>aws ec2 describe-instances</code>, what does the <code>Reservations[*].Instances[*]</code> nesting in the output mean?","a":"<p>Each element of <code>Reservations</code> is one launch request, and it holds a list of <code>Instances</code> started by that request. A flat list of instances therefore needs both levels in the <code>--query</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0651","card":"q-terraform-111-move-old-s3-files-to-glacier","sec":"terraform-s110","q":"What is wrong with using <code>aws s3api put-object --bucket &lt;bucket-name&gt; --key {} --storage-class GLACIER</code> to move existing objects to Glacier?","a":"<p><code>put-object</code> uploads new content and here has no <code>--body</code>. It writes an empty object over the key, so the original data is lost (on an unversioned bucket) or hidden behind a new empty version. To change the storage class of an existing object, copy it onto itself with the new class, for example <code>aws s3 cp s3://bucket/key s3://bucket/key --storage-class GLACIER</code>, or <code>copy-object</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3api list-objects-v2 --bucket &lt;bucket-name&gt; --query &quot;Contents[?LastModified&lt;&#x27;$(date -u -d &#x27;30 days ago&#x27; +%Y-%m-%d)&#x27;].Key&quot; --output text | xargs -I {} aws s3api put-object --bucket &lt;bucket-name&gt; --key {} --storage-class GLACIER</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0652","card":"q-terraform-111-move-old-s3-files-to-glacier","sec":"terraform-s110","q":"In AWS S3, why is a lifecycle rule better than a CLI loop for moving old objects to Glacier?","a":"<p>A lifecycle rule runs inside S3 on a schedule, needs no machine or credentials to run a script, handles any number of objects, and can also expire old objects and old versions. A loop costs request charges per object, can be interrupted halfway, and must be re-run. In Terraform the rule is an <code>aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration</code> with a <code>transition</code> block (for example <code>days = 30</code>, <code>storage_class = \"GLACIER\"</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0653","card":"q-terraform-111-move-old-s3-files-to-glacier","sec":"terraform-s110","q":"After an S3 object is moved to the <code>GLACIER</code> storage class, why does <code>aws s3 cp</code> of that object fail?","a":"<p>Objects in Glacier Flexible Retrieval and Deep Archive are archived and cannot be read directly. You first restore them (<code>aws s3api restore-object</code> with a retrieval tier and a number of days), wait for the retrieval to finish, and then read the temporary copy. <code>GLACIER_IR</code> (Instant Retrieval) is the exception, it is readable at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0654","card":"q-terraform-112-troubleshoot-auto-scaling-group-not-laun","sec":"terraform-s111","q":"In AWS, an Auto Scaling group does not launch instances. What do <code>describe-auto-scaling-groups</code> and <code>describe-scaling-activities</code> each tell you?","a":"<p><code>describe-auto-scaling-groups</code> shows the configuration and the current numbers: <code>MinSize</code>, <code>MaxSize</code>, <code>DesiredCapacity</code>, the instances and their health, the launch template and its version, subnets, and suspended processes. <code>describe-scaling-activities</code> is the history of launches and terminations with a <code>StatusCode</code> and a <code>StatusMessage</code>. A failed activity states the cause, for example an invalid AMI or insufficient capacity.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws autoscaling describe-auto-scaling-groups --auto-scaling-group-names &lt;asg-name&gt;\naws autoscaling describe-scaling-activities --auto-scaling-group-name &lt;asg-name&gt; --max-items 10</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0655","card":"q-terraform-112-troubleshoot-auto-scaling-group-not-laun","sec":"terraform-s111","q":"In AWS, what are the usual reasons a scaling activity to launch an instance fails?","a":"<p>Typical causes, with the message in the failed activity:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The AMI or launch template version does not exist or is not shared with the account.</li>\n  <li>Instance type not available in the chosen Availability Zone (insufficient capacity).</li>\n  <li>An EC2 vCPU or instance quota is reached.</li>\n  <li>The subnet has no free IP addresses.</li>\n  <li>The launch template names a security group or key pair that is in a different VPC or region.</li>\n  <li>Encrypted EBS volumes use a KMS key the service-linked role cannot use.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0656","card":"q-terraform-112-troubleshoot-auto-scaling-group-not-laun","sec":"terraform-s111","q":"In AWS, an Auto Scaling group shows <code>DesiredCapacity</code> 3 but only 2 instances. How can you tell whether it is still trying?","a":"<p>Look at the scaling activities. A recent entry in <code>InProgress</code> or <code>Failed</code> shows the attempts. A group may also be at <code>MaxSize</code>, or have the <code>Launch</code> process suspended (<code>SuspendedProcesses</code> in <code>describe-auto-scaling-groups</code>), in which case it does nothing at all.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0657","card":"q-terraform-112-troubleshoot-auto-scaling-group-not-laun","sec":"terraform-s111","q":"In AWS, how does an Auto Scaling group behave when launches keep failing in one Availability Zone?","a":"<p>The group tries to keep the zones balanced, and after repeated failures it backs off and retries. Listing subnets in several Availability Zones and several instance types (a mixed instances policy) reduces the chance that a capacity problem in one place stops scaling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0658","card":"q-terraform-113-fetch-last-10-minutes-of-ecs-task-logs","sec":"terraform-s112","q":"In AWS CloudWatch Logs, why does <code>aws logs get-log-events</code> need both a log group name and a log stream name?","a":"<p>A log group is a container for streams, and events belong to one stream (usually one task or instance). <code>get-log-events</code> reads a single stream in order. For an ECS task using the <code>awslogs</code> driver the stream is named <code>&lt;stream-prefix&gt;/&lt;container-name&gt;/&lt;task-id&gt;</code>, so you need the task ID to find it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws logs get-log-events --log-group-name &lt;log-group-name&gt; --log-stream-name &lt;log-stream-name&gt; --start-time $(date -d &#x27;10 minutes ago&#x27; +%s000) --end-time $(date +%s000) --output text</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0659","card":"q-terraform-113-fetch-last-10-minutes-of-ecs-task-logs","sec":"terraform-s112","q":"In AWS CloudWatch Logs, what unit do <code>--start-time</code> and <code>--end-time</code> use, and why does the command append <code>000</code> to <code>date +%s</code>?","a":"<p>They are milliseconds since the Unix epoch. <code>date +%s</code> gives seconds, and appending <code>000</code> turns it into milliseconds. A value in seconds would point to a date in 1970 and return nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0660","card":"q-terraform-113-fetch-last-10-minutes-of-ecs-task-logs","sec":"terraform-s112","q":"In AWS, how do you read the last 10 minutes of logs from a whole log group, across all streams, without knowing the stream name?","a":"<p>Use <code>aws logs tail &lt;log-group&gt; --since 10m</code> (AWS CLI v2), which can also <code>--follow</code> and <code>--filter-pattern</code>. Or use <code>aws logs filter-log-events --log-group-name ...</code>, which searches all streams of the group. <code>get-log-events</code> is for one known stream.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0661","card":"q-terraform-113-fetch-last-10-minutes-of-ecs-task-logs","sec":"terraform-s112","q":"In AWS CloudWatch Logs, <code>get-log-events</code> returns no events for an ECS task that is running. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The task definition's <code>logConfiguration</code> uses <code>awslogs</code> with the right group, region and <code>awslogs-stream-prefix</code>.</li>\n  <li>The log group exists (the driver does not create it unless <code>awslogs-create-group</code> is set).</li>\n  <li>The task execution role has <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code>.</li>\n  <li>The time window covers the events.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0662","card":"q-terraform-114-check-rds-status-initiate-failover","sec":"terraform-s113","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between <code>aws rds failover-db-cluster</code> and <code>aws rds reboot-db-instance --force-failover</code>?","a":"<p><code>failover-db-cluster</code> is for Aurora (and Multi-AZ DB clusters). It promotes a reader to be the writer. <code>reboot-db-instance --force-failover</code> is for a Multi-AZ DB instance (non-Aurora): it reboots the primary and fails over to the standby. A single-AZ instance has no standby, so neither makes sense for it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0663","card":"q-terraform-114-check-rds-status-initiate-failover","sec":"terraform-s113","q":"In AWS RDS, what does an application see during a Multi-AZ failover, and how should it be built to cope?","a":"<p>Connections drop for a short time (typically tens of seconds up to a couple of minutes). RDS moves the DNS name of the endpoint to the new primary, so the application must connect with the endpoint name and not an IP address, reconnect on errors, and retry with backoff. Connection pools with cached DNS can keep old connections too long.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0664","card":"q-terraform-114-check-rds-status-initiate-failover","sec":"terraform-s113","q":"In AWS, which <code>DBInstanceStatus</code> values should you expect around a failover or modification, and what does <code>available</code> mean?","a":"<p>Values such as <code>available</code>, <code>modifying</code>, <code>rebooting</code>, <code>backing-up</code>, <code>failing-over</code> and <code>storage-optimization</code>. <code>available</code> means the instance accepts connections and there is no operation in progress. Wait for <code>available</code> (for example with <code>aws rds wait db-instance-available</code>) before the next change or the next step of a script.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0665","card":"q-terraform-114-check-rds-status-initiate-failover","sec":"terraform-s113","q":"Why should you test a failover in a non-production environment before you need it?","a":"<p>You learn the real recovery time, whether the application reconnects and whether DNS caching or hard-coded addresses break it. A planned test is cheap, and a first failover in an outage is not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0666","card":"q-terraform-115-delete-unused-elastic-ips","sec":"terraform-s114","q":"In <code>aws ec2 describe-addresses</code>, why does the query filter on <code>AssociationId==null</code> to find unused Elastic IPs?","a":"<p>An Elastic IP that is attached to an instance or network interface has an <code>AssociationId</code>. One that is allocated but not attached has none. Filtering on that field lists the idle addresses, and <code>AllocationId</code> is what <code>release-address</code> needs for a VPC address.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 describe-addresses --query &quot;Addresses[?AssociationId==null].AllocationId&quot; --output text | xargs -r aws ec2 release-address --allocation-id</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0667","card":"q-terraform-115-delete-unused-elastic-ips","sec":"terraform-s114","q":"In a shell, what breaks when <code>xargs</code> passes several allocation IDs to <code>aws ec2 release-address --allocation-id</code>?","a":"<p><code>--allocation-id</code> takes one value and <code>--output text</code> prints all IDs on one line, so <code>xargs</code> calls the command once with many arguments and the CLI fails on the extra ones. Add <code>-n 1</code> so each call gets one ID.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0668","card":"q-terraform-115-delete-unused-elastic-ips","sec":"terraform-s114","q":"In AWS, what happens if you release an Elastic IP that a DNS record or a partner's allow-list still uses?","a":"<p>The address returns to the AWS pool and cannot be taken back reliably. Anything that pointed to it now points to nobody, or later to another customer. Check DNS records and firewall allow-lists before releasing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0669","card":"q-terraform-115-delete-unused-elastic-ips","sec":"terraform-s114","q":"As of 2024, how does AWS charge for public IPv4 addresses, and how does that affect idle Elastic IPs?","a":"<p>Since February 2024 AWS charges for every public IPv4 address, whether in use or idle (about $0.005 per hour per address, check the current price). Idle Elastic IPs used to be the only charged case, so cleaning them is still worth it, but in-use public IPs also cost money now.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0670","card":"q-terraform-116-check-lambda-function-failure-logs","sec":"terraform-s115","q":"In AWS CloudWatch Logs, what do the log group name <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code> and the <code>--filter-pattern \"ERROR\"</code> do in this command?","a":"<p>Lambda writes each function's logs to a log group named <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code> by default. <code>filter-log-events</code> searches all streams of that group for events matching the pattern, and <code>--limit 10</code> caps the number of events returned. The term match is case-sensitive, so <code>ERROR</code> does not match <code>Error</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws logs filter-log-events --log-group-name /aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt; --filter-pattern &quot;ERROR&quot; --limit 10</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0671","card":"q-terraform-116-check-lambda-function-failure-logs","sec":"terraform-s115","q":"In AWS CloudWatch Logs, why can <code>filter-log-events</code> miss a Python exception in a Lambda log?","a":"<p>The text is matched literally and case-sensitively. A Python traceback contains <code>Traceback</code> and the exception class name, not necessarily <code>ERROR</code>. Lambda's own failure lines include <code>Task timed out</code> and <code>Runtime exited</code>. Filtering on several terms, or using CloudWatch Logs Insights, is more reliable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0672","card":"q-terraform-116-check-lambda-function-failure-logs","sec":"terraform-s115","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are the other places to see failures besides the log group?","a":"<p>The <code>Errors</code>, <code>Throttles</code> and <code>Duration</code> metrics in CloudWatch, the function's asynchronous invocation destinations or dead-letter queue for failed async events, and X-Ray traces if tracing is on. The <code>REPORT</code> line at the end of each invocation shows duration, billed duration and memory used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0673","card":"q-terraform-116-check-lambda-function-failure-logs","sec":"terraform-s115","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is the benefit of <code>aws logs tail /aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt; --follow</code> while debugging?","a":"<p>It streams new events to your terminal as the function runs (AWS CLI v2), so you can invoke the function and see its output at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0674","card":"q-terraform-117-investigate-elb-unhealthy-instances-dere","sec":"terraform-s116","q":"In AWS, which states can a target have in <code>describe-target-health</code>, and what do <code>unhealthy</code> and <code>draining</code> mean?","a":"<p>The states are <code>initial</code>, <code>healthy</code>, <code>unhealthy</code>, <code>unused</code>, <code>draining</code> and <code>unavailable</code>. <code>unhealthy</code> means it failed the health check threshold. <code>draining</code> means it is being deregistered and the load balancer waits for in-flight requests. The response also has a <code>Reason</code> such as <code>Target.Timeout</code> or <code>Target.ResponseCodeMismatch</code> that tells why a check failed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn &lt;target-group-arn&gt; --query &quot;TargetHealthDescriptions[?TargetHealth.State==&#x27;unhealthy&#x27;].Target.Id&quot; --output text</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0675","card":"q-terraform-117-investigate-elb-unhealthy-instances-dere","sec":"terraform-s116","q":"In AWS, what happens to traffic when you run <code>aws elbv2 deregister-targets</code> for a target?","a":"<p>The target stops getting new requests. Existing requests have the deregistration delay (default 300 seconds) to finish while the target is in <code>draining</code>. After that it is removed from the target group. The instance itself keeps running.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0676","card":"q-terraform-117-investigate-elb-unhealthy-instances-dere","sec":"terraform-s116","q":"In AWS, an instance behind an ALB is unhealthy. Why is deregistering it often a poor fix when the instance belongs to an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>The group registers its instances itself. A deregistered instance still counts as a healthy member of the group, so the group does not replace it, and it can be registered again by the group later. Fix the cause or let the group's ELB health check replace the instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0677","card":"q-terraform-117-investigate-elb-unhealthy-instances-dere","sec":"terraform-s116","q":"In AWS, how do you shorten the delay before an ALB stops sending traffic to a target you want to remove?","a":"<p>Lower the <code>deregistration_delay.timeout_seconds</code> attribute of the target group (default 300). Use a small value when requests are short, a larger one for long-running requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0678","card":"q-terraform-118-update-ec2-security-group-for-https","sec":"terraform-s117","q":"In AWS, what does each part of this command set, and what does it allow?","a":"<p>It adds an inbound rule to the security group: TCP on port 443 from any IPv4 address. Because security groups are stateful, replies are allowed automatically. It is normal for a public web server or load balancer, and wrong for ports like SSH or a database.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress --group-id &lt;security-group-id&gt; --protocol tcp --port 443 --cidr 0.0.0.0/0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0679","card":"q-terraform-118-update-ec2-security-group-for-https","sec":"terraform-s117","q":"In AWS, what error do you get when you add a security group rule that already exists, and how do you remove one?","a":"<p><code>InvalidPermission.Duplicate</code>. Remove a rule with <code>aws ec2 revoke-security-group-ingress</code> using the same protocol, port and source.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0680","card":"q-terraform-118-update-ec2-security-group-for-https","sec":"terraform-s117","q":"A Terraform-managed security group gets an extra HTTPS rule added with the CLI during an incident. What happens at the next apply?","a":"<p>If the group uses inline <code>ingress</code> blocks, Terraform treats the group as the full list of rules and removes the extra rule. If the rules are separate <code>aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule</code> resources, the unmanaged rule can stay. Either way, copy the change into the Terraform code afterwards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0681","card":"q-terraform-118-update-ec2-security-group-for-https","sec":"terraform-s117","q":"A security group allows port 443 from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>, but HTTPS to the instance still times out. What else do you check?","a":"<p>Other layers: the network ACL of the subnet (stateless, needs rules both ways), the route table and internet gateway, a public IP or Elastic IP, a firewall on the instance, and whether anything listens on 443. A timeout points to network filtering, a \"connection refused\" points to nothing listening.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0682","card":"q-terraform-118-update-ec2-security-group-for-https","sec":"terraform-s117","q":"Why is a TLS certificate needed in addition to opening port 443?","a":"<p>The port only lets traffic in. The server (or the load balancer or CloudFront in front) must present a certificate for the domain. On AWS you attach an ACM certificate to the load balancer listener, or install one on the instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0683","card":"q-terraform-119-check-s3-bucket-replication-rule-status","sec":"terraform-s118","q":"In AWS S3, what does <code>aws s3api get-bucket-replication</code> return, and what error do you get when none is set?","a":"<p>It returns the replication configuration: the IAM <code>Role</code> S3 assumes, and the list of <code>Rules</code> with their <code>Status</code> (<code>Enabled</code> or <code>Disabled</code>), filter or prefix, destination bucket and storage class. If the bucket has none you get <code>ReplicationConfigurationNotFoundError</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3api get-bucket-replication --bucket &lt;source-bucket-name&gt;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0684","card":"q-terraform-119-check-s3-bucket-replication-rule-status","sec":"terraform-s118","q":"In AWS S3, which prerequisites must a replication setup meet?","a":"<p>Versioning must be enabled on both the source and the destination bucket, S3 needs an IAM role with permission to read from the source and write to the destination, and for cross-account replication the destination bucket policy must allow it. Without versioning the rule cannot be created.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0685","card":"q-terraform-119-check-s3-bucket-replication-rule-status","sec":"terraform-s118","q":"In AWS S3, a replication rule is <code>Enabled</code> but an object is not in the destination bucket. How do you check that object?","a":"<p>Run <code>aws s3api head-object</code> on the source object and read <code>ReplicationStatus</code>: <code>PENDING</code>, <code>COMPLETED</code>, <code>FAILED</code> (or <code>REPLICA</code> on the destination copy). A failed status usually means a missing permission or a KMS key problem for encrypted objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0686","card":"q-terraform-119-check-s3-bucket-replication-rule-status","sec":"terraform-s118","q":"In AWS S3, a replication rule is created today. Are the objects that already exist in the bucket copied?","a":"<p>No. Replication copies only objects uploaded after the rule is active. To copy existing objects use S3 Batch Replication. Delete markers and permanent version deletions are also not replicated by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0687","card":"q-terraform-120-troubleshoot-eks-cluster-not-responding","sec":"terraform-s119","q":"In AWS EKS, what do these three calls tell you, in order?","a":"<p>The first shows the control plane state (<code>CREATING</code>, <code>ACTIVE</code>, <code>UPDATING</code>, <code>FAILED</code>, <code>DELETING</code>). If it is not <code>ACTIVE</code>, the control plane is the problem. The second lists the managed node groups. The third shows one node group's status, scaling config and <code>health.issues</code>, which name problems such as failed node launches or missing IAM permissions.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws eks describe-cluster --name &lt;cluster-name&gt; --query &quot;cluster.status&quot;\naws eks list-nodegroups --cluster-name &lt;cluster-name&gt;\naws eks describe-nodegroup --cluster-name &lt;cluster-name&gt; --nodegroup-name &lt;nodegroup-name&gt;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0688","card":"q-terraform-120-troubleshoot-eks-cluster-not-responding","sec":"terraform-s119","q":"In AWS EKS, the control plane is <code>ACTIVE</code> but <code>kubectl</code> cannot connect. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The kubeconfig is current (<code>aws eks update-kubeconfig --name &lt;cluster&gt;</code>) and your IAM identity is allowed in the cluster (access entries, or the <code>aws-auth</code> ConfigMap on older clusters).</li>\n  <li>The API endpoint access: public, private or both, and the allowed public CIDR list. A private-only endpoint is reachable only from inside the VPC.</li>\n  <li>Security groups and network path to the endpoint.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0689","card":"q-terraform-120-troubleshoot-eks-cluster-not-responding","sec":"terraform-s119","q":"In AWS EKS, how does the cluster endpoint access setting affect where you can run <code>kubectl</code> from?","a":"<p>With public access, <code>kubectl</code> works from any allowed CIDR on the internet. With private access only, the endpoint resolves to private IPs in the VPC, so you need a bastion, VPN or Direct Connect. Many teams use private access, or public access limited to office CIDRs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0690","card":"q-terraform-120-troubleshoot-eks-cluster-not-responding","sec":"terraform-s119","q":"In AWS EKS, pods stay <code>Pending</code> and nodes are missing. Which checks follow from the node group view?","a":"<p>Look at the node group <code>health.issues</code> and the Auto Scaling group behind it, as for any group that cannot launch (quotas, subnets with no IPs, AMI, instance profile). Then check that the nodes' IAM role has the worker node, CNI and ECR read policies, and that the node security group lets nodes talk to the control plane.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0691","card":"q-terraform-121-restore-s3-object-from-specific-version","sec":"terraform-s120","q":"In AWS S3, what does this command do to the version history of the object, and why is that the usual way to restore?","a":"<p>It copies the chosen old version to the same key. The copy becomes a new current version, and all other versions stay. History is not lost, and the restore can itself be undone. An S3 object version cannot be edited in place, and deleting newer versions is the destructive alternative.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3api copy-object --bucket &lt;bucket-name&gt; --key &lt;object-key&gt; --copy-source &lt;bucket-name&gt;/&lt;object-key&gt;?versionId=&lt;version-id&gt;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0692","card":"q-terraform-121-restore-s3-object-from-specific-version","sec":"terraform-s120","q":"In AWS S3, how do you find the <code>versionId</code> to restore, and what do delete markers have to do with it?","a":"<p><code>aws s3api list-object-versions --bucket &lt;bucket&gt; --prefix &lt;key&gt;</code> lists versions with IDs, dates and an <code>IsLatest</code> flag, and also lists delete markers. If the object was deleted, a delete marker is the latest version and hides the object. Removing the delete marker (<code>aws s3api delete-object --version-id &lt;marker-id&gt;</code>) brings the previous version back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0693","card":"q-terraform-121-restore-s3-object-from-specific-version","sec":"terraform-s120","q":"In AWS S3, which limit applies to <code>copy-object</code>, and what do you use for larger objects?","a":"<p>A single <code>CopyObject</code> request works for objects up to 5 GB. For larger objects use a multipart copy (<code>UploadPartCopy</code>). <code>aws s3 cp</code> between S3 locations does it automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0694","card":"q-terraform-121-restore-s3-object-from-specific-version","sec":"terraform-s120","q":"In AWS S3, why must versioning have been on before an object can be restored from an older version?","a":"<p>Without versioning an overwrite or delete replaces the object for good, there are no older versions to copy. Versioning only keeps versions created after it was turned on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0695","card":"q-terraform-122-troubleshoot-cloudwatch-alarm-not-trigge","sec":"terraform-s121","q":"In AWS CloudWatch, what does <code>describe-alarm-history</code> with <code>--history-item-type StateUpdate</code> show, and what are the other history item types?","a":"<p><code>StateUpdate</code> lists each change between <code>OK</code>, <code>ALARM</code> and <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>, with a reason that includes the data points that caused it. The other types are <code>ConfigurationUpdate</code> (the alarm was edited) and <code>Action</code> (an alarm action ran or failed). <code>describe-alarms</code> shows the current state, metric, threshold and whether actions are enabled.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --alarm-names &lt;alarm-name&gt;\naws cloudwatch describe-alarm-history --alarm-name &lt;alarm-name&gt; --history-item-type StateUpdate</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0696","card":"q-terraform-122-troubleshoot-cloudwatch-alarm-not-trigge","sec":"terraform-s121","q":"In AWS CloudWatch, an alarm goes to <code>ALARM</code> but nobody gets a notification. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that <code>ActionsEnabled</code> is true, the alarm has an <code>AlarmActions</code> entry, the SNS topic policy lets CloudWatch publish, and the topic subscription is confirmed. The <code>Action</code> entries in the alarm history show whether the action failed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0697","card":"q-terraform-122-troubleshoot-cloudwatch-alarm-not-trigge","sec":"terraform-s121","q":"In AWS CloudWatch, a metric clearly crossed the threshold but the alarm stays <code>OK</code>. What can explain it?","a":"<p>The alarm may watch different data than the one you look at: another namespace, metric name, dimension, statistic (Average instead of Maximum) or period. Also the breach must hold for <code>EvaluationPeriods</code> (or <code>DatapointsToAlarm</code> of them), and metrics may arrive late.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0698","card":"q-terraform-122-troubleshoot-cloudwatch-alarm-not-trigge","sec":"terraform-s121","q":"In AWS CloudWatch, why does a high-resolution alarm period such as 10 seconds cost more than a standard one?","a":"<p>Alarms with a period under 60 seconds are priced as high-resolution alarms, and the metric must be published at high resolution too. Standard alarms use periods that are multiples of 60 seconds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0699","card":"q-terraform-123-resize-rds-instance-for-load","sec":"terraform-s122","q":"In AWS RDS, what happens to the database when this command changes the instance class, and what downtime should you expect?","a":"<p>A class change needs a restart. For a single-AZ instance expect downtime of several minutes. For Multi-AZ, RDS fails over to the standby, which takes about one to two minutes. Plan it for a quiet period.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws rds modify-db-instance --db-instance-identifier &lt;db-instance-id&gt; --db-instance-class &lt;new-instance-class&gt; --apply-immediately</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0700","card":"q-terraform-123-resize-rds-instance-for-load","sec":"terraform-s122","q":"In Terraform, an RDS instance class is changed with the CLI during an incident. What must you do afterwards, and why?","a":"<p>Set <code>instance_class</code> in the Terraform code to the new value. Otherwise the next <code>plan</code> shows a change back to the old class and an <code>apply</code> would scale the database down again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0701","card":"q-terraform-123-resize-rds-instance-for-load","sec":"terraform-s122","q":"In AWS RDS, how does scaling the instance class differ from adding read replicas, and when do you choose each?","a":"<p>Changing the class is vertical scaling: more CPU and memory for the same database, with a short interruption. Read replicas are horizontal scaling for read traffic only, with replication lag, and the application must send reads to them. Use a bigger class for write-heavy or CPU-bound load, and replicas for read-heavy load.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0702","card":"q-terraform-123-resize-rds-instance-for-load","sec":"terraform-s122","q":"In AWS RDS, how do you reduce the downtime of a resize for a Multi-AZ instance?","a":"<p>RDS modifies the standby first, then fails over to it, and then modifies the old primary. The only interruption is the failover. Schedule it in a quiet period and make sure the application reconnects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0703","card":"q-terraform-124-how-python-integrates-with-terraform","sec":"terraform-s123","q":"How does Python integrate with Terraform for resource management?","a":"<p>The <code>python-terraform</code> library or the <code>subprocess</code> module can execute Terraform commands or manage state files. Python's versatility and rich ecosystem make it a good orchestrator for Terraform alongside other tools.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0704","card":"q-terraform-124-how-python-integrates-with-terraform","sec":"terraform-s123","q":"How is idempotency ensured with Terraform?","a":"<p>Terraform uses state files so resources are only created or updated when needed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0705","card":"q-terraform-124-how-python-integrates-with-terraform","sec":"terraform-s123","q":"In Python, how do you run Terraform commands from a script: the <code>subprocess</code> module or the <code>python-terraform</code> library, and which would you choose?","a":"<p>Both end up running the Terraform CLI as a child process. <code>subprocess</code> is in the standard library, adds no dependency, and gives full control over arguments, environment, timeouts and return codes. <code>python-terraform</code> is a thin wrapper that maps methods like <code>init()</code> and <code>apply()</code> to CLI calls. <code>subprocess</code> is the safer long-term choice because it always supports the options of the installed Terraform version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0706","card":"q-terraform-124-how-python-integrates-with-terraform","sec":"terraform-s123","q":"Why would a team drive Terraform from Python instead of calling <code>terraform</code> in a shell script?","a":"<p>Python is better at logic around Terraform: parsing JSON output, calling cloud APIs, handling errors and retries, building variable files, and running several environments in a loop. A shell script is fine for a short sequence of commands, and a CI pipeline step is often enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0707","card":"q-terraform-124-how-python-integrates-with-terraform","sec":"terraform-s123","q":"Which Terraform CLI outputs are meant to be parsed by a Python script?","a":"<p>Use the machine-readable ones: <code>terraform output -json</code>, <code>terraform show -json</code> (state or a saved plan), <code>terraform validate -json</code> and <code>terraform plan -json</code> (a stream of events). Do not parse the human-readable text, which can change between versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0708","card":"q-terraform-125-purpose-of-python-terraform","sec":"terraform-s124","q":"What is the purpose of the Terraform Python provider (<code>python-terraform</code>), and how can it be used to manage infrastructure?","a":"<p>It is a library that lets Python scripts interact with Terraform commands and state files. It is used to:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Execute Terraform commands (<code>init</code>, <code>plan</code>, <code>apply</code>) programmatically.</li>\n  <li>Read or modify Terraform state files.</li>\n  <li>Automate infrastructure provisioning in workflows.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from python_terraform import Terraform\ntf = Terraform(working_dir=&#x27;.&#x27;)\ntf.init()\ntf.apply(skip_plan=True)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0709","card":"q-terraform-125-purpose-of-python-terraform","sec":"terraform-s124","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, what does the <code>working_dir</code> argument of <code>Terraform(...)</code> set?","a":"<p>It is the directory in which every command runs. It has to contain the <code>.tf</code> files, and <code>.terraform/</code> and local state live there too. Terraform itself does not need a Python-specific provider: this library only starts the CLI.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from python_terraform import Terraform\ntf = Terraform(working_dir=&#x27;.&#x27;)\ntf.init()\ntf.apply(skip_plan=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0710","card":"q-terraform-125-purpose-of-python-terraform","sec":"terraform-s124","q":"Is <code>python-terraform</code> an official HashiCorp library, and does it replace the Terraform CLI?","a":"<p>No. It is a third-party wrapper, and the Terraform binary must be installed because every call runs it. The CLI is the supported interface, so scripts rely on CLI behaviour either way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0711","card":"q-terraform-125-purpose-of-python-terraform","sec":"terraform-s124","q":"What is the difference between <code>python-terraform</code> and the CDK for Terraform (CDKTF) when you want to use Python with Terraform?","a":"<p><code>python-terraform</code> runs an existing configuration written in HCL. CDKTF lets you write the infrastructure itself in Python, which it turns into Terraform JSON configuration, and then runs Terraform. Use the wrapper to automate workflows, and CDKTF if you want the definitions in a general-purpose language.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0712","card":"q-terraform-126-state-management-from-python","sec":"terraform-s125","q":"How do you handle state management in Terraform when using Python to automate workflows?","a":"<p>Terraform state is kept in a <code>terraform.tfstate</code> file. With Python:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use <code>python-terraform</code> to read and write state files.</li>\n  <li>Store state in remote backends (for example S3) for team collaboration.</li>\n  <li>Lock state using DynamoDB to prevent concurrent modifications.</li>\n  <li>Avoid manual state edits to prevent drift.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from python_terraform import Terraform\ntf = Terraform(working_dir=&#x27;.&#x27;)\nstate = tf.read_state_file()\nprint(state)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"terraform-f0713","card":"q-terraform-126-state-management-from-python","sec":"terraform-s125","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, <code>tf.read_state_file()</code> reads the state of the working directory. Why can it not be used when the team stores state in an S3 backend?","a":"<p>It reads a local <code>terraform.tfstate</code> file. With a remote backend there is no such file, only a cache or nothing. Run <code>terraform state pull</code> or <code>terraform show -json</code>, which ask the configured backend.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from python_terraform import Terraform\ntf = Terraform(working_dir=&#x27;.&#x27;)\nstate = tf.read_state_file()\nprint(state)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0714","card":"q-terraform-126-state-management-from-python","sec":"terraform-s125","q":"Why should a script use <code>terraform show -json</code> or <code>terraform output -json</code> instead of opening <code>terraform.tfstate</code> itself?","a":"<p>The file is an internal format that Terraform may change, and with a remote backend it is not on disk. The JSON commands go through the backend, respect locking and are documented as the stable interface. Treat their output as sensitive, because state can contain secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0715","card":"q-terraform-126-state-management-from-python","sec":"terraform-s125","q":"In Terraform, what is the structure of the <code>resources</code> list in a state file?","a":"<p>Each entry has <code>mode</code> (<code>managed</code> or <code>data</code>), <code>type</code>, <code>name</code> and <code>provider</code>, plus an <code>instances</code> list. The <code>attributes</code> (including <code>id</code>) are on each element of <code>instances</code>, because one resource block can create several instances with <code>count</code> or <code>for_each</code>. A script that reads <code>resource[\"attributes\"]</code> directly will fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0716","card":"q-terraform-126-state-management-from-python","sec":"terraform-s125","q":"A Python script that runs Terraform gets an \"Error acquiring the state lock\" message. How should the script react?","a":"<p>Wait and retry with a limit, for example by passing <code>-lock-timeout</code>, or fail with a clear message. Never force-unlock automatically, because the lock may belong to a run that is still active. Python only starts the CLI and must not edit the state file itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0717","card":"q-terraform-127-how-do-you-use-the-python-terraform-libr","sec":"terraform-s126","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, what does <code>tf.init()</code> return and how do you detect failure?","a":"<p>It returns a tuple of the exit code, the standard output and the standard error of the CLI. An exit code of 0 is success and anything else is failure, with the reason in <code>stderr</code>. The library does not raise an exception for a failed command, so the script has to check the code.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from python_terraform import Terraform\ntf = Terraform(working_dir=&#x27;/path/to/terraform/project&#x27;)\nreturn_code, stdout, stderr = tf.init()\nif return_code == 0:\n    print(&quot;Terraform init successful&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(f&quot;Terraform init failed: {stderr}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0718","card":"q-terraform-127-how-do-you-use-the-python-terraform-libr","sec":"terraform-s126","q":"How do you install <code>python-terraform</code>, and what else must be present on the machine?","a":"<p><code>pip install python-terraform</code>, ideally in a virtual environment. The <code>terraform</code> executable must be on the <code>PATH</code> (or its path given to the class), because the library only runs it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0719","card":"q-terraform-128-write-a-python-script-to-initialize-and","sec":"terraform-s127","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, why does the script stop when <code>init</code> fails instead of running <code>apply</code> anyway?","a":"<p><code>apply</code> depends on a successful <code>init</code> (providers and backend). Running it on a failed init gives a second, less clear error and, with a half-configured backend, can use the wrong state. Failing fast with a non-zero exit code also lets CI mark the job failed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return_code, stdout, stderr = tf.init()\nif return_code != 0:\n    print(f&quot;Init failed: {stderr}&quot;)\n    exit(1)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0720","card":"q-terraform-128-write-a-python-script-to-initialize-and","sec":"terraform-s127","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, what does <code>tf.apply(auto_approve=True)</code> do and what is the risk?","a":"<p>It adds <code>-auto-approve</code>, so Terraform applies without asking for confirmation. This is needed for non-interactive scripts. The risk is that nobody reviews the plan, so a mistake (for example a destroy and recreate) goes straight to the cloud. Pipelines usually save a reviewed plan with <code>plan -out</code> and apply that file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0721","card":"q-terraform-128-write-a-python-script-to-initialize-and","sec":"terraform-s127","q":"In a Python script, why use <code>sys.exit(1)</code> instead of <code>exit(1)</code> to end with an error?","a":"<p><code>exit</code> is a helper added by the <code>site</code> module for interactive use, and it is missing when Python runs with <code>-S</code> or in some frozen programs. <code>sys.exit</code> is part of the standard library and always available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0722","card":"q-terraform-128-write-a-python-script-to-initialize-and","sec":"terraform-s127","q":"Why does a script that runs <code>terraform apply</code> non-interactively pass <code>-input=false</code>?","a":"<p>Without it Terraform can stop and wait for a prompt, for example for an unset variable, and a non-interactive job then hangs. With <code>-input=false</code> it fails with an error immediately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0723","card":"q-terraform-129-how-do-you-manage-terraform-state-files","sec":"terraform-s128","q":"In Python, how do you list the resources in the Terraform state without parsing <code>terraform.tfstate</code> yourself?","a":"<p>Run <code>terraform state list</code> for the addresses, or <code>terraform show -json</code> and read <code>values.root_module.resources</code> (and child modules). From Python use <code>subprocess.run([\"terraform\", \"show\", \"-json\"], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)</code> and <code>json.loads(result.stdout)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0724","card":"q-terraform-129-how-do-you-manage-terraform-state-files","sec":"terraform-s128","q":"In Python, which operations on state are safe to automate and which are risky?","a":"<p>Reads (<code>state list</code>, <code>state show</code>, <code>show -json</code>, <code>output -json</code>) are safe. Writes (<code>state rm</code>, <code>state mv</code>, <code>import</code>, <code>state push</code>) change what Terraform believes exists, so a script should run them only after a backup (<code>terraform state pull &gt; backup.json</code>) and with a clear approval. Never edit the JSON file by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0725","card":"q-terraform-129-how-do-you-manage-terraform-state-files","sec":"terraform-s128","q":"In Terraform, why does reading state from a script need the same permissions and care as reading secrets?","a":"<p>State holds resource attributes in plain text, including passwords, keys and connection strings, even for values marked <code>sensitive</code>. A script that prints or logs the state leaks them. Restrict access to the backend and avoid printing the JSON.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0726","card":"q-terraform-130-explain-how-to-use-python-to-parse-terra","sec":"terraform-s129","q":"In Terraform, how do you get a plan in a form a Python script can parse?","a":"<p>Save the plan with <code>terraform plan -out=plan.tfplan</code>, then render it with <code>terraform show -json plan.tfplan</code>. The JSON is the stable machine-readable format, while the plain text of <code>plan</code> is for people.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tf = Terraform(working_dir=&#x27;/path/to/terraform/project&#x27;)\nreturn_code, stdout, stderr = tf.plan(out=&quot;plan.tfplan&quot;, var={&#x27;instance_type&#x27;: &#x27;t2.micro&#x27;}, input=IsFlagged.NO)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0727","card":"q-terraform-130-explain-how-to-use-python-to-parse-terra","sec":"terraform-s129","q":"In the JSON of <code>terraform show -json plan.tfplan</code>, where do you find what will change?","a":"<p>In the <code>resource_changes</code> array. Each element has the resource <code>address</code> and a <code>change</code> object with <code>actions</code> (<code>[\"create\"]</code>, <code>[\"update\"]</code>, <code>[\"delete\"]</code>, <code>[\"no-op\"]</code>, or <code>[\"delete\",\"create\"]</code> for a replacement) plus <code>before</code> and <code>after</code> values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0728","card":"q-terraform-130-explain-how-to-use-python-to-parse-terra","sec":"terraform-s129","q":"How would you fail a CI job when a plan contains a delete action?","a":"<p>The script reads every change and collects those whose <code>actions</code> contain <code>delete</code>, which also covers replacements. If the list is not empty it exits with a non-zero status, which fails the job before <code>apply</code>. The same check works with a policy tool such as OPA.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json\nimport subprocess\n\nplan = subprocess.run(\n    [&quot;terraform&quot;, &quot;show&quot;, &quot;-json&quot;, &quot;plan.tfplan&quot;],\n    capture_output=True, text=True, check=True,\n)\nchanges = json.loads(plan.stdout)[&quot;resource_changes&quot;]\ndeletes = [c[&quot;address&quot;] for c in changes if &quot;delete&quot; in c[&quot;change&quot;][&quot;actions&quot;]]\nif deletes:\n    raise SystemExit(f&quot;Plan deletes: {deletes}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0729","card":"q-terraform-131-write-a-python-script-to-create-a-terraf","sec":"terraform-s130","q":"In Terraform, which variable file names are loaded automatically, and which need <code>-var-file</code>?","a":"<p>Terraform loads <code>terraform.tfvars</code>, <code>terraform.tfvars.json</code>, and any <code>*.auto.tfvars</code> or <code>*.auto.tfvars.json</code> file in the working directory. Any other file name needs <code>-var-file=&lt;file&gt;</code>. Later files override earlier ones, and <code>-var</code> and <code>-var-file</code> on the command line win.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0730","card":"q-terraform-131-write-a-python-script-to-create-a-terraf","sec":"terraform-s130","q":"Why does writing a <code>.tfvars</code> file with <code>f.write(f'{key} = \"{value}\"\\n')</code> break for some values, and what is more robust?","a":"<p>It puts every value in quotes. Numbers, booleans, lists and maps would become strings, and a quote or backslash inside the value breaks the syntax. Write JSON instead with <code>json.dump(variables, f)</code> to <code>terraform.tfvars.json</code>, because JSON represents types and escapes correctly.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with open(&#x27;terraform.tfvars&#x27;, &#x27;w&#x27;) as f:\n    for key, value in variables.items():\n        f.write(f&#x27;{key} = &quot;{value}&quot;\\n&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0731","card":"q-terraform-131-write-a-python-script-to-create-a-terraf","sec":"terraform-s130","q":"In Terraform, how should a script pass a secret such as a database password to a configuration?","a":"<p>Do not write it to a <code>.tfvars</code> file on disk. Set the environment variable <code>TF_VAR_&lt;name&gt;</code> for the Terraform process (for example through <code>env=</code> in <code>subprocess.run</code>), or read it from a secret manager inside Terraform. Mark the variable <code>sensitive = true</code> to keep it out of plan output, but it still lands in state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0732","card":"q-terraform-131-write-a-python-script-to-create-a-terraf","sec":"terraform-s130","q":"When would you generate variable files from Python instead of keeping them in version control?","a":"<p>When values come from somewhere dynamic: an inventory, an API, or per-environment settings that are computed. Static, reviewed values belong in version control so changes are visible in review.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0733","card":"q-terraform-132-how-do-you-handle-terraform-errors-in-py","sec":"terraform-s131","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, what do the return code and <code>stderr</code> tell you when <code>apply</code> fails?","a":"<p>A non-zero code means the command failed, and <code>stderr</code> holds Terraform's error text (for example an invalid configuration or an API error). The <code>except</code> block catches problems in Python itself, such as the <code>terraform</code> binary missing, not failures of Terraform. Both paths must end with a non-zero exit of the script.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    return_code, stdout, stderr = tf.apply(auto_approve=True)\n    if return_code != 0:\n        print(f&quot;Terraform error: {stderr}&quot;)\n    else:\n        print(&quot;Terraform applied successfully&quot;)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Unexpected error: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0734","card":"q-terraform-132-how-do-you-handle-terraform-errors-in-py","sec":"terraform-s131","q":"How do you get a structured list of problems from an invalid configuration in a script?","a":"<p>Run <code>terraform validate -json</code>. The output has <code>valid</code>, error and warning counts, and a <code>diagnostics</code> list with severity, summary, detail and the file and line range. A script can print those instead of the raw text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0735","card":"q-terraform-132-how-do-you-handle-terraform-errors-in-py","sec":"terraform-s131","q":"Which Terraform failures is it reasonable to retry from a script, and which not?","a":"<p>Retry transient errors, such as API throttling, timeouts and a state lock held by another run, with backoff and a limit. Do not retry configuration errors, validation errors or permission errors, because the result will not change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0736","card":"q-terraform-132-how-do-you-handle-terraform-errors-in-py","sec":"terraform-s131","q":"What goes wrong if a script catches the <code>apply</code> failure and prints it, but exits with code 0?","a":"<p>The pipeline reports success while the infrastructure is partly built. A script that wraps Terraform must pass the failure on through its own exit code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0737","card":"q-terraform-133-how-can-python-be-used-to-automate-terra","sec":"terraform-s132","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, how do you create, select, list and delete Terraform workspaces from a script?","a":"<p>The library has helper methods <code>create_workspace</code>, <code>set_workspace</code>, <code>delete_workspace</code> and <code>show_workspace</code>. They run <code>terraform workspace new</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>delete</code> and <code>show</code>. There is no list helper, so use <code>tf.cmd(\"workspace\", \"list\")</code>. Unknown attribute names such as <code>workspace_new</code> are not workspace helpers: the library turns them into a CLI command of that name, which Terraform rejects. Each workspace has its own state in the same backend, so one configuration can manage several copies of an environment.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from python_terraform import Terraform\ntf = Terraform(working_dir=&quot;.&quot;)\ntf.create_workspace(&quot;dev&quot;)\ntf.set_workspace(&quot;dev&quot;)\nreturn_code, stdout, stderr = tf.cmd(&quot;workspace&quot;, &quot;list&quot;)\ntf.delete_workspace(&quot;dev&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0738","card":"q-terraform-133-how-can-python-be-used-to-automate-terra","sec":"terraform-s132","q":"In Terraform, why can two Python scripts selecting different workspaces in the same working directory at the same time interfere with each other?","a":"<p>The selected workspace is stored in a file in the directory (<code>.terraform/environment</code>), so it is shared state on disk. Use the <code>TF_WORKSPACE</code> environment variable for each process, or give each run its own working directory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0739","card":"q-terraform-133-how-can-python-be-used-to-automate-terra","sec":"terraform-s132","q":"In Terraform, when does <code>workspace delete</code> fail?","a":"<p>You cannot delete the workspace you are currently in, nor <code>default</code>. It also fails when the workspace's state still holds resources, unless you use <code>-force</code>, which leaves those resources unmanaged in the cloud.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0740","card":"q-terraform-133-how-can-python-be-used-to-automate-terra","sec":"terraform-s132","q":"In Terraform, when would you choose separate directories or states per environment instead of workspaces driven from a script?","a":"<p>When environments differ in configuration, need different credentials or approvals, or when a mistake in one must not touch another. Workspaces share one codebase and one backend, which makes an accidental apply in the wrong workspace easy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0741","card":"q-terraform-134-write-a-python-script-to-destroy-terrafo","sec":"terraform-s133","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, what does <code>tf.destroy(auto_approve=True)</code> do, and what protects against running it in the wrong place?","a":"<p>It runs <code>terraform destroy -auto-approve</code> and removes everything in the state of the working directory without asking. Protect against mistakes by checking the workspace and account first, running <code>terraform plan -destroy</code> to review the list, restricting the credentials, and requiring a manual approval in the pipeline.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return_code, stdout, stderr = tf.destroy(auto_approve=True)\nif return_code == 0:\n    print(&quot;Resources destroyed successfully&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(f&quot;Destroy failed: {stderr}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0742","card":"q-terraform-134-write-a-python-script-to-destroy-terrafo","sec":"terraform-s133","q":"In Terraform, what happens to resources when <code>terraform destroy</code> fails halfway?","a":"<p>Resources already deleted are removed from state, and the rest stay. Running <code>destroy</code> again continues with what is left. Common causes are dependencies created outside Terraform (for example a non-empty S3 bucket or a network interface in a subnet).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0743","card":"q-terraform-134-write-a-python-script-to-destroy-terrafo","sec":"terraform-s133","q":"When is automated <code>destroy</code> a good idea?","a":"<p>For short-lived environments: preview environments per pull request, test stacks and nightly sandboxes. Production destroy should be a rare, manual and approved action.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0744","card":"q-terraform-135-how-do-you-integrate-python-with-terrafo","sec":"terraform-s134","q":"In Python, when would you use <code>boto3</code> alongside <code>python-terraform</code> for AWS resources?","a":"<p>Terraform creates and owns the infrastructure, and <code>boto3</code> does what Terraform does not: checks after the apply (smoke tests, reading the real state), one-off operations such as starting a task or invalidating a cache, and queries that feed Terraform variables. Resources that live a long time should not be created by <code>boto3</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tf.apply(auto_approve=True, var={&#x27;instance_type&#x27;: &#x27;t2.micro&#x27;})\nec2 = boto3.client(&#x27;ec2&#x27;)\nresponse = ec2.describe_instances()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0745","card":"q-terraform-135-how-do-you-integrate-python-with-terrafo","sec":"terraform-s134","q":"Why create long-lived AWS resources with Terraform and not with <code>boto3</code> calls in a script?","a":"<p><code>boto3</code> is imperative. The script has to check what exists, order the calls, handle partial failure and implement updates and deletes itself. Terraform stores state, builds a dependency graph, shows a plan, and is idempotent, so a second run changes nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0746","card":"q-terraform-135-how-do-you-integrate-python-with-terrafo","sec":"terraform-s134","q":"In <code>boto3</code>, how do you choose credentials and a region safely when a script runs Terraform and <code>boto3</code> together?","a":"<p>Use the default credential chain: environment variables, a shared profile or an IAM role (instance profile, OIDC role in CI). Both Terraform's AWS provider and <code>boto3</code> read the same credential locations, so they act as the same identity. Do not hard-code keys, and set the region explicitly (<code>AWS_REGION</code>) for both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0747","card":"q-terraform-135-how-do-you-integrate-python-with-terrafo","sec":"terraform-s134","q":"In <code>boto3</code>, why does a <code>describe_instances</code> call after <code>terraform apply</code> sometimes not show the new instance, and what do you do?","a":"<p>AWS APIs can be eventually consistent for a short time after a create. Use a waiter, for example <code>ec2.get_waiter(\"instance_running\")</code>, or retry with backoff, before asserting on the result. Also use a paginator for large result sets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0748","card":"q-terraform-136-explain-how-to-use-python-to-validate-te","sec":"terraform-s135","q":"In <code>python-terraform</code>, how does a script tell that <code>tf.validate()</code> found a problem, and where is the detail?","a":"<p>The method returns a tuple of return code, stdout and stderr. A non-zero return code means the validation failed, and the message is in <code>stderr</code> or <code>stdout</code>. The script should stop and exit non-zero so the pipeline fails. It needs <code>terraform init</code> first, and it can run with <code>init -backend=false</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return_code, stdout, stderr = tf.validate()\nif return_code == 0:\n    print(&quot;Configuration is valid&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(f&quot;Validation failed: {stderr}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0749","card":"q-terraform-136-explain-how-to-use-python-to-validate-te","sec":"terraform-s135","q":"Which pre-apply checks would you put in a script, in order?","a":"<p><code>terraform fmt -check</code> (style), <code>terraform init -backend=false</code> and <code>terraform validate</code> (syntax and schema), a linter such as <code>tflint</code> or a policy check (conventions, cost, security), then <code>terraform plan -out</code> for a real diff against the state. Cheap checks run first so a failure comes quickly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0750","card":"q-terraform-137-your-team-needs-to-automate-the-deployme","sec":"terraform-s136","q":"How would you structure a Python deployment script around Terraform so it is safe to run in CI?","a":"<p>Run the steps in order and stop at the first failure:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>init</code> (with the backend configuration).</li>\n  <li><code>validate</code>, then <code>plan -out=plan.tfplan -input=false</code>.</li>\n  <li>Show or store the plan, and require an approval for sensitive environments.</li>\n  <li><code>apply plan.tfplan</code> (applies exactly the reviewed plan).</li>\n  <li>Read <code>terraform output -json</code> for results. Use <code>subprocess.run(..., check=True)</code> or check each return code, a timeout per step, and a non-zero exit code on failure.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0751","card":"q-terraform-137-your-team-needs-to-automate-the-deployme","sec":"terraform-s136","q":"In Python, what is wrong with copying credentials from <code>os.getenv</code> into <code>os.environ</code> before starting Terraform?","a":"<p>It is redundant: the values are already in the environment, and child processes inherit it. If a variable is not set, <code>os.getenv</code> returns <code>None</code> and assigning <code>None</code> to <code>os.environ</code> raises a <code>TypeError</code>. Long-lived access keys are also the weaker option. Prefer an IAM role or short-lived credentials from OIDC or a profile.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>os.environ[&quot;AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID&quot;] = os.getenv(&quot;AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID&quot;)\nos.environ[&quot;AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&quot;] = os.getenv(&quot;AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0752","card":"q-terraform-137-your-team-needs-to-automate-the-deployme","sec":"terraform-s136","q":"Why does the plan and apply of an EKS cluster need a long timeout in a script?","a":"<p>Creating a cluster takes around 10 to 15 minutes, and node groups add more. A short <code>subprocess</code> timeout kills Terraform halfway and can leave a locked state and a partly built cluster. Give the step enough time, and on a kill release the lock only after checking that no run is still active.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0753","card":"q-terraform-138-a-terraform-configuration-is-causing-res","sec":"terraform-s137","q":"In Terraform, how do you reconcile drift: when do you apply and when do you change the code?","a":"<p>Decide which side is right. If the manual change was wrong, run <code>terraform apply</code> to put the resource back as the code says. If the change is wanted, update the configuration to match it, and use <code>terraform apply -refresh-only</code> to record the new real values in state. Never refresh state and leave the code unchanged without a decision.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0754","card":"q-terraform-138-a-terraform-configuration-is-causing-res","sec":"terraform-s137","q":"Why does a script that compares the set of instance IDs in AWS with the set in state find only part of the drift?","a":"<p>It detects resources that exist in AWS and are not in state (unmanaged), or the reverse. It does not see changed attributes of managed resources, such as a modified security group rule or instance type, which is the usual drift. It also lists every instance in the account, including those managed by other teams or tools.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0755","card":"q-terraform-138-a-terraform-configuration-is-causing-res","sec":"terraform-s137","q":"Why is it risky to run <code>terraform import</code> automatically for every resource a script finds that is not in state?","a":"<p>Importing writes only into state, and the configuration does not describe the resource. The next plan wants to change or destroy it to match the code, or the address is wrong. Imports should be reviewed, with a matching resource block (or an <code>import</code> block, Terraform 1.5+) written first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0756","card":"q-terraform-138-a-terraform-configuration-is-causing-res","sec":"terraform-s137","q":"Why should drift detection use read-only credentials?","a":"<p>Detection only needs to read. A job that runs often with write access could change production by mistake, and a read-only role limits the damage from a bug or leaked token.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0757","card":"q-terraform-139-your-terraform-configuration-is-failing","sec":"terraform-s138","q":"In <code>boto3</code>, how can a script check that an AWS region name is valid?","a":"<p>Create the client with a region that is known to work, then call <code>describe_regions</code> with the name under test. An unknown name raises a <code>ClientError</code>. Do not build the client with the region being tested: for a made-up region <code>boto3</code> raises <code>EndpointConnectionError</code> when the call is made, not <code>ClientError</code>. By default <code>describe_regions</code> lists only regions enabled for the account, so an opt-in region that is not enabled also fails. An offline alternative is <code>boto3.session.Session().get_available_regions(\"ec2\")</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nimport botocore.exceptions\n\nec2 = boto3.client(&quot;ec2&quot;, region_name=&quot;us-east-1&quot;)\ntry:\n    ec2.describe_regions(RegionNames=[region])\n    print(f&quot;Region {region} is valid&quot;)\nexcept botocore.exceptions.ClientError:\n    raise Exception(f&quot;Invalid AWS region: {region}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0758","card":"q-terraform-139-your-terraform-configuration-is-failing","sec":"terraform-s138","q":"In Terraform, can an invalid region be caught inside the configuration without Python?","a":"<p>Yes. Add a <code>validation</code> block to the region variable, for example <code>condition = contains([\"us-east-1\", \"eu-west-1\"], var.region)</code> with an <code>error_message</code>. <code>terraform validate</code> and <code>plan</code> then fail with your message. Variable validation exists since Terraform 0.13.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0759","card":"q-terraform-139-your-terraform-configuration-is-failing","sec":"terraform-s138","q":"Why does <code>terraform validate</code> not report an invalid AWS region name by itself?","a":"<p><code>validate</code> is static and does not contact AWS. In the AWS provider the region is checked when the provider is configured during <code>plan</code> or <code>apply</code>, where it fails with an error about the region or the endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0760","card":"q-terraform-140-your-python-script-managing-terraform-re","sec":"terraform-s139","q":"In Terraform, what are the usual reasons a script creates duplicate resources instead of updating the existing ones?","a":"<p>Terraform decides from state, so duplicates mean it did not see the existing resources:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The script runs in a fresh directory with local state, so the state is empty each time.</li>\n  <li>A different workspace or backend key is used by different runs.</li>\n  <li>State was lost or not shared between machines.</li>\n  <li>Resource names are built from random or time-based values, so every run asks for new ones.</li>\n  <li>Resources were created outside Terraform and never imported.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0761","card":"q-terraform-140-your-python-script-managing-terraform-re","sec":"terraform-s139","q":"In Terraform, how do you make a script that runs Terraform idempotent?","a":"<p>Use a remote backend with locking and a fixed state key per environment, keep the configuration deterministic (stable names, <code>for_each</code> keyed by name), and always run the same <code>init</code>, <code>plan</code> and <code>apply</code>. Run twice, the second run must show \"No changes\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0762","card":"q-terraform-140-your-python-script-managing-terraform-re","sec":"terraform-s139","q":"Why is checking with <code>boto3</code> whether an instance exists before calling <code>apply</code> the wrong way to get idempotency?","a":"<p>It skips Terraform for an instance that Terraform itself created, so later changes to it never apply. It has a race between check and create, matches by a tag that anyone can set, and also matches terminated instances. Terraform's state already answers \"does it exist\".</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = ec2.describe_instances(Filters=[{&quot;Name&quot;: &quot;tag:Name&quot;, &quot;Values&quot;: [instance_name]}])\nif response[&quot;Reservations&quot;]:\n    print(f&quot;Instance {instance_name} already exists&quot;)\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0763","card":"q-terraform-140-your-python-script-managing-terraform-re","sec":"terraform-s139","q":"In Terraform, how do you stop two scripts from applying at the same time?","a":"<p>State locking in the backend. The second run fails with \"Error acquiring the state lock\" or waits when <code>-lock-timeout</code> is set. In CI add a concurrency group so only one run per environment happens at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0764","card":"q-terraform-141-your-team-needs-to-automate-the-cleanup","sec":"terraform-s140","q":"Why is \"everything in AWS that is not in the Terraform state\" a dangerous definition of unused?","a":"<p>It treats every instance created by another team, another Terraform state, an Auto Scaling group or a manual test as garbage, and deletes it irreversibly. A resource is unused only when you have evidence: no traffic or attachment, an expired tag, an owner who confirmed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>unused_instances = aws_instances - tf_instances\nfor instance_id in unused_instances:\n    ec2.terminate_instances(InstanceIds=[instance_id])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0765","card":"q-terraform-141-your-team-needs-to-automate-the-cleanup","sec":"terraform-s140","q":"What is a safer process for cleaning up unused AWS resources?","a":"<p>Scope it and make it reversible:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Select by tags such as <code>Owner</code> or <code>ExpiresOn</code>, and limit to one account and region.</li>\n  <li>Produce a report and a dry run first, and get approval.</li>\n  <li>Snapshot or stop before delete, where possible.</li>\n  <li>For Terraform-managed resources, remove them from the configuration and apply, so state stays correct.</li>\n  <li>Log every deletion.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0766","card":"q-terraform-141-your-team-needs-to-automate-the-cleanup","sec":"terraform-s140","q":"In <code>boto3</code>, why does <code>describe_instances()</code> in a cleanup script not return all instances in a large account?","a":"<p>Results are paginated, and one call returns only a page with a <code>NextToken</code>. Use a paginator: <code>ec2.get_paginator(\"describe_instances\")</code> and loop over <code>paginate()</code>. Also, terminated instances stay visible for a while, so filter by state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0767","card":"q-terraform-141-your-team-needs-to-automate-the-cleanup","sec":"terraform-s140","q":"In Terraform, how do you remove resources you no longer need from a stack and keep state consistent?","a":"<p>Delete the resource block and run <code>apply</code>, which destroys the resource and removes it from state. To remove only from state and keep it in the cloud, use a <code>removed</code> block with <code>lifecycle { destroy = false }</code> (Terraform 1.7+) or <code>terraform state rm</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0768","card":"q-terraform-142-your-team-wants-to-enforce-consistent-ta","sec":"terraform-s141","q":"In Terraform, how do you make sure every resource has the required tags?","a":"<p>Layer several controls:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>default_tags</code> in the AWS provider block to apply common tags to everything that supports tags (AWS provider 3.38+).</li>\n  <li>A <code>validation</code> block on a <code>tags</code> variable to require specific keys.</li>\n  <li>A policy or lint check in CI (OPA, Sentinel or <code>tflint</code>) on the plan.</li>\n  <li>Detective and preventive controls in AWS: an AWS Config <code>required-tags</code> rule, tag policies in AWS Organizations, or an SCP that denies creation without a tag.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0769","card":"q-terraform-142-your-team-wants-to-enforce-consistent-ta","sec":"terraform-s141","q":"In AWS provider <code>default_tags</code>, what happens when a resource sets a tag with the same key, and which resources do not get the tags?","a":"<p>The resource-level tag wins over the default tag with the same key. Resources created indirectly by another service, for example EC2 instances launched by an Auto Scaling group, do not inherit them. They need <code>tag</code> blocks with <code>propagate_at_launch = true</code> or tags in the launch template.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"terraform-f0770","card":"q-terraform-142-your-team-wants-to-enforce-consistent-ta","sec":"terraform-s141","q":"Why is it a poor design to fix tags with <code>boto3</code> <code>create_tags</code> calls and then run <code>terraform apply</code> with a tags variable?","a":"<p>Two tools then own the same tags. The next <code>plan</code> shows the manual tags as drift, and Terraform may remove them. It also tags only existing instances and not other resource types. Put tags in the configuration so Terraform is the single source of truth, and use <code>boto3</code> for an audit report of non-compliant resources.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ec2.create_tags(Resources=[instance_id], Tags=[{&quot;Key&quot;: k, &quot;Value&quot;: v} for k, v in missing_tags.items()])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"terraform-f0771","card":"q-terraform-142-your-team-wants-to-enforce-consistent-ta","sec":"terraform-s141","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between enforcing tags with AWS Config and with a service control policy?","a":"<p>AWS Config is detective: it evaluates resources after creation and flags non-compliant ones (with optional remediation). An SCP is preventive: it denies the API call that creates a resource without the required tag (using condition keys such as <code>aws:RequestTag</code>). Use the SCP to stop new violations and Config to find existing ones.</p>","hand":true}];
